        <p>The property to be acquired by the Company consists of— (a) 'lhe 1?. retail depots, pictu-e framing and gilding werks,bassinette works (London), wholesale warehouse and offices, held en leases or agreements. (b) The furnituce and bissinetto works carried on at Bury-street Mills, Steekport. These ircehold mills (now being fitted with Grinnell's automatic sorhaLlera) are erceted IMOn a plot of land situate between and lonnled by Penny-lane and Bury- street, Stcckport. The whole of the land comprises 5,305 sq.lare yards or thereabouts, about one-half of whieh is frontage, vacant, and valuable land. The whete is subject to an annual chief rent of £i6 6s 111 d, payable by the Company. The miiis are well adapted for all present require- ments, and will be abl t o deal with a greatly inereased output, being speeloue, well light2d, substantially built, and in good repair. (e) The goodwill of the businesses,patents,copyrights,de- signs. trade marks, trade names, contracts, the whole of the plant, mach nary. fixtures, fittiuo, furniture, horses, waggons, carts, trade implements, as they ex• isted en the Zi).,h September, 18E9. (d) The St ,ck in trade. (e) The bcals debts awl cash in hand. • The basinesses will be taken ever by the Company as a going concern, subjelt only to the current trade liabilities, as from the 30th day of SepternlyEr, 1899, ~nd the Company will have the benefit of all profits from that date, allowing to the vendor interest at the rate o! 5 per cent per annum on the purchase money until ^air:piano'. The books of Par, John Harrop's businesses have been re;ularly auditrd for the past eight years by Messrs. Anyon and Shelmerd'ne, accouni mats, 6, St. James's Square, Man- chester. who certlfy as follows . . . .</p>
