      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>A LONG-TELT WAN'T.—Continual poverty. THE fool at forty was probably an infant prodigy at four. SELF-PRESERVATION is the first law of nature, with lying a close second. they had their way about it, all the world would indeed l)e players. NEXT in merit to the cheerful giver is the man who growls bat contributes. BOYS will be boys, and girls would be if. they could. IT's a credulous world—every man believes in himself. COBBLE : "Have you got a good lawyer?" Stone: " No ; but he's smart." THERE is hope for the man who knows he is prejudiced. EvA. : " Did May receive much attention at the dance ? " Jack: "I imac,ine so. I heard some of the other girls call her designing.' " Miss YELLOWLEAF (who has just received a proposal) : " But, Major, don't you think there is too much difference in our ages?" Major Sharp, shooter: "No, indeed ! I'm a great deal older than I look, my dear Miss Yellowleaf." THE greatest trouble about blessings in disguise is their dilatoriness about discovering them- selves. .</p>
        <p>PATIENT: "Doctor, I am troubled with insom- nia." Young Doctor: " Er—a—what are the symptom?" THE practical man laughs at the theorist of to- day and ca-zries out the plans of the theorist of yesterday. TELL the world. something it knows, and it will applaud your perspicacity; tell it something new, and it will dcubt your veracity. JONES: "I think I'll have to give up smoking for good. It will come pretty hard, I suppose." Judsoil : "Not at all. I've done it lots of times 1" _ _ _</p>
        <p>SHE: "What a useful man Mr Dolittle is: He's always as busy as a bee." His Rival: "yes, and as useful as the b' in subtle."</p>
        <p>His APOLOGY.—WiIIis : "I'm mad at you. You never laugh when I tell a joke." Wallace: " Oh, you mustn't think strange of that. I'm very for- getful, you know." First Tramp: " Wot are yer goin' ter 'ave fore yer Christmas dinner, Bill ?" Second ditto : " Dunno 'zactly ; but I 'spect I shall dine out !" THE dyspeptic's definitions of Christmas gifts: "The exchange of something you can't afford for something you don't want." When the mince-meat days arrive, And the leaves are crisp and dry, Then the luscious eau de vie Gets a finger in the pie.</p>
        <p>A DISCOVERY.—Mamma: "Why did you give the baby that drum?" Papa: "Because he makes less noise when he has the drum."</p>
        <p>AN EXCEMON.—Wade : "Nothing is ever lost through politeness." Butcher: "Except your seat in a crowded public conveyance." PERPLEXED.—Hooker: "That reminds me of something I've forgotten." Busby : " What is it ?" Hooker "There's the rub—l can't remem- ber."</p>
        <p>INCLUDES THEM BOTH. —" After all love and war are the only proper themes for the poet." "Why do you say love and war? Why don't you just say marriage ?" UNAPPRE ATI VE. —Mrs. Newage : "A Cali- fornian girl is learning blacksmithing. What do you think of that ? " Woman-Hater: "I presume she has pretty arms." BROUGHT To.—" Why do you always throw cold water on everything I say?" "Because so many people have told me that everything you say is so unconscious."</p>
        <p>THE night before Christmas is one of the rare occasions on which the small boy is threatened</p>
        <p>with insomnia.</p>
        <p>DUBIOUS. —Humorist : "I am afraid these jokes are not very good." Editor: "What makes you think so?" Humorist: "My wife laughed so at them."</p>
        <p>Tin botanist is a man who knows all about flowers, and the florist is a party who knows how much the young man will pay to get them for his best girl.</p>
        <p>UNSATISFIED. Though man wants little here below, The small boys, you can bet, At Christmas feel they're full of woe, However much they get. NOVEL CLASSIFICATION.—" Do you prefer th , romantic or the realistic novels?" "Why, I don't know. What's the differences?" "The first are about impossible events, and the others are about impossible people." PROSPECTIVE LIBERALITY.—Young Ikenstein (in- sinuatingly) : "Vat are you going to gif your daughter von she gedts married, Meester Schwindle- baum ?" Old Schwindlebaum (coldly) "Mein conaendt, Aleester Ikenstein ?" A SLIGHT ALTERATION. —Ada : "Do you think the word ' obey " should be omitted from the marriage service?" Ida: " Omitted ? Certainly not. It should merely be transferred to the other party to the contract."</p>
        <p>ALWAYSM 5 DOCTGALL S PATENT SELF-RAISING FLOU h IT IS THE BEST.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>SOON LEARN BETTER.</head>
        <p>SOON LEARN BETTER.</p>
        <p>When yount, we always think it queer, That Christmas comes but once a year; But when we pay for Santa clalls We see the force 9.4 f ..."'ature's laws. XOT 111 NG HALT- WAY ABOUT sllq. GiISOIN seems to be a changed man." "Yes, he is. For- merly he was too busy at work to take exercise, and now he is too busy taking exercise to do any work."</p>
        <p>rt.</p>
        <p>QUITE AT HOME.—" Is that performer familiar with your music ? " she asked at the concert. "He must be," replied the composer, who was writh- ing. "He takes such liberties with it." FIN DE BlCYCLE.—Housekeeper : "And now, Bridget, is there anything more that you require before you become my cook ? " Bridget: " mum; there's wan thing I forgot mum. I'll expect the use of yer bicycle on me afternoons out. I've a pair av bloomers av me own."</p>
        <p>OCT OF A DIFFICULTY.—" Well," said the Admiralty lord to the shipbuilder, "this is really very awkward. We were to have named this ,;un- boat the Speedy,' and its trials only show fifteen knots, instead of the twenty-five expected." "Oh !" replied the shipbuilder, airily, "suppose, then, we call it the Surprise.' " SKILLED LABOUR.—Friend: "I am told that most prescriptions cost little or nothing to make up." Chemist: " Yes; but we must charge some- thing for deciphering the penmanship and trans- lating the Latin." SUMMONED.—" An you really claim that you can summon ghosts into your presence?" "Cer- tainly." " Prove it. "I will." He summons a ghost. " But—l don't see anything." "Nor I. But I summoned ono just the same. I didn't say I could make him co:ne."</p>
        <p>A LOT or TROUBLE.—Lottie : "It's a very in- convenient custom to wear the engagement ring on the third finger of the left hand." Kittie : " Why ? What difference does it make?" Lottie : "Because it's so hard to learn to use your left nstead of your right hand."</p>
        <p>HE GAVE UP HOPE AT ONCE.—" Prisoner," said a Nevada judge, "what have you to say to this indictment are you guilty or not guilty ? " "Before I answer the question, judge, I would like to ask your honour if this spectacled dude is all the lawyer I've got ? " "That is Mr. Ferguson, sir," responded the judge, sternly. "I have appointed him to defend you, as you seem to have Co counsel." "Judge," said the prisoner, sighing heavily, "I'm guilty."</p>
        <p>A TELL-TALE CHAIR. —Charlie (with embarrass- ment) : " H'm—yes—Clara was just showing me— your—a—new chair." Clara's Papa : "Yes, it's something new—registers the weight of the person sitting in it. You see the dial here-- Hello, I didn't suppose either one of you weighed two hundred and sixty pounds " "LISTEN," she said, "and I will tell you my story." "Don't, don't," lie said, in a voice that shook in its emotion. "Remember that we are strangers." " You shall hear it," she hissed. "Not here. Spare me—spare me, in pity's name!" I have no pity for such as you." He buried his face in his hands, and she told him her story—ay, without skipping a page, from the pre- face clear through to the end of the thirty-seventh chapter. And when the grey twilight came, the authoress laid the manuscript on the editor's pros- trate form and strode away.</p>
        <p>THE MOST NUTR!TiC,US.</p>
      </div>
