      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE, Hats generally are getting smaller and many toques have made their appearance. Panne, printed in the medley of colours and the designs so long associated with cashmere shawls, is much used in millinery. The toque is wider than ever. It now describes a high semi-circle, and at each side dips almost to the ears. The newest have a suggestion of spring about them. A dull green straw, neither dark nor pale, is trimmed with shaded violets and their green leaves, admirably reproduced and exactly the colour of the straw. Reverting to the increased size of the toques, it is a great pity they have developed this tendency. For those to whom the larger style of headgear is more becoming, there is the big picture hat still in favour at the court of fashion : a thing of beauty and a joy for ever to all.</p>
        <p>There can be no doubt but that we shall soon become familiar with the tight-fitting bodice. With a really good figure it may be smart and becoming, but it is the lot of few to possess this advantage, and even an indifferent figure is at a disadvantage in this mode. A bad one is a hope- less case indeed. Waistcoats are also returning, and white satin will be a favourite choice for wearing with coates or the bolero, which will most probably spend the season with us. The dressy bodice, to be worn with any skirt, is usually of plissee tulle, in either white or pink, the pleatings kept in shape by appliques of painted velvet, leaves, or lace foliage- The lace slip will also be much worn during the coming season. Sometimes the slip is composed entirely of diamond-shaped pieces of silk embroidery and rich insertion, and so 1Y.,-eomes a somewhat costly trifle.</p>
        <p>I saw yesterday a lady wearing a walking gown of green vicuna made with a capuchon cape—or what now stands for such, namely, a couple of adroitly arranged folds round the shoulders—of velvet the same shade, and lined with satin, also of similar hue. The front was finished (where it fastens) by a knot of the velvet terminated in long ends to below the waist. The neutral shades, the cedars, the greys and the doves, are to be allowed to flourish, and with them such trimmings as white, black, and pearl silk embroideries, and soft chenile mesh borderings and fringes. Perforations also will be available. They are especially useful for spring dresses, and the way in which they are accomplished is by cutting away the cloth to show an ornamental backing of velvet or embroidered material. Of course, the perforation must be most neatly made, and then must be embroidered or overlaid at t.he edges to complete pictureque whole. w</p>
        <p>Flannel or flannelette dressing-gowns have bee* to some extent superseded by the quilted silk robe*, which are now to be had made in accordance with European notions, and at comparatively low prices. Flannel dressing-gowns are in most cases made without a neckband, but with a very broad falling collar, which is frequently carried down to the waist in front in a single revers, which crosses over to the left side, and is there caught in by the girdle. The front is straight and full, the skies are slightly shaped, and there is a pleat at the back. This type of wrapping-gown looks nice in blue or crimson flannel with a kilted frill, either in white or a lighter tone of the colonr of the flannel, round the big cape collar and the turned-up 'gauntlet cuffs. Another pretty model, to be made in twilled flannel, has a small turn-down collar and revers edged with several rows of narrow black velvet ribbon, and, instead of a girdle, a shaped stiffened band of flannel trimmed to match the collar. Cashmere dressing-gowns are smartened with guipure applique and have often a sash of satin ribbon tied at the side of the front with a full bow and long ends. There are many delicious little fancies in buttons now; pearl knobs for gloves, wee fishes in oxy- dysed silver and enamel, and pale pink corals for fastening the babyish-looking lace wristlets and vests of silken shirts. All the quaintest pos sessions are being turned to account, and specially made antique buttons, such as hunt ones and those of old archery clubs, arc eagerly used again. Capes are not likely to be as popular as jackets, but the few new models that are being shown are long—slightly more than three-quarter length-ft and very carefully fitted about the shoulders, the short " bunchy " article being quite out of data, Box-cloth in black or putty colour is at preseni the favourite material, and it is generally trimmed with rows of stitching, narrow bands of velvet, or oue of the many fashionable varieties of cord and bead embroideries.</p>
        <p>Your. loving IvNig.</p>
        <p>wi. _ireiy _move all INESS,CHAPS,;</p>
        <p>:RITATION, etc., 'a very short time. VI M.. BEL' T HAM (1. 50N VA C HE L. TENHAM.</p>
