      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</head>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>The arrangements for the Queea's visit to Ireland,wi It be much the same as on the occasion of Her Majesty's visits to the Centinent. Tho new Royal yacht, doee not seem likely to come into Use this year, and, after the journey to Holyhead, the Queen will spend the night on beard the Victoria and Albernbeforncroesine St. George's Channel. Priecess Chrstian, Princess Victoria of Schleswig-Holstein, and Princess Heery of listenberg, will accompany the Qireen, and the terate will be very nemercus—the Master of the Household and all the members in waiting, attending (Poring the Sovereign's residence in Dublin. Should the Qeeen make a stay of two or three weeks in Ireland, it evil be much longer than any of her three former visits. She was in Leland for four days in 181.9 for five days in 1850, and for eight days in 1861. The nnly other occasion when the Sovereign has visited the Irish peeple, during the present cmtury, was iii 1821, when deorge IV. was in Ireland for a month. Tho Duke and Duchess of York have left town for Sendringham where they will remain until early in May, when they return to York House, Sc. James's Palace. for tbe season. It is an open secret that the Duchess is far from eatisfied with the residences allotted her York Cottage, Sandringham' is certainly not princely, as regards dimensions, zed it must be a very tight fit when the Duke and Duchess and their nursery," are in residence. York House, St James's is gloomy to a degree and Her Royal Hiehness seldom permits -her " babies "to remein there long. It seems however, probable,that the Duchess's wish te be granted the " White Lodge " Richmond, will shortly be realised, an arrangement which will add greatly to the comfort and convenience of Her Royal Higheess's household. General Cronje is said to have a nephew in this coun- try who considers the "war all rot." and it was also re- cently stated that Mrs. Steyn, wife of the President of the Orange Free State, was in Aberdeen. This however is not the ceseahough she is closely connected with Scot- land. Mrs. Sleyn is the daughter of the Rev. Colin Fraser, of Phillippolis in South Africa, who was at ono time a minister in the north of Scotland. His sister married Mr. James Paterson of Aberdeen and hence Mrs. Steyn's connection with the the granite city. There is a large Scotch element ix Bloemfontein, the capital of the Free State, and now the centre of interest in the camoaign. Such names as Fraser, Mackintosh, Innen, Sneert, and Mai:feria:re, are common among the townsfolk; and many members of the Volksraad are also of Scottish descent. As its name implies. Bloemfontein is famous for its flowers, and is situated in one of the favourite resort for those suffering from pulmonary complaints, the pure invigorating air, having a remark- ably beneficial effect noon all such patients. There are several thousand white inhabitant!, but most of them knock off for two hours or so at midday, after Spanish fashion, and there is an entire absence of hurry and bustle about the attests. Like most South African towns Bloemfontein is a city of straight lines and right angles, though Kimberley, which dates frem 1870, is an exception. Joelannesbure. is, of course, of late development dating only from 1886. Several attempts have been made to abolish the hilt in the Army, but tits se have always been so strongly resented that it still survives. There is tbe famous poem called " Curse in the breeks," which a Gaelic bard was moved to write in its defence. Nevertheless, like all the old style uniforms of the British Army, it is not a good dress to fight in. The Highland costume is picturesque, hut that is about all there is to say for it. Keen in the Highlands, where it has been worn from time imme- morial, it has been noticed that the keepers and gillies usually adopt the trousers when out on duty. Another reform in soldiers' dress.which has often been proposed, is the abolition of the famous bearskin helmet of the Guards. It costs £9 or £lO, and however useful It might be as a protection against swerd cuts, no one would ever think of sending the Guards into action In such headgear. It is now recognised that the khaki uniform is the only possible one in tho field in these days, when the chiet art in war consists of making thr troops invisible. Such a dress was recommended by Colonel Luard, several years before the Crimean i'Ve,r, hut it was not until about 1880, that the War Office began to roslise that there was anything in the sugges- tion, and that er.larlet was net the best colour to wear on the battlefield. Two Drawing Rewire in one week mean a very trying time to fashionable dressmakers, and their employees, and the rush this March has been extra great, owing to the uncertainty as to when these functions would be held. As was anticipated black, white, erey, mauve, heliotrope and khaki,were colours most en evidenee,glitter and stiffness being cnrispicuous by its absence. The best worn flowers as trimmings were the African lily, which is purely white with dark red stamens ; the Ixia, a flower of the African veldt,a tall tapering bloom which embraces almost every she&amp; in the scales of pink and mauve, and the English rose. Japaaoi.e silk, promises to have a wide vogue this spring and coming summer. A fact to congratulate our- selves upon. as this soft silky substarce is light, durable and when the right shades see chosen, particularly be- coming. Ihe grey shades are exquisite for day wear, but for day war only should these be indulged in. By artificial light they are far from fascinating even when relieved by touches of vivid colour, by means of which many women endeavour to save the situation. The war provides many emtslems in fancy wares and sweetstuffs, which are everywhere to be seen in the shops. There are all sorts of little charms and triakete made of enamelled soldiers to hang on chains or brave- lets. and there are Khaki celoured warriors made of toffee, whole legisns of which are being annihilated by patriotic juveniles. another popular idea in cheap confectionery consists of chocolate '6 Ls ddite shells " which are also in great demand. In fancy articles there are dainty little menu holders made of a pile of arme, rocket knives after the fashion of the Lee-Metford rifle, scent bottles imitating a cartridge case, and of course artistically framed por- traits of Lord Roberts, Lord Kitchener, Sir Redvers tiller, Sir George White, and other heroes of the cam- paign. MEAT FANDERS.—A scallop shell is required fcr each person. Cut some beef email, line the shell with mashed potateerr, put in the meat, previously mixed with a little esion cut line, breaOcrumbe, and pepper and salt. Cover with more mashed potatoes, place a small knob of butter en each, and take a light brown.</p>
