      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>STEVE, THE OUTLANDER.</head>
        <p>STEVE, THE OUTLANDER.</p>
        <p>BY ARTHUR LAYCOCK, Author of "The Young Gaffer," "Seaside Sketeheat•</p>
        <p>"Lancashire Sketches," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS: Steve Hart, a factory operative, in love with Ella Rayburn, another factory hand, is compelled by the maliciousness of young Cartleigh, the factory manager, to leave his post. He determines to go to South Africa. Cartleigh intends, meanwhile, to win Ella from him. As soon as Hart has gone Cart- leigh lays his plans, and seeks Ella's company in and out of Work hours. By his disgraceful course of life he has alienated his parents, but, spurred by his pursuit of Ella, he determines to reform out- wardly, so as not to be cast off by them. He makes good progress with the girl, in spite of the fact that she pears from her absent love. By an incautious attempt to make love too quickly, however, Cart- leigh loses some ground again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>CHAPTER IX.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER IX.</p>
        <p>It was eight o'clock on a mid-October morning. The "buzzers" were droning out from a score of mills in Dalesworth and district. A biting, frosty wind swept through the streets, causing the few folks who were about to hug their garments tightly around them and hurry on their way. Little piecers were scurrying along,' some blowing their cold fingers; others, hands in pockets, were sliding on the frozen footpath. _ _</p>
        <p>From half-a-dozen cottages adjoining Cartleighs' mill quite a swarm of these lively youngsters rushed forth to the lodge-gates, bearing breakfast- cans, penn'orths of bacon, boiled eggs, and other comestibles for their minders.</p>
        <p>"Hurry up, yo' young beggars!" Ben, the ware- houseman, bawled to the shivering lads. "Get some o' that warm eytin' an' suppin' stuff into yore insides or yo'll be frizzed away to nowt but icicles, then what'll we do for big piecers if yo' come to such a cowd, frosty conclusion as that ? ' "By gow ! It's some an' cowd, lasses, isn't it?" he said, addressing several weaver lasses hurrying across the yard. "If this weather howds on much longer, I shall be wantin' to go to a warmer climate, to Afriky or—or th' engine-house. Eh, hulloa, Ella, is that thee? How's Steve gettin' on like? I reckon while we're bein' fruz up here, he'll be meltin' to a grease spot, eh ? " It was too cold to loiter about, and the girl, heeding not the garrulous warehouseman, passed into the shelter of the weaving-shed. Ben, however, was bent on gossiping this morning, and takinc, up his breakfast bundle and can he followed the girls, and a moment later seated himself in their midst, with a huge pile of bread and butter stacked up before him. "Life's nowt bowt company, eh, lasses ? " and he winked mischievously at Ella. "No, it's nowt when one hasn't company, 'that's what I said when I wed my wife. But one can have too much com- pany sometimes, eh ? If yo' yerd our crowd o' • kids squallin' at baggin'-time, an' most other times in between, yo'd think so." He munched away voraciously, chewing his words and his "butti-cakes " in rare style. "Company's all reet for some folks when they only number two individooals. Two's company, three's none, they say. But I say, let's have more nor three then we're sure to be happy; there's safety i' numbers. That's th' way 'at I try to console mysel' when I look round on our squad at home."</p>
        <p>Half of his bread and butter pile had now vanished in his capacious mouth. "Yes, stick to numbers, lasses, an' yo'll be safe an' happy. Not wed numbers like mine I don't mean. Have mony strings to your bow, dunno stick to an odd 'um Tak' no heed of th' chaps when they ax yo' to go a walk by your two selves. That's th way o' destruction. That way leads to confusion an' aw manner of evil, for that way leads to wed life. I know it's again' human natur'—specially feminine human natur'—to preych this doctrine. But yo' tak' heed of it. It's Ben Potter 'at's advisin' you, an' I've not bin born forty-nine year bowt larnin' summat. Tak' my advice, lasses, an' have nowt to do with chaps. Keep single, never double; thribble or anythin' afore double."</p>
        <p>"Eh, give us a rest, Ben. You're takin' away our appetites," said one of his auditors. "Give yo' a rest!" the jocular fellow repeated. "Why, haven't I bin givin' yo' a rest all this time ? Haven't I bin doin' all th' talkin' while you've been idly listenin', an' haven't I bin spoilin' my own appetite with it. I'm not th' least bit hungry now.' "I should think not, after spoilin' all that pile of tommy " (bread and butter). "Give yo' a rest, indeed," he resumed, heeding not the interpolation. "Yo' dunnot deserve a rest, yo' re'lly dunnot. Doesn't Mestur Shakspere lay: There's no rest for the wicked? ' An' aren't all yo' lasses wicked? Throwin' yore wicked glances about an' tantalisin' th' chaps. Eh, yo've summat to answer for I Give yo' a rest, eh! By gow I There's bin no rest for us poor chaps sin' Mother Eve started her pranks, an' it's my belief there'll be no rest for us as long as there's ony of her dowters livin'." "Yo'll kill a lot of 'em off with talkin"em to death," said the girl who had previously spoken. "Aye, well, better be talked to deeath nor worked to deeath, as Jim Dibbs said when he wur on strike, an' his parrot talked cheeky to him tellin' him to go back to his wark. But never heed. Yo' enjoy my silly nonsense, I know yo' do; and ony mon as con make a foo' of hissel' to entertain a lot of hard-worked wayver lasses is a wholesome benefactor to th' hurnan race, though he may not be looked on as such. A hau'porth of laughter is worth a bob's worth o' pills, an' if yo' lasses had no' some of my nonsense to help yore baggin' down yo'd have to lay out more brass on yarb (herb) tonics." "You mend worse as you grow older, Ben," said the weaver who acted as chief spokeswoman. "Youal never learn any wit, I'm sure." "Dunnot be too sure," Ben rejoined. "It depends what yo' call wit. Some folks think 'at they're varry clever because they are crom full o' larnin', an con talk about g'ografy an"rithmetic, an' bimettylisam, an' mesmirism, an' other sooarts o' isms; but if yol ex 'em how mony loaves o' bread an' pounds o"tatoes an' butter it tak's to feed seven kids an' two grown-ups for a week, well, if yo' ax 'em this question they're ilabber2asted. There's all sooarts of wit, Mary Anna, but th' mon 'at con understond th' everyday questions 'at consarn hisser an' those about hint is th' mon 'at'.B blessed with th' reet sort of wit an' gumption. "Young Mestur Ralph, I dar'say, thinks I'm a bit of a foo' because I aunnot read th' newspappers much, but if I were a single young felley, careless an' unthinkin' like as he is, I might find time to read such stuff instead of wrappin' my baggin' in it, an' then I might know as much about g'ografy o' Afriky as he does. But as it is he knows more about these subjicks nor I do, but then I know about other things far better nor he does. I dunnot consarn my-ser with what happens at t' other side o' th' world. I've enough to do with lookin' after myser on this side." - -</p>
        <p>--I'What was Mestur Ralph saying about Afriky ?" asked a dark-complexioned girl who worked in the same alley as Ella, and was much interested in the country to which her neighbour's lover had travelled.</p>
        <p>"Oh, he was just sayin' it were not so gowden as it were painted, meanin' as they couldn't shovel th' gowd up in waggonsful as they shovel coals at th' Bowoepit. He said 'at this Afriky were made lip of a lot of places, Capetown an' Johnnysburg, an' such like towns, wheer a lot of Dutchmen, made i' Garmany, lived, an' these chaps called all others :at come to live among 'em Outlanders. I howt myser it -were a varry good name to give these gowd-seekers. What think yo' he called Steve, lass ? " _ _</p>
        <p>_ This remark was addressed to Ella Rayburn, who, with her companions, had been hugely enjoy- ing Ben's chatter. "Aye, what do yo' think Mestur Ralph called Steve ? " Ben repeated, .as the girl hesitated. "I can't guess," she said. "'Steve, the Outlander,' he called him, and to me it seemed to fit the lad better nor Slopem's seven-an'-eleVenpenny suits fit me," and the burly fellow grinned at his quaint comparison. "Steve, the Outlander!" repeated the girls, and the name was soon bandied about in a fashion most displeasing to Ella. The company now • dispersed and work was resumed. Ella, as if part and parcel of the machinery before her, went about her duties mechanically and abstractedly. Ever and anon the soubriquet bestowed on her absent lover rang in her ears, and she felt embittered against the toan who had coined it.</p>
        <p>"He is holding Steve and myself up to ridicule," alle said. "It is mean of him, unspeakably mean.</p>
        <p>What right has he to do such a thing? 1 shall tell him just what I think about him," Then she remembered thal she had vowed not to have any urther dealings with him. That evening she was gladdened by a cheery letter from ,Steve. He was in Johannesburg, the Golden. City, and though he was not actually digging the precious metal from the earth he had secured employment as assistant storekeeper at a gold mine and was paid twenty-five pounds a month. He expected to save at least half this amount monthly, so that in a short time he would have quite a nice little "nest-egg." His prospects were exceptionally bright, and all was well with him. An inspiriting, hopeful epistle, full, too, of .endearing sentiments. After perusing it Ella felt supremely happy. Her susceptible nature was easily affected by joy or sorrow, sunshine or shower. Now for a time she was basking in the sunshine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
