      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>CHAPTER X.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER X.</p>
        <p>Ralph Cartleigh and his sporting chum, Barrett, were discussing their affairs in the smoking-room of the Hart Hotel, Oldham. It was but three o'clock in the afternoon, yet the dense, November fog which overhung the town enshrouded it in semi- darkness. The two men, who happened to be the only occupants of the room, were seated before a glowing fire which lighted up their angry faces. The conversation was as heated as the crackling coals in the grate. Barrett was in a towering rage. Cartleigh, who was not easily moved to noisy , wrath, was, nevertheless, in a nasty, ill- tempered humour. . "I tell you, Cartleigh, if you don't square up ',Pith me I'll make this place too hot for you." '3'Tis quite hot enough now, thank you, this place," indicating the room. "Oh, you'll not put me off with your cheap sneering. I know what you're worth." "Indeed? That's more than I do," rejoined Cartleigh. "If I could tell just what I was.worth, financially, of course, I might feel disposed to pay you your— How much do you say I owe you ? " "Three hundred." "Your three hundred. But not knowing my market or other value, I cannot undertake to promise payment of your little account." "You'll have to pay, Cartleigh! Make no mis- take about that." "Oh, gently, gently now! You are unduly exciting yourself. If I owe you all this money„ which I doubt, I'll pay you—sometime." "You'll pay me in a month or I'll raise Cain 1" roared Barrett. "You can.raise Abel, if you're able, for all that I care. But before you resurrect either I would advise you—" "Oh, rot! I want none of your advice," cried Barrett. "I want the money you owe me, not your advice." "Well, not having the rhino, my advice is the most precious thing I can give you just now. Now listen," continued Cartleigh. 'ln the past I've taken your advice and lost by it.. You have advised me to back so many 'favourites' that have turned out wrong 'uns that 1,. who was once favourite' with you, am now a bankrupt wrong 'tin. I shouldn't have owed you this—er--three hundred pounds if I had ignored your precious advice. But never mind that now. Listen to my advice, which I shan't charge you for at your lawyer's rate— three, nought, nought. My advice is this, Barrett. Before you kick up a row or 'raise Cain,' as you euphemistically put it, remember that in that event all your chances of getting any money from me will be gone for ever. Go down to Dalesworth, tell the guv'nor and the rest of the folks about our affairs and I shall be kicked out .of the place without a penny-piece: See ? Not that I should mind that so much, but in that beggarly, bankrupt state I should scarcely be able to meet a bill of three hundred pounds. I might meet it, trill:- but in my then forlorn condition it might, Ge( Samaritan-like, pass me by. I know that / should pass it by." He could not refrain from joking at his own expense, but his flippant words were terribly galling to the sullen, angry Barrett. "You see how you'll laid yourself, and me, Barrett. Now, on the other hand, if you possess your soul and pocket in patience, otherwise keep your mouth shut tightly, I may be able to retrieve my lost ground again, secure the confidence of my honoured pater and my dear betting friends—your- self, for instance, and again be in such a satisfac- tory financial position as will enable me to sett e your little blushing William—bill, I should say." "Oh, I've heard all that talli before, Cartleigh," the sporting man rejoined, in a sulky, surly tone. "I'm going to be paid up this time and no bunk m, so you'd best look to it." "Just as you like, my boy, just as you Ii c," Cartleigh replied, airily. "I've advised you as to your best course, but if you don't care to act on my valued advice you can, of course, do the other thing. I have no particular desire to again go through the trying ordeal of making myself agreeable to my fund-holder; it's too hard work, and, as you know, work of any kind is distasteful to such men as you and me. I have only one end to serve in sacrificing myself on the altar of duty—to pay your precious three hundred." Barrett threatened and fumed, but failed to make any particular impression on his erstwhile friend. Cartleigh remained calm and unconcerned, and as the sporting tipster raved unceasingly, smoked and drank as if in perfect ease. "You're a cool card, Cartleigh," Barrett said at length, when he had well-nigh exhausted him- self with loud and frenzied denunciation of the factory-master's son. "I've been a good friend of yours, Cartleigh, for many a year, and 'l've put thousands into your pocket, and now you calmly try to cheat me out of a paltry three hundred." For a moment the cynical Ralph did not speak, then, fixing his glittering eyes on the elder man, he said, in terse, measured tones: "You may have been a good friend to me, my boy, but you've been a far better friend to yourself at my expense. For every thousand you have put into my pocket you have taken five thousand out, and for calm, deliberate cheating, Barrett, I don't know another man who is your equal." The betting man fell back a pace at these words, and glared wildly at the speaker, then, with a murderous oath, rushed upon him. Cartleigh was ready for the attack, and by an adroit movement parried the lunging blow, and, closing with his adversary, tripped him backwards and threw him to the floor. The noise of the scuffle brought a crowd of Imen from the bar, and the two combatants were, after some diffieulty, separated. Ralph had sustained little injury, but an ugly bruise, caused by striking the fender as he fell, sliewed prominently over his right eye. , As he examined his disfigured face in the mirror he realised that it would be inadvisable to appear at Dalesworth in that condition. For three days, therefore, he absented himself from home, and the mill. His father visited the latter place several times and made inquiries, but no one had seen Master Ralph or had heard news of him. Late on ,the third evening the truant returned home. No iluestions were put to him, and he gave no explana- tion of his absence. For many weeks after this incident.. Ralph was on his best behaviour. He regularly and punctually attended to his 'manageria'l duties at the mill, absenting himself only on one occasion when Barrett called at the office and insisted on having an interview with him. Ralph went out with the betting man, and as they parted peaceably an hour later • it may be assumed that the meeting was a satisfactory one • so far as Barrettt was concerned. Ralph, too, though looking rather anxious, seemed. pleased that he had succeeded in quietly getting rid of the unwelcome visitor. As lie entered the office the cashier who had just returned from the bank, said: "Kindly keep your eye on this a moment, Mr. Ralph. I'm just going across to see Crowther." He placed the cash-bag, containing over four hundred pounds in gold and silver, on the desk and hurried from the office. Ralph glanced through the inner door into the adjoining office. The two ,clerks were bent over their desks making up the wages list. He closed the street door noiselessly. No one could observe him. With trembling hands he opened the bag The gold and silver therein was made up in five: ten, and fifty . pound packages. He quickly abstracted a fifty pound bag of gold and slipped it into _the back pocket of his frock coat, then closing the cash-bag quietly lie cast a scared glance at the two clerks again. They were still wholly absorbed in their work. Passing out at the street door and leaving it wide open behind him be entered the mill-yard through the lodge-gates and walked quickly towards the weaving-shed. He met the cashier at the door. "I was coming to tell you, Meadstone, that I must hurry away at once. My friend who called an hour ago has sent word he wishes to see me. Urgent business. Bye-bye." As he left the mill-yard and jumped on to a passing tramcar, the anxious cashier entered the office. - ------• •</p>
        <p>"It was thoughtless of Mr. Ralph to leave ther office for a moment with all this money about," said Mr. Meadstone. "Dear! dear And left the street door wide open, too! Just like him, care- less and thoughtless in everything. I'm sure I can't think what will become of him. I really can't think."</p>
        <p>A few moments later he had something serious to think about. The senior clerk had taken the cash-bag into the next office, and, with his com- panion, was dividing its contents into some hundred little piles. It was soon discovered that there was not sufficient money to pay the wages. "What 1" exclaimed the cashier, incredulously. "Not sufficient? How much have you there?" "Four hundred and nine pounds ten shillings,! said the senior clerk, consulting his lists. "There are fifty pounds missing, then. I brought from the bank four hundred and fifty.. nine. There must be some mistake. Count up. There were seven fifties of gold. How many packets have you there ? " The clerk counted them. "Only six." "It must be about, it must be about," said the excited cashier.</p>
        <p>Then began a search for the missing fifty pound&amp; "It has been stolen, sir," said the cashier to hhp employer who entered at this stage. "I left Mr. Ralph in charge of the money, and, unfortunately, he left the office for a few moments leaving the bag, which I had previously unlocked, for anyonfr to tamper with. When I returned here I found the street door open. It's a wonder the whole lot: was not stolen."</p>
        <p>"Careless, stupid fool ! " cried James Cartleigh,. enraged by his son's apparent neglect. "Someone has entered the office from the street, that is certain. Why they didn't take the lot, though, while they had the chance, I can't understand. The stupid, careless fool! Make sure that no one else about here has stolen it," he added, glancing suspiciously, at the two clerks. It was proved beyond doubt, however, that these young men were innocent of the theft. No other employes were suspected. When Ralph returned about noon he was apprised of the robbery, and unblushingly expressed his sorrow at the loss. _</p>
        <p>-"It was most careless of me to leave the office with the money lying about," he said, "but I was so concerned about my other business that I quite forgot the cash." The fifty pounds he had so cleverly stolen was, at that moment, in the hands of Barrett.</p>
        <p>"I've quieted him for a time, at any rate," reflected Ralph. "That is some consolation if there is any consolation to be had out of a business of this kind. But I can't go on pinching fifties in this fashion. I shall have to work soma other game."</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
