      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>LOCAL MILITARY CHANGES.</head>
        <p>LOCAL MILITARY CHANGES.</p>
        <p>Majqr R. H. Hall. who has since April, 1898, been second in command ot the Ist Battalion South Lan- cashire Regiment, becomes Lieutenant-Oolonel in command of the corps in succession to Lieutenant- Colonel W. McCarthy •O'Leary killed in action in South Africa. Major Hall joined the south LancsAire Regiment from the militia in Novemb,r 1873; became Captain in 1883 and Major ten years afterwards. He was adja!ant of the 2nd Battalion from 1883 to 1888 and prior to going to South Africa had seen no war service.</p>
        <p>Major T. Lainb, who has been returned as :wounded in South Africa will be appointed second command of the Ist b.itti,lion South Lancashire .."Riment when he recavers. He was a short time A'Rci serving at Warrington in command of the "Pei t companies of the regiment. Captain Prendergast of the 2ad Battalion else obtains promotion to the rank of Major in the South Lancashire Regiment. He entered the service from he militia in l'ecember 183.3 and got his company 111 the South Lancashire Regiment in January 1890. Major General Thankwell's Retirement. In consequence of the retirement of Major General Tiaackwell U.B. the command of the Lanca- shire Volunteer Infantry brigade has become vacant. 'General Thackwe'l who was years ago serving at Warrington as commandant of the Bth and 40th Regimen tal Districts was in 1888 appointed chief of the Mersey Volunteer Infantry Brigade Which consisted of Lancashire and Cheshire Volunteer Battalions. In 1890 when the Mersey Brigade was sub-divided, and the Cheshire and Lancashire Brigade formed, General Thackwell took over the command of the battalion, and he retained Command when last year the Cheshire battalions were formed into an independent hrigade, and the 'Lancashire Corps including the Ist and 2nd Vol un- test. Battalions South Lancashire Regiment became the Lancashire Brigade. As at present arranged, Colonel J L Boss, cora- lnanding the 20th Regimental District at Bury will become cornmaader of the Lancashire Volunteer Infantry Brigade, the let. 2nd and 3rd Volunteer ,Battalinn South Lancashire Fusiliers forming part of.the brigade and boibeging to his regimental dis- trict.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>MILITARY NOTES.</head>
        <p>MILITARY NOTES.</p>
        <p>Ex-soldiers who have served in. the South Lan-1 cashire Regiment and the King' Liverpool Regi- ment desirous of reenlistment will join what will be known as the Royal Lancashire Reserve Regi- ment te be formed at Preston. They will bs re- enlisted at Warrington but all men residing at Liverpool can be re-enlisted at Rupert-lane Barracks and those residing at Manceester at 62, Vietoria- street. Soldiers who have served in the Cheshire Regiment will join the Royal Northern Reserve Regie.ent which will be formed at Woking Bar- racks. it is well known that a large number of ex- soldiers in South Lancaehire and Cheshire are desir- ous of re-enlisting for temporary service. A large number of (ilea are being enlisted at the depot at Warrington for service in the King's Liverpool Regiment in view of the formation of the new 3rd and 4th battalions of the corps. Some time however will elapse b-fore these battalions can be regarded as real and eff-ctive units and for some time to come will be mere skeleton organisations. Lieutenant Colonel Gratton who has been second lin command of the Ist battalion Liverpool RPgi- meat which formed part of the force in Ledysmith, now comes home to take over command of the new 4th battalion of V:e regiment and he will as at pre- sent arranged join at the depot at Warrington in the first instance. Lieutenant Colonel Grattou was a short time ago serving as adjutant ot the 2nd Volunteer Battalion,livei pool Regiment. Major Cotton. who was a few years age serving I at Warrin,eton as adjutant of the 3rd Battalion King's Liverpool Regiment, will for the present act -as temporary second in command of the let batta- lion South Africa. The latter corps has suffred severely in connection with the defence of Lady- smith, and arrangements are now being made to send out large reinforcements to bring the corps up to its full strength. Captain Prendergast, 2nd Battalion South Lan- cashire Regiment, obtains promotion to the rank of major and will be transferred to the let Battalion of the regiment in South Africa. Major Wallis lately promoted to that rank in the 4th Battalion Cheshire Regiment. has retired from the service. He has been 18 years in the corps. having joined it in 1882. Captain Woodward has been promoted major to fill the vacancy and he joined the old 2ad Cheshire Militia in 1887. Captain Rathbone of the 2nd Volunteer Battalion Royal Welsh Fusiliers has been transferred to the 4th Battalion Cheshire Regiment and assumes com- mand of Major Woodward's Company. Lieutenant Jackson of.the 3rd battalion Cheshire Regiment, has been given a commission as Second Lieutenant in the regular infantry and will join the 2nd battalion Cheshire Regiment, which is now on active service. fie joined the let Cheshire militia in February 1899.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>FORGING A WARRINGTON MUSIC</head>
        <p>FORGING A WARRINGTON MUSIC</p>
        <p>DEALER'S NAME.</p>
        <p>Case at Chester Assizes,</p>
        <p>At the Chester Assizri on M.nalay, Henry Lyons, clerk, was charged with telowouily f4r4in4 a certain acqnittancs and reneipt fur moony for th surn ot £1 5, with intent to defraud at Th.,lwall, on the 19.,t0 April, 1899.—Prisoner pleaded net Euilry.—Vir. Trcv,ar Lloyd appeared to prosecute, and Mr. Colt Williams de- fended. _ . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Lloyd said that it apar,ared prisoner was in the employ of the Tao aai GrsppeanAll Choral Societv. On toe 19:h April, 1899, there was handed to him the money for the purposl of paying Br.,othroyd's account, and he came back with a receipt to game day, signed El Becthroyd. Things went en, and it fwas not found out until July, 1899, when another hill was sent in. Arthur Roebuck. Thelwall, said he was treasurer of the society and prisoner eves librarian. Ia April hot year the society hired a piano from W. Bcothrwd for the sum of 254. On the 19!-.11 April witness gave prisoner 30s. 25,, for the hire, and 54 for the carriege of it, and teld him where to pay it. The same afternoon prisoner brought a receipt signed "13. Beethroyd." The following July the Secretary received another bill for the arrioant. Witness went to the shop hut there was nothing done then, and they received another account last Christmas. lie had a conversation with Mr. Boothroyd about it, and then wrote to the prisoner, and received a note back asking him not to do anything until he had seen him. Witness saw the president and he instructed him to see the prisoner. witnees saw the prisoner, and he said he bad not paid the money,but he would get it that night.— By Mr. Cott-Williams: As treasurer,witneas had to pay the bills. He wanted the receipt that night,and ceuld not go down,so asked the prisoner to pay it. He said he would with pleasure. Be did not say he would give him a voucher, and that would do to pass the amounts. Prisoner had done a lot of work for the society. The Rev. Mr. Williamson was the president of the societe, and he said it was a bad case. Witness would leave been content if proceedings had not been taken: Prisoner had been honest as far as he knew. W. Beotlaroyd, piano dealer, 134. Bridge-street, War- rington, said the account was made out by him. There was no one with the sigaature H. Boothroyd,aud he had not auttiorised it.—By Mr. Williams: The signature was nothing like his writing P.S. Gregory depoceu to apprehending the prisoner. Mr. Williams for the defenee contended there was no attempt to forge the signature of Mr. Boothroyd,and no intention whatever to defraud. His Lordship said the qaestion of the writing did not affect it at all. If it was pat forward and meant te be the receipt for the man Boottircyd it was a forged document in the eyes of the law. If the prisoner bad meant to go and pay Mr. Boothroyd, then they might say there was no intention to defraud. The jury found the prisoner guilty, but recommended him to mercy. Mr. Lloyd said there was no conviction against the prisoner, but there were very serious matters if they were true. P.S. Gregory* said be had known the prisoner for the last eight years. At one time he was employed at the Warrington County Court, and he and hi a brother ab- sconded taking £5 or £B. No proceedings were taken against him. Witness bad made inquiries and found that prisoner had celleeted £3 15s. belonging to the society which he had not handed over. His Lordship said that forgery sometimes was a very serieus offence, but in this case he was willing to trod it rather as a case of stealing money intrusted to him. It was rather a bad ease. aPrisoner was sentenced to nine c t7eniar months' hard abcur.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, ITIDAY, MARCH 16 1900.</p>
        <p>scribed, and he considered the working rren of Warring- ton hal done their duty to the inttitution. (Hear, hear.) It teal necessary that the working men should use every endeavour to keep up their subscriptions, and if possible to increatie them. Trio di`Ecair..io4 they Liad encountered during the las' few rn.settis on account of the many ether calls m,cie upon working men were suh that was no surprise that tl'e toi of Um eub•criptions was somewhat less than in the ['nylons yiar. In fact, be thought they had occasi m to be 11;0.4 satisfied with the result. (Applause). Oa tho m.,ti uof M•. BINe, secen led by Mr. CR )S4. KILL , Mr. D. F.tnehaw,•wua unanimously re-Aected vice- chairman. Mr. FANSHAR briefly retuned thanks. HON T ;MABIIIIEI3 AND MON. SHCRETARY. Councillor ROBRIITS said tie was sorry to rofr,rm the meting that their hoe tr iasurer (Councillor T Burton), was unable owing to ill-health to again take office. A gentleman who took a great deal of interest in the in- stitution and woo had rer,dsred valuaWe s-rvice to it— he referr-ti to. Mr. John Tunstall—ha:t kindly coosewed to occupy the position, and he had great p'eaure in moving his election. (A.pplause ) Councillor WILIEINION c mded. Mr. FANSHAW supporiV, and the moti.m was heatily carried. Mr. Tr:INSTALL returned thankr. Mr. G. WALIEZI3 proposed the re-election of Mr Joseph Taylor as boa. secretary. Toey all knew, he said, how earnestly Mr. Ttylor had labeured and how he devoted hit time with)ut grudge to further the interests of the institution. Mr. W. DAINTITH seconded, and it was unanimously agreed to.</p>
        <p>VOTES OF THANKS. Mr. WALKER prop 'soil a vote of thsnks to the chair- depaty-ohairman, treasurer mid secretary for their past services. In regard to Cluecill,r Burton he moved also that an expression of sympathy be forwarded to him from the meeting in addition to an acknowledge- ment of his past services. Mr. G. Co rE a seconded, and it was carried. Oa the moidon of Mr. FAIRCLOUGH, seconded by Mr. CncssuLn, a vote of chanki was accorded to the hon. medical officers and the senior and junior house sur- geons. Dr. Fox acknowledged the vote as the senior hon. medical officer. Votes -•f thanks were also passed to the matron :sod nurseA, the chiof constable and police, Mr. T. J. Down and the members of his band, the Press and the Mayor —to the last-named for presiding and allowing the use of the Conant Chamber for the meeting.</p>
