      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>I WARRINGTON INFIRMARY AND DISPENSARY.</head>
        <p>I WARRINGTON INFIRMARY AND DISPENSARY.</p>
        <p>A Large Adverse Balance. The annual report of the Warriagten Itifirmary an-1 Diepensary for the sear 1899 has just bsee issued. REFOUT 13Y TUh. BOAIW OF "MA.N&amp;G'IMENT F.l.l7Arlia7islug he variations of income and :expendi- ture, tae BeArd o ago toe,,nt state that the expenaii- rue- te £146 17 , 5 in exeese th it of taa pre- cenieg year. Thie is doe in part to Isteli r rates of avaees and increased prices of cemmeditiee, b the con- stent growth in the wotle of the Distitution ts n teraliv acconepaeied by a correspo !ding iacrease of expenditure. The income, as cotnpived with the p -:c. d•rig year, show e a reduction of £375 as 9d To Coe extent of £193 6 , 7t this is accounted ter by the fact that in toe year 1898 three half-re .r 1 y divi- dends were received from t to Lancashire and Yot k hire Raitwae' Company. The donations, however, are £272 8s 9d lower. Ota the whole the aeclonts are worse trate those of 1898 to the extent of £522 Os 2d, and vrtitst in that Year the adverse balance was reclined by the sum of £434 49 91. it, is now increased br £B7 lbs s', and stands at £789 15s 21. It is hoped the friends of the Institution will di all they can during the current year to increase the rincome, more especially as it is certain that, in some direttiens, there will be reductions. Though the number of patieets treated in the house shows a considerable increase, the average number of beds oceupied during the year was, within a fradeion,tlae same as in 1893, viz., 31 Time cost per occupied bed, after allowing the usual 13 8 , per head for out-oatieets , and 4s per head for home-patients, was £57 3; 91, as against £57 10,k 8d in the preeeding year. Tini Board ' have to record with regret the death of Mr. Thomas Ghzebrook Ryle..nds, one of the trustees, and of Colonel Greenall and Mr. John flartley,who were Life Governors of the Irdaitution. SENIOR ROUSE SURGEON'S REPORT. Dr. E W BATTLE,the senior house-surgeon,in his report on the- work of the institution states that during the veer 1899, 562 patients were admitted to toe Infirmary, 373 bei;er male,s,tend 189 ternales. This &amp;lows an increase of 98 patients over the number for the oreceditsg year. Of ,:tiese 454 were surgical cases ; 108 medical. The eecidsats included ameng the eurgieal C M 405 were 260. 30 -loathe occurred during the year, 17 belne due to acc'dent, 13 from disease. In connection with the dispensary, 1,501 new home patients were treated during the year, shearing an increase of 487 over the erevious one. 5,361 visits were paid to patients at their eenti homes. rhp new ont-patiente numbered 2,s66,giving an increase of 222 2,393 siccident a:4 emergency cages were treated during the year, showing an ineteese of 280, thus making a total of 6,460 Patients treated at the Despen- eary. The average turinner of beds oc-upied was 31. The year was mark- d by a large increrse in the number of patients treated both in the Infitmary and at the Dispensary, and in connection with tile home-visiting. On Nevember Ist, the Nursing Staff was increased by , the addition of one probationer, the Medical Board tieing ( of the opinion that the then present staff was insufficient o meet the requirements of the Institution.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting of the Workingmen's Committee. The Mayor (Aldermen Renerts) presided over the annual me tin,: of the W. eitinemeles Committee of the Warringtp lefirmare ani Dieleueare, weich was held in the Town Hall on Saturtlay afternoon. Amongst Roberts others present were :—Councillor Jobe R h(hC__ meet of the Cemenittee), Mr D Fa.nshaw (vice-chairman), Mr Joseph Taylor (hien secretary), the Rev 11 V Pigot, Dr Fax, Dr Battle, Mr John Fairclough, Mr J L Tunstall, Councilor Wilkinenn, and Messrs J P Taylor, W Crete- kili, A Blake, G Walker, J Elliett, and W D siateth. EINANCIAL STATEMENT. The financial statement, which was submitted and adopted. showed that the subseriptione amoented to £689 03 91, less £24 04 5 expanses, £665 01 4d, which had been handed to the treavarer of the Infirmary, a balance in hand of £lO with which the year was starte being rettired. In 1898 the total of the subscriptions was £712 10e 6d and, with the expense 3 only £l4 53 31, the slam of £698 5s 31 was given to the institution. A statement was alse submittel showing that during lastevear £59 143 101 had been subscribed for the Ntar:,- hag Asgociatien. THE MAYOR AND THE INSTITUTION. The MAYOR delivered an aldrese, daring the coarse of which he said that he heel had pte7 before him a few weeks ago by Mr Councillor Jahn Rebores. their worthy chairman, a few facts,in c mnectioa with the operations et the infirmary clurine the past year. Tile uuraber of patients treated in the Infirmary beds had been 562, the horn petien,s he'd amoented to 1,501, the out-door patients: 2,566, and the accident and emergency ceees 2,393. making a grese total of 6,460 patients. Those fieures spoke volumes for the necessity and usefulnece o the inetitution, (Hoer, halo) It must he exceed- ingly satisfactory to find that as a result of their labours the contributions of the working men of War- rington toed been so genetmus and ise well sustained for sech a long time. The total amount of subscriplons for the twenty years during which their orgeeiserieu had been in existence amounted to no less than £8,901 1.4 e sd, after payment of all expeases. That was squat to an average of £445 10a per annum. (Hear, hear.), boeelise back at 1830, when the fist year's subscrip- tioes only amounted to £165 19 4d, it spoke very highly indeed in proof of the permanent and well-sustained in- tertst which working men had shown in the institution. fie wee quite aware that the sebscriptions this year must have euffered some entailment on acceuat of the generous response which had heel made to the appaal on behalf of the men and wiye3 and children connected with the sad war now raging ia South Africa Tne people of Warriagton and eseeeially the work- me' had shown by theer weekly Subscrip- tions, constantly kept up, that in regard to the war they had done their duty. (Hoer, hear.) At the same time. however, the corst:ibutione this year to infirmary funds were only less than those of last year be £133 4,11 d., 'and he boieved their Chairman could oXer a very satisfactory explanation of that. He need net iespress upon them the desirability of giving con- tinued support to the institution. There was ample proof that they felt quite as keenly as anyone in War- rington the value of the institution, but as the popula- tion of the town had increased, and, was increasing EI) rapidly each year the necessity for bring- ing the claims of the institution to the notice of those won came into their midst from out- side in connection with the various developments of in- dustry they saw on all heeds made it necessary that their orgenialtien should be kept up to date and the influx of as much additional blood as possible brought into it. (Hear. hear.) In addition to the very handsome annual subecriptien to the Infirmary Fend they had taken utoier their protection to a certain extent the Nurses' Association of Warrington and district, and had rairel for it derieg the last year so less than £59 14i 101. Of courati that association was to some Went kindred to the Infirmary. Their interest had not been coufieed to the old institution entirely, but had been allowed to spread itself to the subsidiary one.which they were all agreed was doing a great amount of goad in the town. (Applause.) GENERAL AND EXECUTIrE COMMITTEES. en the motien of Mr -TIP r e.Amon,seconded eMr CROSS KILL the delegates present were appointed a general committee with power to add to their number. The following were elected an executive committee : Messrs J Roberts, Devid Fanehsw, T Burton, Joseph Taylor, J P Taylor, G Lenge, PT Crosskill, T Thornton, C Simpson, MeeMillan, E Griffiths, T Wadsworth, C H Hibbert, J F Wariate, C Wetkies, A Sinks, T loce, W Gleave, TCerbett. W Deinteth, N Einmerion,F T Bennie W Todd, John Harrison, W Jackson, John Wilkinson, John Hartlerand J Everett. CHAIRMAN AND VICE-CHAIRMAN. Councillor WILKINSON proposed the election of Coen- cillor John Roberts as chairman. Me. J. P. TAYLOR, who seconded remarked that Coun- cillor Roberts had rend:red great service to the iustitu-</p>
        <p>tin, The motion -'as unanimously carried,. Geuncillor ROBERTS, in returning thanks said he had had 20 years of office without, a break, and had willingly doEc eve: thing he could to support and further the interest of the institution. Twenty years ago the inati- t•iition was very mush in debt. Indeed,he was sorry to say that at the present time it was in debt to the anse.unt of marly £BOO. But twacty years ago the a.nount was wiry c insiclerable. At ens time the annual subscription fe •in the working men did not amount to Imre than £5O or £6O. The smallness of the amount wAs the cause of the organizltion of the Working Men's Committee. SC "780 close upon £9,090 had been sub-</p>
