      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>CRANE FATALITY AT DALLAM-LANE SIDING. -_ Coroner's Jury Make a Recommendation.</head>
        <p>THE CRANE FATALITY AT DALLAM-LANE SIDING. -_ Coroner's Jury Make a Recommendation.</p>
        <p>An inquiry was cenducted at tbe Police Station, War- rington on Monday afternoon. relative to the death of John Owens, 49 years of age, a forge labourer. of 9, Town Hill, Warrington, who died from the result of in- juries received at Dallam-lane siding on Tuursday morn- ing last. Mr. F. R. Roberts represented the relatives and Messrs. Thomas Atherton and C Bunch(works' manager) were preseet on benalf of Messrs. Pearson and Ineo wles. The first witness called was Mary Owens who said the deceased was her husband and was a forge labourer in the employ of Messrs. Pearson anu Knowles. She last saw him alive at nine oarlock en Thursday morning. He told her that he had objected to being put on shunting in wet weather and net in dry weather. He bad been shunting for a number of years when he lost the sight of one eye. The other hid since been failing. Tke Coroner asked what conclusion they were to draw from that ? Mr. Roberts said it was that they had put him to work he shoald not have done and which ke protested against. He objected to being put on okunting. Witness said he bad told her teat he was put to work that he was not capable of doing. Oa the previous Thursday he saw Mr. Hoult the foreman and asked why he had been sent to shunt in we; weather. Hoult said he could do nothing until Mr. J. J. Atherton came back. Mr. Roberts said deceased did not los!. the sight of his eye through an accident. It was through cataract. George William Jones, crane driver, said he took charge of the crane at six o'cloek ee Thersday morning. ' About 9.1.0 deceased c ime te within about a hundred yards et the crane and shouted to witness that the ' crane, then in the siding, was to be taken to Bawsey. ' Deceased had not bona sent to assist him. After taking the crane back over the points witness wens forward towardi deceased. He did not see deceased get on the crane. The crane was driven at about four or five miles lan hour. Alter going about a quarter of a mile, and eiter jibbing to face a curve, witness heard a scream. He stopped and found that the deceased had been run over by the crane. Deceased most have been keocked off the plate. By Mr Roberts : He allowed no one to sit nn his crane. By doing so he would be liable to a fine. He did not know that decea.sed was on the crace. When he had seen persons riding on the crane he had ordered them off. James fault, foreman at Dallam Forge, said he sent deceased with the message shorly alter nine o'clock. He had be tell the crane driver to bring the crane to Sammy. Deceased worked in the locomotive ebed, and had that morning been cleaning the pit out. He saw deceased on the crane, but was toe far away to warn him. The accident happened 120 yards from w here the crane started. By Mr. Roberts : He did net see deceased get on the crane. Mr, Roberts; Was it reasonable to put the Man On shunting? The Coroner °aid that if the evidence was to be believed the man had been sent to do whet the smallest boy in the plice could have dno. He had simply to take a message. He toot it, and then did what be had no right te do. The thing was in a nutshell. A Juror : ; Was it net risky to send a man nearly blind down the line? Witness : He had only to deliver a message. The Coroner said that if juries thought it was risky the employers would very naturally reins() to employ men with defects. They would not run any risks. Be Mr. Roberti: He had no need to go on the line. He (witness) told the foreman shunter to take cbarge of the crane and pilot it along the line. The deceased had nothing whatever to do with the crane but simply deliver the message. Joseph Rathbono,foreman shunter said Lie was told to pilot the crane. The crane had moved 50 yerds when ho got to it. Deceased was then aiding on it. He (witness) got on the other side and gave the driver instructions. He did not speak to deceased ai he was a man of experience and had formerly ben witness's manager. He (witness) left the Grano and went in front to keep the croseing clear. He thought deceaned would have the sense) to get off before he gotno the crossing. The crane driver could not possibly see deceased and witness bed not toll him he was there. Ho saw the deceased killed by being knocked off the plate and run over. By Mr Roberts: He did not know whether be binsself had permission to get on the crane when it was travel- neg. It was a common thing for men to get on the crane. William Eldridge said he was on the crane with the driver. He heard Owens shout to the driver to bring the crane up, but did not know he afterwards got on the crane. The Coroner said the jury bad nothing to do with the question as to whether the dependents were entitled te cempensatien under the Act of Parliament. They had to say hew he came by his death, and if there was any criminal responsibility. If not, it must come under the category of an accident. The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death," but expressed the opinion that Rethbone who red" for a short distance with the deceased on the crane ought to havo told him that there was a certain amount of danger and that it was his duty to get off. The Coroner expressed his agreement with the recom- mondation of the jury. Mr. Atherton said the firm usel every care and framed rules which experience taught them were advisable to prevent men from getting in accidents. Joseph Rathbone was then called into the room and acquainted with the decision of the jury. The Coroner added that it woule nave been much better if he (Rath- bone), had told the deceaeed to get off. Mr. Rathbene said he did not doubt but that it would have been better if he had done so. The deceased, how- ever, was a man of great experie.nce and at one time he (Rathbone), was working under the dsceased. He there- fore naturally thought that the deceased would 5e hie danger and get off at the crossing. Tne Coroner said there was no reason why he (Rath bone) did not remind the deceased of his danger. Mr. Rathbone : You don't always think of these things. rho Cormier said the works' manner told him that it did not effect the recompense to dependents whether the deceased's death was due to negligence or not The firm had a scheme by which all men who came by an accident were recompensed and the dependents received all the advantages of the Wsrkmen's Compensation Act I whether the accident wss doe to negligence or riot. ' (Hear, hear.) Mr. Bunch said the matter was left in the hands of a works' committee which granted any sum they thought fit under the terms of the Ant. If the firm considered the grant too large they reserved to thernselvee the right of e.eming before a C enaty Court judge te fir the amount of compensation. The inquiry then closed.</p>
      </div>
