      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>A special Board meeting Nem held on Welntsday morning, Mr A J Jolley presided and there were also present : Rev W H Dtunarnend, Dr Patterion, Mrs Clay, Messrs S Davies, T B Malkin, J Billing% Wham Hickman, T 13 Ashcroft, E L Bent, H Hibbert, J Clarke William Houghton, AJolley, J Degnuaa, A Brooks, Henry Thornton' G Pilling, with J C Sutton (clerk), S Sutton (assistant clerk), C H Ward .(workaouse master). A Wright (superintendent Padg ate Schools), William Owen' (architect). ENTERTAINMENP AT THE SCHOOLS. Mr. WRIGHT (superintendent) rein:tete that a malic lantern entertainment was given in Padgate Indus- trial Schools on March 9th by Quartermaster-S!rgeaet Burke. THE CHILDREN TO VISIT THE EX3IBITION. The CHAIRMAN said he had received a letter from Messrs. King and Pigot, secretaries to the promoters of the Arts and Crafts Echibition at Parr Hall, inviting the children of the house and schools to inspect the ex- hibition on Thursday afternoon. The letter stated that the children would be admitted free of charge to the American Midgets' entertainment. He moved that the invitation be accepted. Mr. ALFRED JOLLEY seconded, and the motion was agreed to. APPOINTMENTS IN CONNECTION WITH THE WORKHOUSE. Tie House Committee reported that the Master had been empowed to engage a temporary assistant during the illness of the porter, and to .lay for the same out of his petty cash. Nurses Allen, McCormick and Taylor having completed twelve months service mstisfecterily, it was recommended that their salaries be increased in accordance with the terms of their engagement from £25 to £27 103. per annum as from January 3rd, Feb- ruary 9th, and February 21st, 18c10 respectively. Miss Browne having tendered her resignation the same was accepted and it was recommended that the Clerk give her an official testimonial to her satisfactory service. It was recommended that Alice Barton be engaged as assistant to the matron to take charge of the infants and young children and to parform such other duties as may be allotted to her at an annual salary of £2O with uni- form, rations &amp;-. The minutes were approved on the proposition of the Chairman seconded by Mr. Hickman. THE OPENING OF THE NEW INFIRMARY. A meeting of the Infirmary Committee was hold on the 9th March, Mr. Houghton presiding. A letter from the Right Hon. Sir .1. T. Hibbert, president of the Poor Lew Conference and Chairman of the:Lancashire County Council was read, in which he stated that he agreed to open the new Infirmary builainles on some day to be fixed in May. It was decided that the new buildings should be opened on Thursday, the 31st May, and that the Chairman of the Committee (Mr. William Houghton) and the Chairman of the Board of Guardians (Mr. A. J. Jolley and Mr. George Pilling) lea appointed an Execu- tive Committee to take in hand the programme, and to carry ovt all arrangements for the opening day. With respect to the formation of the reads to the new build- ing, the architect stated that by reducing the epecifica- time of the proposed foundation in the roads from burr stone to forge clinkers and redwing the quantity of mae.adarn, and also reclining- the widths of the roads from 15r,t: to 1217 t. an estimate might be obtained of about £620. After full consideration it was resolved that the main read from the Workhouse grounds to the front of the Infirmary sieould be made a width of 15 feet and that the road to the back of the buildings next the railway should be 12 feet and that the road from the old hospital buildings to the Infirmary over which the food would la! wheeled fer distribution iis the Infirmary buildings.should be ale° 12 feet wide. It was also resolved that the last named road should be either asphalt or concret by preference instead of macadam. The architect was instructed to obtain further revised estimetes from the three competing contractors and to present them at the meeting of the Guardians on Wednesday morning the 14th inst.—The estimates having been opened it was found that they were as follow : Mr Bennie £619 14s, end Mr C W Davenport £632. It was resolved that Mr Bennie'e tender be recommended for acceptance 1)7 the Board. Mr. Horroirrox moved the adoption of the minutes which were carried. THE ESTIMATES. The CLERK presented an estimate of the Board's ex- penditure for the half year ending at Michaelmas,l9oo.To the common charges of the Union exceptional additions were made of £3,375 to meet deficiency of £32,000 loan account, to furnish the new infirratry and to supply</p>
      </div>
