      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>Brooke, Bond's 1/3 Tea</head>
        <p>Brooke, Bond's 1/3 Tea</p>
        <p>—Delightfully Fragrant and Refreshing— is sold by 30,000 Lgents. Brooke, Bond's, other Teas are sold at 1/2, 1/4, 1/8, 2/-, 2/4 &amp; '2./8 per lb.</p>
        <p>Those who like to have infcrmation presented picturesquely, will b 3 inter- ested to learn that in o-a?, vi,3ek the quantity blended of Brooke, Bond's Tea was sufficient to supply a CUD of tea to the whole of the British Troops now engaged in South Africa, the whole of the British Troops in India, all the English Colonists in Canada, Australia and New Zealand, and ev,-;:ry man, woman and child in England, Scotland, Ireland and Wales. When all these were supplied there would still be suffi- cient left for fifteen million (15,000,000)</p>
        <p>more people.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>ROOKE,</head>
        <p>ROOKE,</p>
        <p>BR•OKE _v2E 3</p>
        <p>ROOKE/</p>
        <p>water mains and coenections, anJ of £1,030 f3r altera- tion and irnoroeement ef workhouse. Toe total esti- m tte is £17,233 Si whiln after deducting receipts amounting to £3,197 leaves a total of £14,036 51 to iam p-ovelPd for. It; conclusion the Clerk states that It is unnecessary to repeat what was written in the state- meat of M trch 1899 and Septemner 1899 and what was further exolained in a rpcent paper on finance except to to say that increases of the county rates of the past and present half year had redueee an anticipated balance in the Trearnr,r's hands by £7OO. It was also now found hat £1,509 for furniture and laying out of land Would meet about one half of the actual cost. Tbe purpose of the Board kad been to effect all important improvements decided upon without addition to the loan account and without increase of the ordinary sixpenny rate. To avoid departure from thr.t purpose it had become of more serious necessity to proceed with other exeeptional works by slow hta4es. How slow would depend upon , the state of the Board's finances at the close of the next , half year. It was unwise at this dat,in view of prob- able increases of outlay under important hmds to reckon upon any surplus at Michaelmas next in excess of a reasonable workable balance yet it might prove suffi- cient to admit of other contemplated works being under- taken without further borrowing of money and without incretse of the sixpenny rate. The CHAIRMAN moved the following resolution "That the estimates prepared by the Clerk be approved. That after approval by the AFsessment Committee of new eupplemental valultion lists precepts be prepared by the Clerk for contributions by overseers of townships after tbe rate of sixp 'nee in the £ upon ars.ssable value and for county rates on the basis of the county precept" He remarked that while the tYs)lution expressed approval of the estimates it did not bind the Board to a policy of carrying out certain improvements out of revenue. Mr. Pilling seronded and the resolution was carried. THE PROPOSED OFFICER'S RESIDENCE AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>r. B. SHEARIIR presented a report of the Newton Re- lief Committee dealing with the proposed establishment of an officer's residence at Earlestown. It stated that at a meeting of the committee held en the 6th inst., ale. t'r was read from the Local Government Beard with refer- ence to the building of a relief station and relieving officer's residence, asking the Guardians for their reasons for building an officer's residence. The committee state that the present premises are ill-adapted for the adminis- tration of relic', and hold by the Guardians on suffer- ave.. The committee are unable to find a suitable build- inc to be let. They consider that it will be to the advantap of the public to have the officer's residence attached- The district is very large in area, some of the , applicants living miles away from the relief station. and it would b 3 an advantage to the paupers for the officer's residence and office to be together a, ic would enalom them to find him much easier than if he were living a distance from the office. The committee were else of opinion thst an office and committ.e room alone would be such a small building that it would probably be im- possible to get the plans passed for a suitable position. The committee were unanimous in their opinion that the house and office together would be in every way more convenient, and reqoested the Board of Guardians to ask the Local Government Board to issue an order ' empowering the Guardians to purchase land and erect suitable offices, committee room and dwelling-house. Mr. BILLINGII proposed the adoption of the report. Dr. Parreesure secenii6d and said from a business point of view the Board would save money by building an officer's residence. Mr. Flineerie said h. diff qed from the conclusions of the committee and objected to the erection of an officer's house at Earlestown. M.asuceorr moved an amendment that the ofEeer'e house be left out.</p>
      </div>
