      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <p>ling )01 use and</p>
        <p>MARCH 16TH, 1900.</p>
        <p>1111 E EXA EH,</p>
        <p>COMBINING masculine Moral Aspects of the intellect with fine War. ethical sympathies, Mr. LECZY is just the kind of man to review the moral aBPects of the war in a quiet and convincing tuanner. Accordingly, ws turn to his careful and shiest exhaustive contribution on the subject in the "Daily News •' with the conviction that he malice, and extenuate her set down anything in and we have not been disappointed, The Survey is conceived throughout in a dispassionate 4,udicial temper, and with a c.reful avoidance of Irritating personal wrangles. Mr. LECKY thinks We had no warrant to arrest the military prepara- tions of the Transvaal. "An intelligent military llesPotism " might have done it ; a Government bound by conventions was compelled to respect their tenour and obligations, and to pay regard to the Dutch feeling at the Cape. Avoidance of war Was permanently impossible, he thinks, where °lle White population was held in subjection to another. Equal rights might have been granted, and friction avoided. But there were irreconcil- able elements. The Transvaal Government was "not Only different from, but profounely hostile to, the Whole colonial system of England." This is a new and a most important point,and it will bear studious exaraination. It has been examined in our own free Colonies, and the result we know. We give equal rights, and expect to receive their recog- nition in return. English disqualification, how- ever, was the" corner-stone of Transvaal policy. Incurable dislike and distrust of British ideas and airns was implanted in the mind of the Transvaa poers. We could have wished Mr. LECKY had en- -1 arged upon this point. He lays stress upon it ne proves that it e-risted, and he has to argue from it by way of explaining much that is mysterious. But he has not fully accounted for it. The policy f°llewing Majuba Hill will not explain it. The surrender has proved "a great calamity" he thinks. It would have been better to have des- cribed it as a great misunderstanding. Mr. GLAD °NE desired to reconcile the two great races in South Africa. He thought he had doneit,and the eoffobrt was worth something; but it has turned out t e unsuccessfel. Why it has been unsuccessful is the mystery. It was unsuccessful before the Raid, and it became doubly so afterwards. Mr. CHAM- kERLAIN's policy of making Johannesburg a dis- tinct self-governing municipality would have saved the Transvaal. The Boers had derived wealth and influence from taxing the mines, and they Were unwilling to surrender the power. This form of capitalism has to be borne in mind as well a the other and more frequently presented side. Mr. 1415c10r censures us for lionising Mr. Rhodes, and he doubts whether Mr. Chamberlain's negotiations and speeches were "always judicious," though he laboured hard for peace. The Transvaal was less Utious,because it had become "the most powerfu 1 State in South A.frica." Nevertheless, public Opinion in this country would not have sanctioned War With the Transvaal in October last but for the Ultimatum and the invasion. Undoubtedly, he is ight, and the Boers will live to see their mistake T he final pacification is declared to be "a task Which must tax the highest resources of statesman- The English and the Dutch have to live f`egether, and on terms of amity and equality. The °ruler are proud and the latter stubborn. Per nit anent coercion and subjection are impossible. ..,eeal autonomy and faleratien are suggested. A 'ahellghttul estimate of Mr. LECKY'S survey must give it a high place. It is conceived and written in the temper of the true historian.</p>
        <p>Tux weekly revenue returns Our National Resources, exhibit the effect of the rush to get goods out of bond that were expected to be affected by the Budget. The actual increase of receipts for the week is over two Millions, and that sum will probably be deficient in the next weekly return. Tea, tobacco and spirits are on hand sufficient to meet the public demands tor weeks to come, and the holders of these stoc ks Will be in the position of receiving the extra duties and putting them into their own pockets. How- ever, none of the additions to indirect taxation Will be burdensome, and as the ultimate payer in every case is the consumer, it would be well if the illustration were brought home to the muddled 14dividuals who are always dreaming about making the foreigner pay to our taxation by putting some :,!1 on the goods he sends us after they arrive in Zis Country. We tax China and Indian tea, and dllerican and other tobaccos, but the producer a"a Slot pay the tax at all. It woull be just the laNtne We taxed !manufactured goods of any kind. a .(3 lised them hero we should pay the tax here, nd the foreigner would escape. The only way to tax the foreigner is to impose a tax on our ex- 'orts, at the time of export, when the shipper would pay, charge the duty on the price, and get the Price and the duty from the foreign customer. on coal exported abroad finds advocates "ci'llow. One shilling a ton would probably pro- lice a considerable sum, if the foreigner would e°lltlnue to buy the coal. But even here the vi' 11°18 of the coal exported could hardly be made to Pay. Some of it goes to distant ports for the lit se Of British merchant ships. It would be unfair ° tax that, and coal-venders in Hong Kong and *3l,;se where would bn outside tke jurisdiction of the juritish Customs officials so that either special arrangements ought to be made or there would be tmuch friction. In time to come, when our coal uPPIy is visibly declining, exporting may be taxed or it may be altegether forbidden. For the pre- sent the home supply is ample, and coal must be, regarded as one of our largest national resources</p>
        <p>When it declines to a perceptible extent many</p>
        <p>other things will decline with it, including national income. Hence the wisdom of keep- ing down and trying to clear away our national debt. Two hundred years hence our national income may fall, and our expenditure will have to fall with it. We may import coal, but its price will affect the cost of produc- tion, and that, in turn, will diminish our ability to compete in the industrial world. No review of our national resources is fair which omits all re- ference to the probable decline of our coal supply. Sir H. FOWLER, curiously enough, omitted all re- ference to this contingency in his otherwise encouraging estintate of our national financial posi- tion on Tuesday night. Imperial expansion may add to our supply of men for defensive purposes, it may add to our income for these objects, and it may improve our coal resources. But unless it should do the last, and unless we can hope for substitutes for coal, as effective and as cheap, a time must be regarded as possible when our national resources will be on the down grads. Naturally, the subjeet is a painful one,and nobody is thanked for calling serious attention to it. At the same time, no large estimate of present and future resources is complete that is not tinged with doubt as to the permanent maintenance of existing favourable conditions.</p>
        <p>In a sense we are entitled to say that the war is now half over, for the British flag floats over the capital town of one of the two belligerent States. Lord Roberts entered Bloemfontein on Tuesday at the head of his army, the enemy retir- ing northwards. Some of the pronainent Free State officials met the British Commander outside the town and if they did not welcome him with open arms they hauled over the "keys of office" with unexpected willingness. Most of the inhabitants appeared glad to see the victorious troops, recognising that for them there would now be peace. Lord Roberts announced his great suc- cess in modest terms, but with significant allusions to the "late President," and the "late Executive Government," which were rather amusing reading at this side of the world and in- dicated, perhaps more clearly than anything else, the completeness of our recent successes in the Free State. Johannesburg and Pretoria are the next objectives, and once Buller and Roberts get a footing on Transvaal soil they should not be long in bringing the struggle to an end. That end will apparently have to be fought out for the latest despatah from the two Presidents regarding peace terms was not entirely free from that insolence which characterised the famous ulti. matum,</p>
        <p>Mr. Sheldon's ideal newspaper is reported to be a gigantic success. The plan under which the author of "In His Steps" has been enabled to run a journal for a week is very simple. The editor of the Topeka Daily Capital lent him that organ for the limited period—and the limitation of the period has a good deal to do with the boom. The religious world of America is ready to subscribe for a religious daily for a week,and the advertisers have realised the fact—thousands of advertise- ments having been rejected. The first experimental number of the paper has been practically monopo- lised by the famine in India and temperance did- actics. But ther 3 are no lessons as to the horrors of drunkenness,such as are to be found in the bare records of fact in our 'own police-court reprts. There is not a single Stock Exchange quotation, although presumably the new editor does not hold we have reached the day when buying and selling should be ended. There is, of course, no theatrical news. Even an Independent Theatre, with Rad Ibsen lessons, would not be noticed. It is somewhat difficult to understand why such a journal should be called a newspaper. It does not reflect the events that are passing around. Its ostrich-like indifference to the currents of human eYistence does not augur for usefulness or for a prolonged sale. The most admirable feature is the selection made in the matter of the advertise- ments. It is to be strongly commended as an ex- ample to the teligious weekly newspapers of our own land, where appeals to faith and credulity jostle each other side by side in the letterpress and haziness columns.</p>
        <p>India continues to suffer in an appalling degree. There is no diminution in the famine area, no hopeful sign of a cessation of distress. Including Madras, over five million sufferers are now em- ployed on relief works, but this does not represent anything like the amount of misery which is being endured, and from which the exhausted people are dying in droves daily. What is most serious of all, the resources of the Government cannot stand the strain indefinitely. There is the infinitely horrible calamity in sight of an increasing famine and an exchequer totally depleted, so that there could be no increase in relief works without help from with- out. We fear the British Ge•vernment will have to withdraw from the position they have taken up in respect of the suggested grant in aid.lndia cannot be left unassisted. The Mansion House Fund is most slender, owing, as we believe, to the way in which the original suggestion for a fund was botched by the India Office. The Secretary for India shoul have thrown India upon the mercy of this nation frankly and unreservedly. Had the central fund been opened in London instead of Calcutta, and an eloquent appeal addressed to the British people we are convinced that the response, in spite of the multitude of domestic claims, would have eclipsed the great total of two years ago. As things stand the fund is disappointingly meagre in comparison with the claims upon it. and we do not see how a national grant can conscientiously be avoided.</p>
        <p>Prudential Assurance Company, Limited. The fifty-first annual meeting of this company was held on the let instant and another year of steady progress was reported. A carefully prepared summary of the report will be found on our first page. The total number of policies in force at the end of the year, it appears, was 13,433,785; and their average duration exceeds eight and three- quarter years. The 9,4 sets of the company in both branches are £36,832,059, being an increase of £3,232,351 over those of 1898. The staff provident fund which was founded two years since for the benefit of the outdoor staff, shows a very satisfac- tory increase for the year, the total amount standing to the credit of the fund be- ing £73,085. Such facts convey but a very meagre idea of the extent of the company's operations. The policies in force are equal to more than one-third of the population of Groat Britain, and another paint illustrating the scope of the company is found in the fact that the Prudential has paid claims on one sixth of the total number killed since the war began. Some curious incidents have occurred of men having been reported as killed which have afterwards turned out to be erroneous. One in particular may be meationed. A man in the Argyle regiment was reported by the War Office as " killed " awl in a few days later as " seriously wounded," followed at a short interval by "died of wounds." at Cape Town. The amount of the assurance was paid to his widow, or to be more strictly accurate his wife, for his relatives were not a little aston- ished a short time afterwards to hear that he had safely reached Netley Hospital. Another re- markable circumstance occurred in relation to a number of the Gordon Highlanders, who were re- ported not only as killed but buried by the Boers. Eleven claims were paid and the Hignlanders were afterwards found to he prisoners at Pretoria. The relatives have willingly repaid the amounts with the exception of one who very cautiously says she will only do so when she receives a letter from her husband. It is also worthy of note that the 68 volunteers who have left Warrington for the Cape were insured for the sum of £lOO each in the Pru- dential Assurance Company. Mr J T Rowland is the energetic superintendent for this district and his offices are at 69, Sankey-street.</p>
        <p>• FREE TO EHRIDIATIO AND PARALYTIC SUFFERERS, an Illustrated Book on the complete horn Cure of these Diseases, preface by F. W. Cole. M. 0., who regards the • a hor as the highest authority. send st imp for book to the VE$O IPTEITITO'TE, 18, Devonshir-qtreet, Man- chester. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATSRS.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MARCH 16, 1900.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MARCH 16, 1900.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES</p>
        <p>CHARITY CONCERT AT itUNCO2N The Halton-road choir, on Monday evening, pro- moted a most successful charity concert, at the Public Hall. As a result a handsome Stl:7l was ealised for Mr. James Harrison, 4, Wiverns place, who is incapacitared from earning his own living, by an acaident which occurred aboat 8 years age. GOSPEL UNION MISSION, WIDNES Of4Nlonday evening, a lantern entertainment was given in the above Hall by Mr J Millir. The slides were descriptive of South Africa, and numbered ' about one hundred. ihey were elucidated by Mr J Millor, and were greatly enjoyed. Mr A &amp;awry I superintended the limelight apparatus. MILTON P.S.A., WIDNES.. Thera was only a modente gathering at this ser- Tice'on Sanday afternoon. The chair was occupied by Mr George Lacey (one of the vice-presidents), who, in place of the five minutes' address, sang a S3lO I entitled " Take time to be holy," which was well I received. —A very good add re-s was delivered by Mr A Barney on "'The hand end the wing," Mrs Pedlar sang two solos very effectively. BRIDOING THE MEROEY AT RUNCORN GAP." This was the subject of an admirable lecture delivered by Mr William Handley. of Runcorn, at Widnes in connection with the Free Library of the town. Mr H B Shores occupied the chair, and a good attendance listened inter- estedly to Sir Handley's able historical discourse.— At the conclusion Mr D Lewis proposed and Mr Williamson seconded a vote of thanks to the lecturer. A similar vote was accorded the chair- man on the motion of the lecturer. NON-PAYMENT OF RATES. At the Runcorn Petty Sessions, last Monday, 81 moos of default of payment of the Runcorn Urban District Council District and General Rate were heard and dealt with. Evideace was given by Mr. W. Sutton (Assistant Overseer). 'UNLAWFUL WOUNDING AT RUNCORN. At Chester Assizes on Tuesday Nicholas Johnson (20), labourer, pleaded guilty to unlawfully and maliciously wounding his wife, Mary Johnson, at Runcorn, on the 20th January. It was stated that prisoner, when in a fle of passion, dealt his wife a violent blow with a poker, inflicting serious injury. Mrs Johnson inforaued the Judge that he had hitherto behaved well towards her. Prisoner Was sentenced to two calendar months imprisonment. ACCIDENT TO MRS. Js. TAYLOR, LIELSNY. On Tuesday morning Mrs. James Taylor. Helsby, met with a serious accident while cycling, which resulted in her right leg being badly broken near the ankle. It appears that Mrs. Taylor, who is an expert cyclist, was riding in the vicinity of the Telegraph Manufacturing Company's Works. Her bicycle slipped and she was thrown. Mr. Taylor who was in the works at the time,immediately pro- ceeded to her assistance. After receiving first aid Mrs. Taylor was removed on a stretcher to her residence, and was afterwards medically attended to by Dr. Brian% She is now progessing as favour- ably as can be expected. POLICE INSPECTION AT WIDNES. Sir Herbert Croft, Birt., her M ,jesty's Govern- ment Inspector of Police for the Northern Co untie', inspected the Widnes Police on Monday. There ware present his Worship the Mayor of Wilmes,and Captain Ibbetson, chief constable of the county. 'I hose on parade included Superintendent Keighley, ' two inspectors, six sergeants, and forty-fire can - stables. One sergeant and several constables were sick, whilst Sergeant Bennett and P.C. Worsfold were in the office., and a nember of men on duty Sir Herbert iaspscted the clothing and accoutre- ments of the men, after which they were put through a course of drills by Sergeant Ratcliffe.— 'At the conclusion, Sir Herbert expressed himself perfectly satisfied with everything he had wit- nessed. He suleseqaently made inspections at Ditton and Hale Police Stations. NARROW ESCAPE OF A CHILD AT RUNCORN. ! List, Saturday merning an accident, haopily at- tended with no serious consequences, occurred in aridge-street, Ruee.era. r 4.3 cyclists Albert Bate 0 Amelia-street. Warrington, and Robert Maxwell, Tilley-,.troet, Warrington, were preceeding down the iaeliae fron D eph Bridge, when Bate, who had no lei•eak, lost control of his machine. Coming ,va the bre v., at a fast rate, he observed a boy with a wl4elharro ve nsar the Market Hall, ant in ,-reontr, to avoid the obstruction he accidentally kaockel down the infant daughter of Mr. Samuel Barker, pork butcher, Bridge-stre.Nt, and finally col- lided with the iron railings in front of the building known as flops Hall. Mr. Barker happened to be in the street at the time, and picked up the child, only to find that, it was his daughter. Both the cyclist and the child were attended by Dr. McDou- gall, and it was found that the latter had escaped with an abrasion of the skin on the right arm. The injury Bate sustained was not regarded as serious.</p>
        <p>_ _ O.A.XLAND-STREET P.S.E. WIDNES. There was only a moderate attendance at the above meeting on Saturday evening, when Mr 1-1 Mcßride presided. The programme had been left in the hands of Mr. T Williams, the secretary of the Primitive Methodist Choir, Runcorn, and without doubt it was a musical treat. Mr W Hopwoon, the prize winner at the Widnes Eisteddfod, was warmly encored in his songs, "rile mighty deep," and "The Bandoliero.' A hearty reception was accorded to Miss Hetherilizton, who rendered the items" I couldn't, could I ?" and "Come lasses and lads "in a pleasing manner. Miss Saunders delivered with Iconsiderable effect the recitation" The Cavalry Charge," and she was recalled. She also recited. "The Bishop and the caterpillar," a piece which created a great deal of amu4ement. Messrs. Lunt and Hopwood gave a beautiful interpretation of the duet " Excelsior." and were vociferously encored. Songs, Ste„ were also rendered by Messrs W Lunt, S Becket c and Master J Edwards. Mr Sydney Beckett accompanied in a very able fashion. A vote ef thanks to the artistes, on the metion of the Chairman, concluded the meeting. A RIFNCOM CONTRACT LEADS TO BANKRUPTCY. At the London Bankruptcy Court on Wednesday, la sitting was held before Mr. Registrar Clifford for Ithe public examination of Vincent James Barton, iron merchant, 25, Walbrook, London, E C., who applied to pass upon accounts showing total liabili- ties £6,660, and a deficiency of £2,097. Under ex- amination by the Official Receiver the debtor stated that he had carried on businese for the last forty years as an iron merchant and contractor. His failure was partly due to the loss of an action in connection with the fitting up of the Runcorn works of the Lancashire White Lead Company; also to bad debts, and to the insufficiency of profits to meet, ex- penses for some years past. In December, 1397, he contracted to fit up the Runcorn factory with Iplant and machinery for £13,000 payable in shares of the company. About the same time he made a sub-contract with Messrs. H. B. and R. S. Daglish, engineers, of S. Helens, to supply and erect part of the plant for £2,315. That firm commenced pro- ceedings against him in August, 1898, for the re- covery of a balance of £587. He defended the action,partly on the ground that the plant supplied was not in accordance with contract, and he counterclaimed £537 expenses and £2,300 damages. The action came before the Official Receiver in April, 1899, and judgment was given for the plain- tiffs. He appealed to the Divisional Coact. who allowed the appeal, but Messrs. Daglish in Novem- ber last took the case to the Court of &amp;ppeal, who reversed the decision of the lower courtond eventu- ally Messrs. Daglish instituted these bankruptcy proceedings.—The examination was ordered to be concluded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>CASE OF OYFRLLYINe AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>CASE OF OYFRLLYINe AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening last Coroner Dobson, held an inquest at the Runcorn Police Court. on the body of Thomas George, infant son of George Thomas and Harriet Higgins, who died on the boat " Dido " at "Runcorn on the 11th of March.—The mother of the deceased said her husband was captain of the boats" Lien" and" Dido," and employed by lileFers Simpson, Davies and Co. The child was about four months old. On Saturday night witness and her husband went to bed as usual with the child sleep- ing between then. She awoke about 6.15 o'clock on Sunday morning and found the child dead. She got •up and dressed, and subsequently she told her mother-in-law and Dr. Delaney was sent for. Deceased was their only child, and was insured for 30s. Both parents were teetotalers.— Dr. J. B. Delaney said ha had seen the body of the child and examined it, about a little after 7 o'clock on Sundey morning. The deceased's body was then cold. There were no marks of violence upon it. There were traces of blood congestion on the side of the head which might or might not be due to pressure. Having carefully inquired into the his- tory of the case and from his examination of the body, he was of opinion that death was due to suf- focation caused by over-laying. A child of such tender years would be very easily suffoeated. There was no question of convulsions or anything ot that nature, lie would like to add that the bunks in the beats being rather narrow they mild easily un- derstand how the child would be smothered.—The jury returned a verdict in accordance with the me- dical evidence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT.] The nominations for vacancies on the Urban Dis- trict Chanel" are to hand, In Halton, Heath, New- town, Bridgewater Warai=, the retiring candidates Niessrs 8 Taylor, John Wilson, Geerge Hewitt, mil J W bightburn have been returned unopposed. No nomination was received for Mersey Ward, and Mr II G Grice will resume his seat by virtue of the Local Groverninent Board general order 1803. For the Cantrai Ward, however, there are three cindi- dates : Messrs S raylof, T Wood, and J if Salkeld. Ur Taylor retiree on account of being returned for Halton Ward, and it is not unlikely that Mr Salkeld will give way to Mr Wood, who by the way repre- sents the Runcorn Ratopayers' Association. There- fore, the probability is that the Cntricil will again be reconstituted withoutan appeal to the polls.</p>
        <p>The estimates submitted by the Runc3rn Board of Guardians and the Runcorn Rural District Council, at their usual meetings last Tuesday, call for nothing beyond ordinary observation. The Guardians aek the Overseers for 7,1. in the £, whilst the Council express sati nfaction at being able to manage their affairs on 441. in Ltie X. The former rate is equal to the last cad, but that of the Cauacil is a decrease of id in the X on the corresponding period, and a reduction of 4d in the .£ on the last call.</p>
        <p>The attitude of the Rural District Council, in re- fusing to entertain an application to pass a resolu- tion supporting the Transporter Bridge Bill—(aeross the Mersey) —may give rise to remark ; and, to many, may appear an unkindly action. However, the Council is faced with a grave consideration— one, which, hitherto, has not received as much public attention as it should have done—the con- sideration that the promoters of the Transporter Bridge will not give an undertaking not to use the Bridge as a means of conveying a brine service pipe across to Lancashire. This new issue was not men- tioned at the Council meeting. 0 ! no! But then there are Councillors wide awake enough at Dut- ton to "see beyond their noses," and doubtlesß this "undercurrent " caused them to fight shy of the request.</p>
        <p>On the same account, I understani the Transpor- ter Bridge Bill is likely to receive serious opposi- tion in its course thiough Parliament—(if it has not already done so)—from the Cheshire County Council; toe Weaver Trustees ; the Salt Union, and numerous other Cheshire undertakings. They will sarely take up arms against the possibility of Cheshire's staple industry being conveyed to the County Palatine. If, therefore, the bridge promo- ters are not content to be purely Bridge Promoters, I see little hope of the project getting through amicably. This morning (Friday). the Runcorn Urban District Council hold a spacial meeting for the purpose of considering the question of the Transporter Bridge, and, possibly, this contingency may receive consideration along with other points.</p>
        <p>When writing on this subject before, I strongly advised the support of such a bridge as the Trans- parter Bridge provided theifour councils—Cheshire and Lancashire County; Runcorn Urban District, and Widnes Town Councils—were agreed upon its feasibility as a means of intercommanicatioa be- tween the two counties at Runcorn Gap, and still wish to emphasize that opinion, but that the bridge might be used for a purpose detrimental to the welfare of Cheshire never occurred to me, and I can only express the hope that in the end we may have abridge and preserve our brine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>OTIOLLL</head>
        <p>OTIOLLL</p>
        <p>On all sides Runcorn footballers have been con- gratulated upon their achievement of Saturday last. As illustrative of the genuine sportsmanship of the Oldham Club, it may be mentioned, that the secre- tary of that organisation will oue of the first to congratulate the Runcorn Club. Mr. Samuel Taylor (president of the Runcorn Club). and Mr. C. J. Hughes (secretary of the Cheshire Football Associa- tion) are amongst others who came forward with congratulations.</p>
        <p>Runcorn enter the arena on Saturday next with the intention of making a bold bid for the Northern Union Cup, Their opponents (Birstall), are looked upon as easy prey, but in this age of surprises it is as well not to think too lightly even of the most inferior opponents.</p>
        <p>A coincidence worthy of remark is to note that not one of the Runcorn Rugby Football Clubs, in- cluding the junior one% have suffered defeat this season upon their own ground.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>LITERARY AND PHILOSOPHICAL</head>
        <p>LITERARY AND PHILOSOPHICAL</p>
        <p>SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the above was held in the Masonic Els.ll, Bold-street, Warrington on Monday even- ing. The Rev. Morley Stevemon presided,and there was a fair attendance. ELECTION OF PRESIDENT AND VICE-PRESIDENT._ _</p>
        <p>Ceuncillor A. J. JOLLMY proposed the eleetion of the Rev. W. H. Drummond as president of the socie'y. All the members of the society, he said, were familiar with Mr. Drummond's abilities as a speaker. It was his (Councillor Jolley's) pleasure to have him as a colleague in several branches of public work, and he knew better than many people his capa- city for business. He telt sure he would maintain in a very high degree the best traditions of the society which Mr. Steveesen had so ably filled during the last two years. Mr. BIIROISS seconded. Tee Rev. MORLEY STEVINSON said that as retiring president he was very glad that the choice had fallen where it had, as he was perfectly certain that the best interests of the society and the honour of the chair were being placed in the hands of one who would fill them worthily. (Hear, hear.) TN; motion was unattimously Carried. The Rev. W. H. DRUMMOND said he hoped that when elr. Stevenson retired into the position of vice-president he would extend to the society the same interest that he had shoein during his years of presidency. It had un- fortunately happened occasienally that the election of a gentleman as president of the society bad been the sign for him to disappear from the ranks as soon as his term of presidency was over. He was sure Mr. Stevenson would not do that. He had been so valuable in the way he bad conducted the business and in the new interests he had introduced that he (Mr Drummeed) accepted office withal( the greater pleasure.feeline that they were still having his assistance. (Applause.) Everyone would have been pleased had Mr Ed Isten agreed to ac- cept the presidency, and had be been willing he (Mr Drummond would not have allowed himself to be nomin- ated. As Mr Elelsten' however, could not see WS way to take the presidency he (Mr Druinmene) had pleasure in accepting the position, and he thanked them for the election. (Applause.) Dr WRITE proposed the election of the Rev Merley Stevenson as viee-preeidene. It went without saying, he said, that anyone who was a good president could be a good vice-president, if he would only attend. With Mr Dremmond as president, and Mr Steveneoa as vice- president, the interests of the society would be in good hands indeed. (Applause.) Dr. Fox seconded, and the motion was heartily carried.</p>
        <p>Tile Rev MORLEY STsvEssarr thanked the members aincerely for the honour they had done him. ne could only say that during the two years he had had tho honour to be president he had taken not only the great- est possible interest in it, but it had bean to him the greatst possible pleasure and privilege to fill the enair, however uoworthily. Anything he could do in any capacity to further the interests of the society he would do from the bottom of his heart. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>SECRETARIES, TREASURER, AND COUNCIL. Dr. Goaneu., proprsed the following elections : Me!srs. Eielsten and Carruthers (incentarie4), Mr. Si!- cock (treasurer), and the Rev. F E Powell and Messrs. A Bennett, T May, T Rymer Young, C Madeley and 11 N Boughton (council). Mr. KENYON seconded. The motion was unanimously carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>LATE MR. T. G. RYLANDS.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. T. G. RYLANDS.</p>
        <p>Dr. WRITE proposed that a vote of condolence should be forwarded to Mrs. Rylandg and her family in the loss she hsd sustained by the death of hot. husband, Mr. Thomas Glaz,hreok Rylands. He said that Mr. Bylands had a great deal( to do with the formation of the Literary and Philosophical Society,, and was the first president and president on sev.9ral ntfzer occasions. He also read several papers. He was an ideal president,and it was ewirg a great deal to his personality that in ths first year the Sscirty had 70 members. Mr. MAY seconded, and it was carried.</p>
        <p>LECTURE BY THE REV. F. E. POWNLL.</p>
        <p>The Rev7-lEr--O—WELL read a Paper on "Souse interest- ing qiestions arising out of ths relation of psychol,,g9 to metaphysics and physiology." He dealt with the difference between (1) philosophy ; and soience and (2) metsphysics and philosophy ; the confusion of metaphssts and psychology, and the relation between philosophy and piychology, exemplified in the mind or conszioasne,s ; the "free will " c mrorersy, the theory of apperception, and the soul or eye. Toe lecture was followed throughout with the greateit kterest. On it conclusion, -</p>
        <p>Dr. GOKNALL prlpr)s.ld a vate of thanks to the lecturer. _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor A. J. JOLLY Seconded, Mr. C. MADELEY supported, and the motion WaB heartily carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnig,htiv meeting of this Council, was held at the Workhouse Datton, lat Tuesday afternoon. There were present :—Mr. J Knowles (chairman), Admiral Jones-Parry, Mesirs, T Lewis, P Leather, T Darlington, T Baxter, J Spruce, Jos,sph Nickgon, C Roylance, W Fiether, E Bretherton, E Clark,, JTeylor, J G Kydd, T Wright, J B Chadwick, CT Pilling, A 11 T‘lioott. with Mr. George F Ashton (clerk), Mr J3senh Farrington (inspec- tor), Messrs W Diggle and J Swiaton (surveyors) and Dr. J Adams (medical cffic...er).</p>
        <p>HELSBY SEWERAGE SCHEME. £35) RD:WIRED,</p>
        <p>At a meting of the Hnlsby Parochial Cornmttee, held on March s:h, in accordance, with n.otice of motioes,Mr. J. C. Taylor moved, that application ba made to the Lcal Government Board, for their sanction to a loan not exceeding £350, to cover the expenses already in- curred in connection with the proposed sewerage scheme For the parish of Helsby ; and, that the Local Govern- ment Ssard be requested to extend the repayment of the loan over a period of O years.—The motion was seconded by Mr James White, and on being put waa car- ried unanimously.—The committee also approved of the report of a sub-committee respecting several sites for the pumping station and sewage purification tanks, and tne Clerk was asked to ascertain the prohlble cost of wayle eves connected therewith. On the motion of Mr. J. Taylor, the Helsby Parochial Committee minutes were approved.</p>
        <p>THE TAX&amp;TION OF CYCLISTS.-WHAT THE COUNCIL</p>
        <p>THINKS.</p>
        <p>Mr Algernon Fletcher, clerk to the Northwich Rural District Council forwarded a copy of the memorial of that body to the Chancellor of the Exchequer, and invited the Runcorn Rural Council to suppers it. The memorial expressed the opinion that the time had arrived for the imposition of a tax on motor cars, light locomotives, and cycles. There was no reason For tax- ing vehicles drawn by horses, which did not apply with equal force to neetor cars and cycles. Good roads were essentill in one case as in the other, and a contribution towards the coet of the highways was surely a reason- able levy. It was suggested that every motor car owner and cyclist should be required to take out an annual lieense, collected as in tue case of sporting and dog licenses. The amount payable for such license might be fixed at a sum which would not burden any licensee; but, having regard to the number of motor cars and cyclists in the country, the aggregate sum real- ind would be large, and woule be available for a grant in aid to every highway authority. Mr. Bradford proposed, and Mr. Spruce seconded that the Council support the memorial.</p>
        <p>Mr. Georgt:ling moved a direct negative. Mr. T Baxter obaorved he should ;ilge to sae cyclists tx4d, and the money devoted to the repair of the roads. It was only reasonable they should be asked to nay a small tax, for the roads had now to be kept in condition for them, and they got a fair share of the roads.</p>
        <p>Mr. Kydd supported Mr. Pilling, although he felt that cyclists should pay some small losal tax towards main- taining local roads ; if it was to be an imperial tax how- ever, he objected to it. Upon a show of hands there was a decided majority in support of the memorial. Mr. Pilling : All right,! wi'.l give in. (Laughter.) Mr. Wright : Perhaps Mr. Pilling ridei a bicycle. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>DELEGATE 3 REQUIRED.</p>
        <p>The Lord Provost of Aberdeen (Mr. John Fleming) forwarded an invitation to the Rural D;strict Couruil, to attend the annul congress bf the Royal Institute of Public Health, to b 3 held in Aberdeen, from Thursday, August 2nd, to Tuesday, August 7th (both days inclu- sive). Mr. Pilling gave notice be woul2 at the next meeting submit the names of the requisite number of delegates.</p>
        <p>WIDNES AND ETTNCORN BRID6E.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Alsop, Stevens,Harvey, and Crooks, solicitors, Liverpool, wrote inquiring whether the District Council had passed a resolution in support of the scheme. If not, they hoped the Council would see their way to do so within the next few days as they expect the Bill will come before the Select Committee in the Hous3 of Com- mons next week or the week after. No action was taken in the matter.</p>
        <p>WAYS AND MEANS.-TIIE ESTIMATE SATISFACTORY,</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Wright submi,ted the basis of cuntributions required from the overseers, for ensuing half year, end- ing at Michaelmas, 1900, for consideration, being calcu- lated on the assessable value as per valuation lists in force. The lea•al capital for the current half year indieg Lady Day, 1900, amounts to £6,304 and the nett prob- able expenditure is estimated at £3,356, which shows a loalance at Lady Day of £2,948. The probable expendi- ture during the ensuing half year ending edichaelreras, 1900, as set out in the detailed estimate is as under -- General expenses (public health). £700; less grant under Agricultural Rates Act, 1896, £7O; highway expenses, £2BOO ; salaries, £l6O ; less grant under Agricultural Rates Act, 1896, £374 ; repayment of principal and in- terest (Highway loan). £160; balance and contingeney fund, £2500; total, £5876. The total armunt required for ensuing halt year ending Michaelrna,s 1900,ia £5,876, and after deducting the probable balance in favour of the parishes at Lady Day, 1900, the amount required by contribution orders from the overseers is £2,928. The assessable value of the district as per valuation lists in force is £151284. A rate of qd in the £ will produce the sum of £2835, and it is recommended that a call for the slam of £2928 be made upon the overseers in accord- ance with detailed estimate after deducting the estimated balances in favour of each parish at Lady Day, 1900, being a decrease of a iin the £on last half year. The foregoing calculations are subject to alterations which may be made in the valuation lists previous to the Con- tribution orders being signed. The estimates were considered very satisfactory and approved.</p>
        <p>-RREORT ON THE DIVERSION OF HAY-LANE, WALTON.</p>
        <p>Tbe Council appointed to conFiiler the proposed diver- sion of Hay-lane, Walton Inferior, and to consider the application of Messrs. Heenan and Froude, the contrac- tors to the Loudon and North Western Railway Com- pany, for permission to lay a temporary line of railway across the said lane, met on the ground on Wednesday the 21Ith February, and at a subsequent rneetino it was unanimously resolved that it be a recommendation to the Council that the railway company be asked to straighten the lane on the north westerly side of the pro- posed new viaduct and so take out the present dangor- oils corner and they be informed that the' Council consent to the brook, which runs under the lane into the river, being diverted further w Nit and the Council would agree to the company laying the 24 inch pipes for carrying the said brook under the road and into the river instead of extending the culvert which at present exists. The Council then considered the application of Messrs. Heenan and Fronde for permission to lay a temporary line of railway, and it was resolved that the application be granted. Subject to an agreement being entered into.</p>
        <p>THE MEDICAL OFFICER "MAULED OVER THE COALS."</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomis Wright,naoved that Dr. Joseph Adams be rPappointed medical officer of health for the year ending 25.1 h March, 1901, at a salary of £l5O. In doing so, he was quite aware that the doeter's attendances at the Council had not been what they expected of him. In- deed, he had been rather lax in coming there; and when he had been" hauled over the coals" as it were, he had, perhaps, not taken the matter quite as seriously as the District Council might have expected. At the same time, be (Mr Wright) wished to say, there was not a more caDable medical officer in Cheshire than Dr. Adams. Further, during the Nat few montha,there had been vrry considerable excuse for him, for Dr. Adams had been engaged in passing many hundreds of recruits; and he did not think that the Council would seriously delay anyone's ring to the front. (Langhter.) He had also had it from Dr. Adams, that very day, that his at- tendances would be better in the ,future than in the past. Several guardians : Bosh ! and laughter. Mr. Wright continuing: And there will be a qualified assistant in attendance when he cannot attend person- ally. Mr. A. H. Talbott seconded, but, wished also that the Chairman would remind Dr. Adams for the future to show proper respect to the chair by rising to address the chairman during discussions. (Hear, bear.) Mr. Syria observed that he liked to address the chair upon his feet, end felt sure they all I,ked to see their servants do the same. (Hear, hear.) Mr. E. Clarke thought that Mr. Wright bad spoken in very mild terms. HJ was really disgusted with him (the doctor.) He had watehed him for the last two years, and he might say so far as in him lay, he (the speaker) would not support the Doctor's re-appaintment. As to Dr. Adams being one of the best medical officers in Cheshire he would say nothing about that,for pe.rhapa Mr. Wright had more experience of him than he (Mr. Clarke) had. If in order, he would move that the Coun• cil ;dvertise for another medical offic-r. The Chairman said Mr. Clarke'e motion would be in order.</p>
        <p>No one:seconded ?dr. Clarke. The original motion to re-appoint the Doctor was then put, and carried. Mr. Clarke asked for a shove of hands. The Chairman : I don't think it II( ceseary in this case, as tilers is only the ono propesitioa. Mr. Bydd did not want the Chairman to say any hard things to the Doctor, but thought a suggestion to him would be sufficient.</p>
        <p>Mr. Clarke requ^sted that thi Council should ask the reporters not to make a note of it. Mr. T. Wright : I think the reporters will do as they like in this matter Dr. Adams was then called ante the room. an acquainted by the Chairm-tn with what bad transpired. The Dictor thanked the Council for the appointment, and added that all the points mentionfd would be regarded in tht future. He thanked them for the re- newed expression of vnfidence.</p>
        <p>THE RESPONSIBLE PARTY.</p>
        <p>Admiral Jones Parry painted out the difficulti6s of te C)uocil, in fixing the responsibility for the carrying out of proper mk•ag-areA in cas?s of infrctious diseast,s, upon Eicher the Medical Officf,r or the NiIiSaIICO LepPctor, and with a view to settling the mattcr once and for all pro-</p>
        <p>posed the following resolution :—" In future cases the medical officer of health on being notified of the infect- WWI cases will give what instructions he may deem nee- cessary to the Inspector of Nuisances and will supervise all steps taken in which premises have been disinfezted." That rmlution, said the Admiral, would make the Medical Officer answerable to them, and cases could be dealt with without the loss of time hitherto experienced and without the shuffling of the responsibility on to the Council; back to the Medical Officer ; and again on to the inspector ; and back and to as it had been going on. (Hear, hear.) The reiolution was ulanirnously adopted. This was all the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The usual fortnightly meeting of the above Board was held at the Workhouse, Dalton on Tuesday, when there were present :—Mr. George Pilling (presiding) Me3srs. Samuel Taylor. A It Norm in, T Wivnolds, and Rev Dr. Gastaldi, the members of the Rural District Councii,with Mr George F Ashton (clerk), and Mr Joaeph Cunnah, (master.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>ABSENCE OF CHAIRMEN.</head>
        <p>ABSENCE OF CHAIRMEN.</p>
        <p>The Clerk xplAimed that Jttr. M3se,ley Williams was prevented from attending owing to suffering fram a bad cold. Mr. S Woodward (vice-chairman) was also unable to attend :—Subsequently Mr George Pilling was elected to preside.</p>
        <p>THE SALE OF LIQUOR TO CHILDREN. CPT OF THE PETITION.</p>
        <p>The following is a copy of the Petition the Guardians forward to Parliament respecting the sale of LAdor to children :—" To the Honourable the Comm-4ns of Great Britain and Ireland in Parliament assembled. The humble petition of the Guardians of the poor of the Run- corn Union in the County of Chester. Sheweth that your petitioners regard the practice of permitting children to be served with intoxicating liquors in public houses RS one fraught with serious moral dangers to the children Wu served, aid pray your honourable house to pass a bill prohibiting the sale of intoxicating liquors to children below the age of sixteen years. The Common Seal of the Guardians of the Poor of the Runcorn Union was here- unto affixed in the presence of Chairman and Clerk." 'lhe petition was unanimously approved-</p>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDLNCH COMMITTEE RE-APPOINTMENTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Chadwick (Weston Point), submitted the report of the School Attendance Committee, as to the remunera- tion to be paid to the Clerk for his services to the com- mittee during the past sear, and as t 3 the re-appoint- ment of the School Attendance Officers for the Bud worth, Frelsham, Halton, and Weston Districts.—The com- mittee recommended that the Clerk be allowed £35 in respect of his services—The committee also recom- mended the re-appointm nt of ne following School AttendAllcs Officers, at the salarias mentioned : George Jones, £35, Frodshani ; J R Wilkinson, £35, Budworth; Samuel TAnlinson, £1.5, Haltos ; and W B Brimelow, £l5, Weston.-- the committee also recornmendtd that cells be made upon the overseers for * of a I.d for sclaoel attendance expenses. The repJrt was unanimously adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>FINANCIAL STATEMENT.</head>
        <p>FINANCIAL STATEMENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas Wright sulsmitted the fin*:ncial statement. Out-rilief was ararted as follows :—Fredsham, £23 ; Runcorn, £B5; Eudworth, £25. The report was adopted.</p>
        <p>ESTIBUTES FOR THE HALF-YEAR. RkTE UNCHANGED.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Wright submitted the bards of contributions required from the overaeers for the ensuing half year ending Michaelmas 1900 for consideration, calculated on the assessable value as per valuation lists in force. The total capital for the current half year ending Lady Day. 1900, amounts to £7,384, an 4 the nett probable expenditure is estimated at £4,857 which shows a balance at Lady- Day of £2,527. The probate expenditure during the half year under consideration as set out in the deailed estimate is made up as under :—Common charges, £4,383; c aonty rate, £3 295 ; balance and contingency fund, £1,167 ; total, £8,845. After deducting the probable balance in favour of the parishes at Lady Day 1900, the amount cc quired by contribution orders for the overseers is £6,318. The assessable value of the Union as per valu- ation lists in force is £206,944. A rate of id in the £on the assessable value of the union will produce the aum of £6,034 and it is recommended that a call for the sum of £6,318 be made upon the overseers in accordance with the detailed estimate after deducting the estimated bal- ance in favour of each Parish at Lady Day 1900, being equal to the call made for the corresponding period of last year. The estimates were unanimously approved of. CELEBRATING THE RELIEF OF LADYSMITH AT DUTTON WORKHOUSE.</p>
        <p>The Muter reported th it on " Ladrsmith Dly" Mr. E. 'raison, of Runcorn, left 103. for the inarateq, and he expended the same in buns- Mrs. Biker, of Epworth Hous., Meughland-:ane, Runcorn, had sent a quantity of " Graphics" for the inmates.</p>
        <p>TIIE AUDITS.</p>
        <p>The Auditor's certificat WAS submitted by the Clerk, from which it appe‘red all receipts and payments were legal and no surcharges made. This was considered bi4,hly satisfactory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Contest in Central Ward Improbable.</p>
        <p>Mr F A Like (law clerk to the Urban District Coun- cil) as returning officer closed the time for receiv- ing nominations for the vacancies on the Run- corn Urban District Council at 12 noon on Thurs- day afternoon. On the whole, very little interest was manifested in the event and candidates were very slew et coming forward ; with the result that only the following nominations were received CENTRAL WARD: Samuel Taylor, Rydal Mount, lime merchaLt ; Thomas Wood, Victoria Buildings, High- street, draper ; Joseph Hall Salkeld ; Pool House, Halton, gentleman. HALTON WARD: *Samuel Taylor, Rydal Mount, lime burner. NEWTON: *John Wilson, the Vines, Oxford-road; Runcorn, architect and surveyor. BRIDGEWATER WARD : *George Hewitt, Percival's- late, Runcorn. HEATEI WARD : *John Wilson Lightburn, Heath House, Heath-read, Runcorn, charterud accountant. MERSEY WARD • NJ nomination was received, the retiring member, Mr. H G Grice, will therefore repreeent the Ward. He baing &amp;dared " deemed to be re-elected under the provisions o the General Order of the Local Government Board, 1898." There is only one seat in each Ward, and the proba- bility of an eleetion is rem ite, even in Central Ward. In this Ward it will be noticed there are three candidates, but Mr S Tayler being returned unopposed for Halton, leaves but Mr T Woods and Mr J H Salkeld to contest the vacancy. The last day for withdrawal es fixed for Tuesday next, and probably by then Mr J H Salkeld will be prevailed upon to withdraw his candidature. Mr. T. Woods is the representative of the Runcorn Ratepayers' Association, by whom he was nominated, and as the representative of that body will doubtless re- ceive general support, should he be called upon to face a poll. The gentleman first approached by the Associa- tion was Mr. J. W. Weston.eheinist, but he was obliged to decline as not hay ing sufficient time at his disposal. The election, if any is Rxed for Saturday, March 31st.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>DRAUGHTS.</head>
        <p>DRAUGHTS.</p>
        <p>NEWS OF THE GAME. WARRINGTON LEAGUE. IST DIVISION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON V, CAIRO-sr. —This match, which we think, may be classed as the principal match in the above League took place on Monday last at Morris's Restaurant, Bank Quay, when a closely contested game resulted in a tie of 3 wins each and 5 games drawn with 3 more games to be played. Play was very keen and even, each individual player playing "for all he was worth." Although the match was started punctually to time at 8 o'clock, when 10 o'clock arrived it was very evident that at least six of ,e players would have to meet again as they were only then playing their irst games, and, as the rules say that two games must be played, there was nothing for it but to adjudicate upon the three games then in progress and to arrange some future date for the six players to meet again. Accordingly the three unfinished games were abandoned as draws. Scores : Wins. WARRINGTON. Draws. CAIRO-STREET. Wins. 1 F Dunae 0 W Tomkison ......... 1 1 J Roberts 1 S Tavlin .....................0 PWalsh 1 W Rigby 1 J R Grieve *3 F Warburton *3 W Warburton 1 F Wright 0 0 31 Gorman</p>
        <p>3 5 3 *Adjudicated, and one mo:e game to be played.</p>
        <p>IMPROVEMENTS .AT RUNCORN .LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>We understand that the extensive alterations in progress at the Liberal Club, Regent-street, are in all likelihood going to reach completion in a fort- night. and the memb?rA will be comfortably esconced in their new abode before Easter. This is good news, and will be hailed with delight by the members who have put up with the inconveniences of their temporary buildings long enongh. We further learn that the re-opening ceremony is likely to be a" red-letter day," but of that we bh4ll hear more anon.</p>
        <p>INEw F.PING JACK 7T9, Capes, Macinto3hes and Im- perial Cloaks. Choice assortment of Waite Sailor Hats. Just recel7el.—W. T. WILLIAMS, 74, Sankey-street, Warrington.. USHFUL PIiftSUNTS of every description, See Win- dow.—J. S. tiABLLYn Ironmonger, B:idge-st.—Advt- PUBLIC BNliiT UNDSR THE CLOCK TOR BBST OOTSus LA.IICASHIRB.—Adyt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>INFIRMARY FINANCES.</head>
        <p>INFIRMARY FINANCES.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting of Governors.</p>
        <p>There was a representative attendance at the annual meeting of the governors of the Warrington Infirmary and Dispensary held in the Council Chamber at the Town Hall on Thursday afternoon. The President of the institution, Mr J C Parr, occupied the chair and there were also present : Mrs Thomas Litton. Mrs John White, Mrs J R Pick- mere, Revs II V Pigot, John Yonge, J F Matthews, EC E Carleton, Drs. E A Fox, C E Richmond, E E Bowden, E Whirmby Battle, Alderman Sutton, Councillor John Roberts, Messrs Monks, John White, John Fairclough, H Thornton, C Leigh, Joseph Taylor, W Daintith, J Everett, with J L Tunstall (hon. secretary), and G Dakin (hon. treasurer). A letter was read from Mr. Charles Jackson Holmes apologising for his inability to be present.</p>
        <p>THE PRESIDENT AND THE INSTITUTIONS' P051T101..T.</p>
        <p>The reports and accounts for the past year, extracts from which were given in the " Examiner "last week were taken as read. Briefly stated the aecounts are worse than those of 1898 to the extent 01 £822 Os 2d,and the adverse balance now stands at £789 15s 21. The CHAIRMAN in moving the adoption of the report and accounts said he was once again privileged to occupy that position—a privilege he could assure them he esteemed very highly—°f being at the front of such a magnificent institution that was doing so much good in this town of Warringtoa. (Hear, hear.) He only wished that one more eloquent than he and one more better able to influence those whe ought to take an interest in it, occupied the position. He was very pleased to do what lay in his ;sewer to make it a suc- cess. The report before them was in many ways emin- ently satisfactory. The figures given in the House Sur- geon's report seemed to him most interesting. In con- nection with the dispensary 1,501 new home patiente were treated during the year which seri wed an increase of 487 over the previous one. Then 5,361 visits were paid to patients at their own hones, the new out-patients numbering 2,563, giving an in- crease of 222. There were 2,393 accident and emergency cases treated showing an increase of 280, making a total et 6,460 patients treated at the 'dispen- sary. 'This meant he hoped and believed, relief or cure or at all events, the mitigation in some degree of this large amount of human suffering. It must be a great satisfaction to those eng,igel in the management of the inbtitution and to those engaged in tending the sick and wounded that they were able to de so much to assist suffering humanity. Tnat brought him to another phrase of their hospital management—ha funds. He wished he could speak in the same hopeful and enceur- aging manner of this matter as be had done of the work accomplished. Here alas there was much reason for improvement and nu little cause for anxiety. They would notice in the report a growing deficit-3 deficit more marked this year in consequence of an accident in book-keeping in which a half year's dividend which really belonged to this year was ineludei in the previous year's balance sheet. Notwithstandingthat the increased deficit was serioua and was becoming increasingly !serious. In looking through their list of subseriptions they were all struck with the noble and handsome amount of their workingmen's contributions. 'They had respsnded to , the call for help as indeed they were in duty I bound to do, as this Clams benefitted most, he might almost say exclusively by the in- stitution. He recognised with pride and pleasure their appreciation ot the blessings conferred, for it was not always those who benefited most who contributed proportionately. In looking through the list of sub- scribers he was rather struck with the vacancies that ocourred from time te time and the very few addi- tional names that appear to fill them up. He could not help thinking that this might be possible from want of opportunity rather than through any other cause and that if the necele of the institution were brought before the people more prominently they might have a very large addition to their annual sub- scriptions. (Hear, hear.) He hardly knew how it ought to be done unless a committee were organized to make applications to likely subscribers. He merely threw it out as a suggestion. It was very desirable to consider it and that something should be done. (Hear, hear.) Mr. GEORGE DAKIN seconded the report and accounts which were unanimously approved.</p>
        <p>THE HON. TREA.SI7RHE.</p>
        <p>The CHIJIIM&amp;N proposed and M. F. MONES seconded the re-election of Mr. George Dakin as the bon treasurer. The resolution was agreed to. Nr. DAEIN said he was very much obliged for this mark of confidence. His p)sition was almost a sinecure owing to the admirable way Mr. Tunstall did his work. He had very great pleasure in taking the office for another year (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>gON AUDITOR.</p>
        <p>Mr C JACKSON HOLMES Was re-appoted Lion auditor on be proposition of Mr John Fah*dough.</p>
        <p>THE ON SECRETARY.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN in proposing the re-election of Mr J L Tunstall as hon secretary sAid they could not have a more assiduous and earful man for the position or one who had the interests of the institution more fully at heart than Mr Tunstall. - - - -</p>
        <p>Dr. Fox seconded, and the resolution was carried with acclamation.</p>
        <p>Mr TIINSTALL E aid he was much obligati for the honour they had done hirn in asking him to again take the office of hon. secretary. He should have much pleasure in doing what he could for the Institution. (Hear, hear).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>OUR</head>
