      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>EIOUSE-BREABING- SOLDIER SMARTLY PUNISHED</p>
        <p>At the Warrineton Police Court on Wednesday, before ISltsre Gediee tin the chair) and T Birchen, John Patrick Coariolly, a soldier was chergej with steeling from the dwellinc houo) of Geeree Wagstaff, 49. It nth- House Lace on Tuesday night, a clock, piece of oil- cloth and four small ornaments valued at 15, hie prop- perty.—Prisener admitted being in the house but did cot remember takiug the ertieles.—Ele was further charged with assaulting Deteetive Constable Delahay and com- mitting damage to his uniform to the atnount of 2s 6.1. Prisoner said te could not remember committing the offence as he had had some drink.—Superintendent Monaghan briefly stated the facts of the case. He said tbat the house of. Mr Wagstaff() was secure at 9.30 the psevioue night when the occupants retired to bed. At one o'clock that morning Detective Delahay was passing the back of the house when he saw the prisoner throw the oil cloth into the back yard. Ha went round to the front of the house and met the , prisoner who on seeing him went into the house again. . The officer aroused the family and arrested the prisener. He had a struggle with the man and eventually cot him ' to the Police Statien The prisoner was quite sober.— Evidence was then calted.—William Wagstaffe, son of George Wegstaffe, 49, Marshhouee-lane, said that at 9.30 the doors and windows if the house were fastened and the family scent to bed. At one o'clock in the morn- ing witne•e was aroused by heating some one homiest upetairs. flt- get up and saw it was the prisoaer. He asked him what ho wentee and be said "a man was after him." Detective Delattav then came to the bottem of the stairs ,stiel apprehendei the man. The clock, oilcloth and ornarnenl'3 which he valued at 15s were found in the beck yard:The prisoner must have eetersei the house by the windows, which were wide open. The prisoner appeared to be sober.—Dstective D,-lahay said that at 1.10 that mornieg he was in Marshhouse-lane. As he was passing the back of Mr. Wagstaff, ei heuse he looked over the yard gate and saw the prisoner handing a roll of oilcloth through the window. When pri)oner aaw him he threw the oilcloth in the yard and went into the hence. Witness went round to tLe front and saw the nrieoner attemptine to jump over the rails and get into the street. On seeleg him prisoner rushed int) the house. Witness followed and feuod him standing on the steirs with the last witnees He took him imo eu,tody and on the way to the Police Station he was very violent. He tripped witoees, threv him to the eroued and kicked him about the ankles. His uniform was damagvd in the struggle. He eventually secured him and took him to the Police Star ion. Oa being charged with tee theft he said he did not steel the articles. Witness afterwards, iR company with Inepsctoe Tyler, examined the premises and turd the oiicioth and the timepiece in the yard. Toe ornaments were seettered &amp;blue the yard broken.—Pri.oner said he " had a drop of drink." and could not tell how he got into the house. ft) had been with some friends and they !eft him at 12 30 —The Chairman asked the prisoner why he was out of Barracks at that time.—Prisener said he had a paste to remain outside till 12 o'clock,—Deteeive Dela- hay : No pats wee found in his p )sse-sion.—ln anewer to the Chairman Detective Delahay said prisoter was perfectly soker.—Prisoner was committee to goal for three months for the robbery and a month for the assault.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>MINOR CASES. Warr'ngton Borough Court.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES. Warr'ngton Borough Court.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman Wright Connelllor Smahurst. Dr. Gomall and Mr Hepherd. CPXELTY TO A Hos .-11enry Berite;, Rush Green, Lynam, was summoned for having on the 2nd inst. illtreated a horse by working It in an unfit state and Arthur Gibson, the Woodlands, Newton-le-Willows, was charged with causini the earns to be worked.—lnspector Brown, R.S P.0.A.. said that at easut 10-30 on the date in question he was on duty In Winwizk-street, when he saw the defen- dant Burkes in charge of a black mare which was attached to a cart laden with a quantity of manure, a number of boxes containing soap, and other things. He noticed the animal was lame and stopped it. On examinalon he found it to be lame on bofh fore limbs and it was walking cona eletely on its heels. lie called !hakes' attention to the condition of the mare and he said he anew it was lame but he was obliged to do what he was told. He had come from Maesey Brook. Witness afterwards saw the other defendant end he said he gave Burkes the order to work the mare. He knew 'it WAS lame in the near fore foot but not in the other. Mr. Hewitt, veterinary surgeon was called in and made an examination of the animal. He said it was quite unfit to work. The defendant Gibson prornioed the Bench that the animal should be destroy d and the magistrates took a lenient view of the ease and fined him 53 and costs and Burkes in the Costs,</p>
        <p>A BOY :X TRoUBLE. —Arthur Stephens, 11 years of age residing at 34, Wartnirtoa-street, was eurnmoneri for wilfully daranging an outbuilding in warburton-street the property of Mary Byrom. of Penketb, on the sth inst.—He pleaded not guilty.—P.O. Clough said t het at noon on Monday he was on duty in pIF in clothes At the back of Warburton-street, near the tiorse and Jockey Inn, when he saw the defewlknt In the yard of an empty house. had got a hammer and was knockithr the bricks from the wall of an outbuilding. If he had knoake,; many more do e'n the roof of the building would hay fallen in. Witness caught him and obtained his name and address.— Ars Byrom. Gli - owner of the property estimated the damays at about £s.—Aide.rnean Wright said he had received a large number of eomplakts from that distrirt abnit damage being do:te !o p-opst ty. lie hoped the police would keep watch and catch sornelne bigger than the oefendanr. The damage was not All dons by little boys like birn:-9uperintendent Monaghan said the defend- ant had been before the court in December last year for stealing p rter And was birched.—Pfe was ordered to be kept in the cells until 3 o'clock that night. MoNne Y.—Before 'sir F Monke(in the chair)ll',lerrnan Black hurst, Councillor Smethurst arid Messrs T fireball, d Wright and J W Hayward.</p>
        <p>- .• GAMBLIN4.—Thomas B-nnett, 12 Melydle,street. and William England, 33, xatkin street. were charged with this- log coins in Jones' Building,. a-. three o'clock en Saturday afternooe.—P.o Clough s ,id that at the time in question he W 5 on duty in p`kii, cothes at the back of Watkin-street in company with P.C. Kane and saw the prisoner.; in c3mpsny With ten other men to-siag c :ins in Jouas' Buildings. The 7 were shouting and swearing. He caught Bennett and P.C. Kane arrested Bugland. They refused to give the names of the other men, and a,ted in a violent manner. Tbey were taken to the Police Statini end loc:iel up. Witne,s picked a penny up from tie ground ! hat the men hod bnen Loosing. pris„„, were each finel 10a and costs with the alternative ot 14 days' insprisonment,—Joseph Gallop and :folio Gallop of Hopwood street were firrA 5s tor a similar offence in fildo:1- street, BFoorxcf.—Frederick Le., of no settled abode, was charged With begging in Butterrrarkot-street at five o'clock ou Satur- day afternoon and also with making rise obscene language —eU Clongh proved the case and the prisoner -.vas sent tn gaol for 14 days.</p>
