        <p>THEFT FRO3I A BACH YARD.—William Oldham of 37. Scotland-road, was charged with stealing from the back yard of 13, Tanners-lane, between two and five o'clock on I.riday aftetaiion, two frying pans, a saucepan, washing pan, kettle and iron roaker the property of Thomas Ratcliffe.—He pleaded net guilty—Su,erintendent Monaghan stated the facts of the case. He said that Mrs. Ratcliffe, the wife of the prosecutor, wen r out of the house at 2 o'clock on Friday aft.rnoon leaving the articles mentioned in the charge in the back yard. She returned at 5 o'clock and found them to los missing. On making inquiries it was ascertained that the pria,mer wts seen gatherlial rags in the rt-ighbourhood dur- iriv. the afternoon, anl hail sold several of the articles missing to" Ar. Chorley, marine store dreier. He had broken some of the things. Witness apprehended the prisoner in Win- wick-street. —Evidence in support of this statement was given by Ellen Ratcliffe, Ann Taylor and Richa-d Harding.— Prisoner said be found the articles among the rags near the ashtub—Suoerintendent Monaghan said that was his third appearane- The m tgistrates sent him to gaol for a month with hard labour. Prisoner ; Thank you, that's just what I wanted PLAYING AT •' DUCE-STONES" IN THE STREET. —John Cart- wright, 6, York-street; Christopher Moore, Henry Moore, William Fearnley, Thomas Hollowell, Frederick Benton, Robert Wilicoek end Herbert Willoack,all of New-road, boys. were summoned for playing "duck-stones" in Canterbury- street to the annoyance of passengers on the 4th March.— They pleaded guilty—P.O. Clough proved the case and a fine of Es 6d each was imposed.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDIY.—Beford Messrs. J. Geddes (in the chair) and</p>
        <p>T. Birchall. WILFUL DIMAGE.—" NOT THE SHADIW OF A MAN A1311.1T MlNl."—John William Travis, 53, Winwick-road, was charged with committing wilful damage to 17 panes of glass at the house of his father. George Travis, on the 20th Feb.— Prisoner's mother stated that the prisoner broke the windows with a brush. The damage amounted to is.—George Travis, the prisoner's father, described his son as being one of the worst men in Warrington. Owing to his conduct his mother was afraid of her life. The previous afternoon he threatened her and she lived in terror of him. Prisoner would not de any work.—The Chairman (to prisoner) : There is not a shadow of a man about a fellow like you ; threatening your own mother.—Superintendent Monaghan said that was pris- oner's leith appearance. He had been before the court for assaulting his parents and breaking windows. He had been in the Army and was drummed out. —Ptisoner was sent to gaol for two months with hard labour. GONE To Sourll AFRlCA.—jArnes Mannion. ?, Bakehouse Yard, was summoned for neglecting to send his child to school regularly.—Mrs Mannion appeared and in answer to the Clerk said her husband was serving in South Africa.— The Clerk said he could not he held responsible for the child not going to schnol.—Mr Joaes, chief school attendance officer who appeared to prosecute, withdrew the case.</p>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions. MaxcaY.—Before Messrs. G. L. Davies (presiding), and A. R. Norman. LICEN9ING.—The following transfers were granted, and the ingoing tenant granted the necessary tempsrary authority :—" Three Crowns," Bine-street HUTICOrn, from James Robinson to Samuel Span; " Duke of Edinburgh," Devonshire-square. from Samuel Buxton to John Bvan Davies; "Brunswick Vaults," Brunswice-street,from WillLm Ushin to Charles Sleernau. DIVLINKENNE2S.-1111k.n Jones, for being drunk in Lock-st., wae fined 55.--Fatrick Handley, charged In custody with being drunk and disorderly at Runcorn WAS fined 6s and costs or 7 days. The case w s pr3ved by Constable Gilpin Elizabeth Bentham, pleaded not guilty to a charge of drunkenness, committed in Mersey-street on the 7th inst. She attributed it to over excitement conrequent on "flitting," —Superintendent D. Pearson said cleferviant's husband was removing. Defendant objected to th removal, and was in the act of pulling a basket of thing e off the lurry. As the resut of the woman's drunken conduct there was a large crowd. Her husband dealt very gently with her, but defendant nould not desist from her attempt to stop the removal, and he (witness) ha.rl to remove her forcibly from the cart. As she would not give a promise of good behaviour he loceed her up, and admitted her to bail the second day.— P.C. Carrington stated ha saw the w )man in the cells about quarter of an hour after she was locked up. She was help- lessly drunk.--Fined 6s or 7 days.</p>
