      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>BLACK SKINS AND OTHERS.</head>
        <p>BLACK SKINS AND OTHERS.</p>
        <p>"The splendid black satin skin of the South African Zulus of Durban," says Mark Twain, "seemed to me t 3 be nearly perfect." The great humorist is right, although Europeans who have never seen the Zulus may enter an exception. Yet see how beautiful almost all brown skins are, and how ' rare a beautiful white skin is. White skins are apt to be bleached out, reddened up coarse, and more or less covered with eruptions, waxy and thick, dingy and like old leather, grey with areas of purple, sallow and dotted with freckles, yellow and bilious, flabby, with c,inkling veins ; and not infre- quently ghastly—nothing short of it. Take notice that the white complf xion cannot conce anything. All defects and marks show like reins, thrown on a snow bank. It catches all the ugly things that are flying about. Perhaps one person in fifty has a good complexion, and that is the person with the cleanest; blood. " Ladies," says The-Man-Who-Tdlls-ths-Truth " paint and powder the face. They', cosmetic it, diet it with arsenic, and enamel it. They are always enticing it. pestering it, and fussing at it tomake it beautiful, and they fail." The true beautifier of the complexion is the thing which purifies the blood and mak s the digestion what it ought to be. Axel that is Mother Seigel's Syrup. Under its u.se the skin becomes firm, smooth, clear, and restful to the eye, harmonising with al CO:9IITS, ani adding a grace to all. Try it, and try it thoroughly, and you will find that the eucceseful prescription for a perfect complexion is perfect health. " Beauty is only shin deep," says the ancient saw. The saw lies. Beauty is as deep as the stomach, liver, kidneys, etc., and Mcater Seigel's Syrup controls and trengthens these.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>Latchford V. Pendleton,</p>
        <p>This match was played at L Achferd. wo forties were arraneed, and the visitors wioning the toes decided to play uphill with tha sue in their feveur. The first engagement of the s, aeon between these clubs was a very pleasant avid evenly-contested game, Latchford winning by four to three, but the return match on Seturday was not so evenly-contested or qeite so pleasant,as there was some rough play on both sidee,end a good deal of reckless hitting. For the first part of the game play was chiefly in midfield, until the home forwards broke away giving E Grounds an easy chance which he accepted. Again frem the bully there was some eice-and-take play, when Carter secured, and after a short run he scored a second goal. This early reverse aroused the Pendleton team, and they made sevoral determined rushes, but failed to break the home defence. Jenkins and Uneworth being especially prominent. Half time arrived with the score —Latchford two, Pendleton none. Toe home team were the first to score, and then the visitors put two goals on in A few minutes. They tried hard to eqeelize,but their attempte were frustrated by the home halves,Heald put- tine, in sarne solendid stroke. Some vieoreue hitting by Unswerth at back gave the home forwards another chance, which Pearson accepted and netted a splendid goal from a difficult position. All the home forwards had a turn at shooting at goal, and each was successful, Carter, Pearson. and F Groure!s sco7ing two goals each, and Knight and E Grounds one each. Just before the end of the eame Pendleton made another rush and scored a third goal. Final scere—Letchturi eight, Pendleton three.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH</p>
        <p>BAND OF HOP?, ENTERTAINMANT.—An entertairment in connection wi, h the all Ciao ant Stocktwo Heath United raid of Ellpa wag given on Mondsy evening in rho Eheneze.r Wesleyan Chapel. It was "The Children's N,ght," the meeting tieing entirely conducted by the ynung pnple. The first part of the programme w.s in the hands of the young ladies,and Miss Clar Kenworthy, who preside', discharged her duties in a creditable manner. Miss 'do Grourds contributed a march on the nrgan,and solos were given by tho MiS,3A3 W Poles and J Ehon. M's S J Worrell was the speaker. The dia- logues "The teetotal j icket " anti" C,usirt Mire's story" were given wit a goad eff,,ct by the Misses V Taylor. B. Lowerl Ploughton,M Bsrten,and Lucy March respectively The second part was in the haudsni the bees. The Chairfnan,Master J Worrall.in his opening address,urged upon the youths prrsent the noeessitr of growing up steady nvn. The first item on the program' was a dialogue, "The Temp-rance Ststodard," which was illus- trated by six boys each carrying a flea. The homereue songs of Masters Percy and John Grounds wereoencored. A practical addr-ss was given by Mr Richord Torne/, in which ha pointed out some of the woes of temperanc". and appealed to all young nesple to join the temperance cause, after which, the dislegue " 'Whv should sign the pledge ?" was given by Tom, Charlie, and Samßrereton. Burin!! the evening the foll-wing mem!" re nentributed recitations. &amp; .—M'sses Edith Coles, A Br/reton, E Moss, E Bond, P Brereton, and S E Whi,fi-td, and Masters L Ben wotthy, S Jones. J Whitfield, S Isherwood Joseph Moss, II Moores, John Moss, Mrs Lieerwool, W BArtui, P.,ter Moss, B Lho.wood, and George A Whitfield. A vote of thanks to the young people was heartily accorded on th mption of the President (Rev J 8 Hughes), and seconded by Mr E Lloyd Jones, Miss Ada Grounds discharged the dutit 3 of accompsnist.</p>
        <p>OLD FALSB TBRTH BOUGHT. fdanv ladies and gentlemen have by them old or disused fal,e teeth. which might as well be tu-ned into money. Messrs. R. D. and J. B. Fraser, of Princess-str-et. Ipswich (estasllehed since 1`684). buy old false tePth. If you send your teeth to them they will remit you by return of post ehe utmost value ; or if preferred, they will make you the best offer; and hold the teeth over for your reply. If references necessary, apply to Messrs. BACON and Co., Bankers Ipswich. —Advt.</p>
