      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION. LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION. LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Runcorn Win the Championship. 411662 9s, 104 d. Taken at the Gate.</p>
        <p>Local football records, do not show a similar instance to that of the maecti between Runcorn and Oldham, which was played off on the Irwell-lane enclosure, on Ssturday last. Since the commencement of the season Runcorn have figured at the top of the table, and kept their poiition througheut, with the exception of one in- stance, when the compilers of the table made a mis- calculation and gave Oldham the preference. The com- petition between Oldham and Runcorn has been most keen, and for a few matches back there has been but very few points between them. On Saturday morning last, the teams occupied the interesting position of having but one point between them, and a match to ! play. A win on either side or a draw, settled the championship. There wt ano danger of" playing off," and no getting out of the fact that the game once corn- meeced. must end decisively. No match in the history of Runcorn or Oldhem ever aroused such enthusiasm and absorbiug interest. The Runcorn Committee realising the accommodation on the club's ground would be insufficient to cope with the ex- pected crowd set about in the endeavour to improve things ; and be it said to their credit—though all the vast sn eltitude could net see—the committee succeeded beyond exprct Before two o'clock the spectators began te roll up in solid streams ; attracted, no doubt, by the fact that after 230 the prices would go up. Special trains were run from Oletiarn, an I a crowd of about 3,000 availed themselves of the opportunity of accompanying their favourites. Warrington, Widnes, Liverpool, Birkenhead. Mid-Cheshire, and even Man- et:tester contributed to the figeres. The Runcorn "A " team would not turn out, and though offered 103. per man to fulfil the fixture at Oldham, declined with thanks, preferring to stay at home and partake as sight- seers in the " match of matches." The Runcorn Volun- teer Baud pl teed selections on the field, conducted by Bandmaster Outhwaite Prompt to time Houghton led the men oet,and was Bones followed by Oldhem. Both teams received eplendid ovations. Fletcher was miesel from the Oldham ranks, but Runcorn had the team of its ceoice, " every man Runcorn born and bred." The names were as follows : Runcorn S Houghton, back; C Butterworth, J Butterworth,. Warder, and 1' Wilkinson. three-quarter backs ; J Jules' and J Faulkner, ball-backs; S Walker, H Farmer. F Geyter, J 'Tomlinson, A Taylor, J Langley, 0 Hughes, ane R L,ghtfoet, forwards. Oldham : Thomas, back; Williams, Davies, S Lees, and Owen, three-quarter backs; A Lees and Lawton, half-backs ; Bonier, Breoene, Frater, Aloff atc, Telfer, Wilkinson, J Lees, and T Sellers, forwards. Referee Mr. J Oakland, Wakefield. Mr. S. Taylor (president of the Runcorn Club) was present on the ground. Houghton favoured with the spin of the coin, decided to let Oldham commence with the sun in their faces, al- though there was a slight breeze assisting them. Moffatt kicked off, and Wilkinson was tackled before he could return. Jolley and Houghton hesitates, but, the latter recovered before Oldham got near, and returned. Run corn forwards dribbled and Faulkner and Warder gained further ground. Oldham returned with a rust, but Houghton sent them to the right about smartly. Dick Thomas was then called upon to save, and did so by kicking into touch several times : Exchange kicking led to Oldham gaining a slignt advantage, and in attempting a save Jolley was hurt. He resumed. Oldham backs made several attempts at pabisiag, but the keen- ness of the green-jerseyed men rendered all their efforts abortive. Steadily hut firmly the Runcorn men pressed, end a footing W4S obtained in tNe Oldham " twenty- five ' Do what he would, Thomas could not shake the " Linnets" off and several dead balls were conceded. Sam Williems at length raised the soige, and ruanine finely dcds,,ed several opponents ; only, however, to fall a prey to Houghton who carried him into touch. Back went the ball, and back went Runcorn, and a very anxious time was spent near the adham lice. Owen was next to break awa.y,but Faulkner accounted for him in like manner to 'hat in which Houehten accounted for his man. Jelley,Faulkner,Warder and Butterworth,again made headway ; efforts to which Davies and Williams responded, Oldhem got away, and could have scored, but for a forward pass. Runcorn were still pressing when half time arrived with a dent sheet. Farmer restartee, and several dangerous rushes were made by the Runcorn pack. Dick Thomas saved by again kicking into touch. Runcora got dangerous, and C Butterworth got the first chance, but was too keenly attended by Williams. Pressing grandly,Jolley,Feulkner, Walker, and Farmer all made attempts to cross the Old- ham line, but could not. Welke:- was held upon the line. Then J Butterworth was baulked by Williams and Davies, who just pushed him on one side and guided the ball over the dead-ball line. Jolley picking up from a scrimmage passed to Faulkner, who made way and gave to Butterworth, the latter seeing no opening again transferred to Faulkner and amid a scene of tremendous excitement Faulkner secured a magnificent try. Joilay failed to convert. Fallowing this Faulkner had hard lines in not securing a second try by following up a kick of J Butterworth's. Williams hooked the ball from under him, just as he fell to make sure of it. Lawton, working grandly behind the scrimmage, put in one of his characteristic dashes, and made a lot of ground Warder fa' e ied him up with a pretty tackle. Fine kick- ing by Thomas, then transferred play into Ileughton's quarters, and the veteran had quite an anxious few moments negotiating rush after rush. Relief came none too soon in a free kick, and play gravitated to mid-field where it remained until the ene. The final result was Runcorn one try (three points), Oldham nil. The gate realised £362 9s 100, a record for Runcorn ; and the boys in" Khaki" collecting for the war funds, are said to have realised over £3 33 Od.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>After the Match.</head>
        <p>After the Match.</p>
        <p>The scene after the match was one to be remembered long by those who witnessed it. rho home sectien of the crowd,literally went mad, and caps, hats, sticks and umbrellas, were thrown into the air in frantic confusion. Sam HoLighton was carried sho_ !der high frora the field, led by the band playing "Cock o'th' North." Opposite the drPesing room the band pl lye,' the well-known "No 3" "We beat them once before hors; and so we will again," —and opposite the" Nelson Hotel," nearing that Mrs. Houghton was not quite well the flighted bind were induced to give'• Auld Lang Syne." Sections of the town were paraded by large crowds, and wherever a footballer could he found he was lioaised,and mtde the guest of the evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Warrington v. Morecambe.</head>
        <p>Warrington v. Morecambe.</p>
        <p>The Morecambe team never was what is known in football circles as a " drawing team," and when as was the case on Saturday there was a counter attractian at Runcorn, it was not surorising to find that barely 2,000 spectators assembled on the Wildersposl enclosure to bave a look at the seaeiders. 'rh, fixture was favonred with ideal football weata?r. Promptly to time, the teams lined up as follow : Warrington : Hallam, full back ; Heckenhall. Jones, Isherwood awl Fish, three-givartara ; Burgess and Bete, half baele3 ; E lan, Swlf", Antrelnis, Fairhurst, Board- man, Morrison, Schrltze, Moors, forwards. Morecambe. L Jackson, lull hack ; Bland, R Wi'san, H Wits )n and Bell, threreqaarter backs, R Belt and Wood, half backs; Elkin, Jackson, Catnpber, hompson, Dawson, Shaw, Wilson, and Feamf )rd, forwards. Eikin stared operations avid for the first few mtntitea there was little done by either team ; what little advan- tages there was, lay with the vi,iting term. Isherwaod at lengtn by a strong run placed Warrington on the offensive. Ban good combinA play, the visitors trans- ferred play and a pretty passing movement between Wood, WiDon and Bell almost esidted in the latter scoring. The pressure ;was cantinuei and Warrington were kept busily defandiage B.irgess initiztad a pass- ing movement which relievad the preeaura. Bate and Hockenhall were next prominent with a paYsing runnand a moment latar Bweess was o.loi.!fiSl for a fine break away from behind the pack. He threw across to Jones but the ball went a trifle forward and spoiled a cod opening. Having now gained a distinct advantage Warrington pressed it home with vigour and a combinefl. revising nenvement bet Yean Moors, Eden, Bate and Fish resulted in tho latter dodging over the tin' near the corner. The kick at goal by the same player was no a succPss. Play ea/111161M in favour of the home team and latvrwood daubed away in vigorous fashion. He then nafred to Jones who kicked across to Haekenhall and that player succeeded in planting the hall over the c•aveterl line. Again no goal was kicked. The last minute of this half was a surprise to all. The ball was kicked over Fiallaat's head, and through some rnisuneerranding, R. Wilcon who gairael possession was allowed to cress the line withaut any attempt being made to stop him The evergreen Elkin was entrusted with the kick and placed a fine goal. The 4histle then soundel and when tha teanas croaced over, the home team was leading by six points to five. On resume ng it we seen that Burgess was tinablo to continue playing owiag ta his leg again troubling him. A change in the Warrinet -n team was mane. Moores wan brought out of the torwards and pi .coe.full-hick. Hallam going to the assistance of Bate at half-back. A change was also made in the three-quarter lisle, Janes</p>
        <p>going on the wing while Hoekenhall took his place at centre. Despite the fact that Warrington were thus handicappd with only fourteen men they were mare than a matcli for their opponents. A pass from Isherwood to Fiat) almost resulted in the latter seoring, and a momeot later Jones alnoost placed a goal from near the half way line. Hockenhall broke away and passed to Hallam hut he was not supported and a good opportunity was lost. Amends were made shortly afterwards fur Ishsrwood neatly intercepted a pass and Fish following up in quick time was given the hall. Be rounthd his opponent in a beautiful lasnion and did not stop until he uad plac2cl the bail under the posts. Jones failea at the easy place. Taylor dropped a goal but the whistle having sounded, the point was not allowed: The visitors showed up prominently at times but were unable to get through, and the game ended in a victory for Warrington by three triea (nine points) to Morecambe one goal one try (ci vs point a). LATCHFORD RANGEM T. CADISHEAD. —On the ground of the first-named on Saturday. During the first half, Latchford go:Abe upper hand, Jones scoring a try. In I the second half, the game was of a more even nature, until by clover play Ray scored. Cadishead now resumed the off ,nsive, and Riley scored a try. Final : Latcniord, oat goal two tries ; Cadishead, one try.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ST. MARY'S V. WIGAN "A."—At War- ringtoa on S iturday. Too home team commenced. Nevins scored for the Sz ints after a few minutes, and before tha iaterval Lowe scored again for the hom-, team. Half-time : Warrington St. Mary's, two tries ; Wigan, nil. In the second half, Lavrless scored for the home side. They kept up the pressure, and very seldom were Wigan dasgerou3. Carless scored for St. Mary's. Final : Warrington S. Mary's, four tries ; Wigan. nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
