        <p>TAKE CARE OF YOUR CHEST.</p>
        <p>Have you a tickling sensation in the throat ! If 80 be aware of cstarrii, and its auc•Jinpanyieg effaats• Lavans Lula; Linctus acts on the throat and longs immediately, and will remove that tickling. Have you a feeling of tightness across your chest ? If SD, and it you do not at once remove it by one dose of Lavans Lung Luactus, you are simply courting bronchitis, pseunaonia, and that dreadful disease, con- sumption. H4ve you a difficulty In hrrathinf;, and does it pain you to speak ? Toon get a b-ittle of Lavar.s Lung Linctus, if you value your health, because it is a most serious thing, and will ultimately lead to bronchitis or asthma. Do you feel full and surfeited in the morning when YOU wake ? That is clused by p4lergm that has accumu- lated in the nigh'. A dose of Lavans Lung Linctus when you go to bed. will absolutely prevent all that. Do you have a difficulty in sleTing through couching during the night ? Then by all means take a dose of Lavans Lung Linctus ; one will be sufficient, and it will ensure you co:nplete repose, and cure your cold without trouble. Lavans Lung Lirtcsus is sold by all the loc :I Chemists, Patent Medicine Vendors, and Co-aperative Stores or the Proprietors, Green and Co., Bolton,will forward a battle, post free, is 41, 2-! 9d, and 4561.</p>
        <p>Serious Farm Fire at Lymm.</p>
        <p>Information was received at the Warrington Fire Station at half-past four on Monday morning that a fire had broken out on the farm of Mr. Samuel C44ufd, at Lymm. The deputy Superintendent and a number of firemen immediately proceeded to the farm, and on arrival found thau their services would be uselese, as the fire had accomplished in work of destruction. Four stacks of hay, six stacks of corn, a bay sbed, four carte, a lurry and several farming implements were destroyed, and the damage, w‘aien is covered uy insurance, exceeds £l,OOO. The Accident at the Workhouse Infirmary.— An Appeal Case.</p>
        <p>Oa Saturday the Case of C3oper v. Davenport (Winstanley third party) came before the Court of Appeal on the appeal of the third party from the award of the County Court judge of Warrington, ordering him to indemnify Mr. Davenport in an award he had made under the Workmen's Compen- sation Act, awar ling the widow of Cooper £260 comperuation. Mr. Ituegg, for the appellantotated that Davenport was building for the Warrington Guardians an infirraary, but had sub-let the plumb- ing work to Mr. Winstanley. Cooper was in the employ of Winstanley, and the, building on which the accident happened was admittedly over 30 feet high. The scaffolding I'Vftq erected by Winstanley. Counsel contended that the County Court judge was wrong in holding that Winstanley was liable to indemnify Davenport, inasmuch as he was only a sub-contractor and therefore not an " undertaker within the weaning of the Act. In the result their lordships allowed the appeal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
