      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>[By "OvaUsti</p>
        <p>The struggle for the championship of the Lancashire Senior Competition is ended, and Runcorn are for the second tlm3 pro3laimed winnera of the trophy. Right well do the " Linnets" de3erve the honours they have done, for they have shown remarkable c3usistency in attack and defence right through the season. Not once has the tide of success shown a disposition to ebb, though just lately many p.ople have fancied that Run- corn's powers were weakening becauce they filed to apt the better of such bitter and formidable lose as St. Helens and Widnes on their own grounds. They falsified their critics on Saturday however by disposing of Old- ham in a satisfactory fashion.</p>
        <p>A more interesting finish for the Competition could not have been devised, as the championship absolutely depended on the last match between Oldham and Run- corn, which was sot down for decisien at Irwall-lane on Saturday. Beth teams had a large following, and the confidence of one faction was only equalled by that of the other. Perms,lly I fs,veured the " Linnets," speci- ally as they had already beaten Oldham, and had the advantage of performing on their awn peculiar enclo- sure. In addition, they only needed to make a draw of it to secure the trophy, but lam glad to say that this rather unsatisfactory finish was not come to. The Old- ham men were fairly and squarely beaten.</p>
        <p>I think all Lancashire and Cheshire must have been at Runcern on Saturday, for tha crowd was out of all pro- portion to the fuze of the ground and testified strikingly to the remarkable interest taken in the match. 101 Sot's genial influence was sufficient to persuade me to make the journey by road per crele, accompanied by a few friends, and some *odd sights wa saw. The Rancorn road was crowded with vehicles of every type and generation " bikem " predominating though the large number of lumbering omnibuses suggested wholeeale commandeering from the bus routes. Here were unfor- tunate wheelmen mending puncture and commenting freely fluently and profanely on unforseeu breakdowns, but I think all managed to reach their destinetion some- how. The sight from the Canal bridge below Halton castle, looking back across the rolling veldt—if I may so term no man's land—was a remarkable one, ler the stream of vehicles and pedestrians seemed endless.</p>
        <p>It was three o'clock when we reached Runcorn. and we were just congratulating ourselves on being comfort• ably carly, when the high road brought the ground into view. There was space within the rails, but no where she, and what was to become at the crowd of people who must arrive during the next 30 minutes was a question we did not stop to answer, but hurried down to the field in double quick time. They were still taking money at the gates, and th,y took ours, but would not guarantee reserved seats. In fact the only seats visible were the roof-ridges of the neighbouring cottages, and these were promptly hired or stolen by people who hadn't had their first ride of the season, and were not averse to sitting on anything between a telegraph wire anc an arna-ehair. Like 10,000 other ordinary iadivi- cinal3 we had to be content with a square foot of cinder path, and as much of the game as could be seen over the heads of the excited individuals in front.</p>
        <p>An air of patriotism was added to the some by the 10.3a1 band, which contrived to make the period of wait- ing pass more pleasantly by playir. g " Soldiers of the Q.oen" awi. other popular airs, the crowd joining in the chorus with great heartiness. A number of venturesome spirits took advantage of the prevailing good humour to climb to the top iif the stand, from whence they had an ex3ollent view of the proceedings. Despite all the ex- pustule:ions and threats of the Runcorn officials they h-ld their ground till the cl3se of the match. Two little boys in kharki parAded the enclosure with collecting boxes and tho shower of coin which greeted them on all hands was overwhelming,l should fancy there would b 3 a fair harvest on the following day for anybody who took the trouble to visit the scene of battle and grope aboaat the grass. Th- war fund got a considerable augmentation, however, and that was the main thing.</p>
        <p>As a display of good football, the match doe e not I rank very high. 'there was too much excitemerd, on both sides for pretty plar, while the tactics of the Run- cornians were not calcul Ited to make the game an open one. Runcorn's policy was obviously to smother the Oldham backs, an,4 this tlpy ao3omplished in a very effective fashion. Eis a long time since Oldham were compelled to abandon th•tir moat etyle of play so com- pletely as on Saturday, and when they tried to play the men in green at their own game they got the worst of it. It was her that they made their principal blunder. The Oldham men are not cot out for kick and ruah,punt and tackle, and they would probably have been more successful had they persevered with passing. In fact it was only when they did pace that they were dangerous. Still there was plPnty of excuse for them in abandoning their own style of play, for the men be- hind wore wonderfully well watched and seldom got go- ing with any swing. To a man the Runcorn !ads played grandly, aod towards the close they plainly wore down the Oldham men. It was when the latter were looking their worst that the home backs tried a bit of quick passing, and Faulkner slipped through amidst a scene of wild excitement. Oldham came again, were repulsed. and all was over, the Rtmeornians winning a most stub- born game by a try to nil.</p>
        <p>The score jiis*, about reprmen's the play, and the merits of the two teams. It is rather hard for Oldham tg, have to play second fiddle once more to the lads from the b4nks of the Mersey, but they must admit that thd latter are rather mere than their masters.</p>
        <p>All the competition game have now been plard, Millorn brinciax them to a close by feating Rochdale Hornots at RJcbdale on Monday, by a goal to nil.</p>
        <p>The complete table is aa follows :</p>
        <p>For. Against. P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. P. Runcorn 29-22— 2.. 2...35... 54-212 6... 7... 33...46 Oldham 26. 21... 4... 1...32... 93-343... 9...19... 7)...43 Swinton —26...19 6... 1...24... 54—,10...24...20...108.. 39 St. Helens 26...16.. T... 3...24... 63-207...13...31...119...35 Widnes.. 26-12.30... 4...24... 42-174...19...36...146...23 Warrington 26...12...13... 1...24... 42-174 ..16...32..123...25 *Brought'n R 6-13-12 1...21... 30 - 132...24_3u...138-25 Salford** z6. .12-14 0...26... 48-19i...31_3R-176-24 Stockport 26 ..10...14... 2...21... 28-126...20-33-139-22 Leigh 8...13... 5-22 25-119...26...53...219...21 *Hochdale Wt 526... 9...16... 1...18... 18— 90...20...47...181...17 Mlllom 26. 7...18... 1...20... 24-112...30...58...234...15 Wigan 26. 7..18. 1. 8 19 73. Tyldesley 26... 2...23... 1... 9... 16— 66 42.. 84...336... 5 *Broughton Rangers and Rochdale Hornets have each had two points deducted for breach of rules.</p>
        <p>Warrington somewhat easily accounted for More- cambe on Saturday. The engagement was robbed of much of its interest by the fact that it was a " were friendly." No longer does the blue-and-white jer- Bayed gentlemen from the pleasant watering place on the Lancashire coast occupy a position in the first league and consequently ne points were to be scored at their expense on Saturday. But an even greater factor in determining the small attendance as the great attraction at Runcorn where the two crack clubs in the Lancashire competition were deciding who were to be the possessors of the silver trophy for the next twelve months. Glancing round the Wilderspool enc osure en Saturday afternoon too much timber met the eye and as Treasurer Pemberton prefers to see men to half-filled stands he must have longed to " swop " gates tor the nonce with the Runcorn club's treasurer.</p>
        <p>Warrington placed a very satisfactory team in the field. Burgess made his reappearanae and locked like a well seasoned feotball warrior with his leg ar.d knee swathed in bandages. He played a good game until half time and t ben the injured limb began to trouble its poseessar to such an extent that he was compelled to cease playing, and for the remainder of the match War- rington played only fourteen men. le is a curious fact but none the !Psi true that the visitors were overplayed to a greater ex set When Warrington were a man short than in the first bait when they played on level terms. When the whiatle sounded for half-time the home team wen) only one point ahead. Fida and Hockenbull had hotel succeencl in placing the ball on the right side of the desired rot, but immediately before the whistle sounded H. Wi:son did a like performance on behalf of the visitors, and as Elkin also demonstrated bis ability to kick goala, the margin between the two scores was the least rossible when the teams crossed over.</p>
        <p>The locnini half was distinguished by the dash shown by the Warringron men. Isherwood in particular placed a fine game ad as a eeutre three-q uarter he has few superiors.lt was he who led the movement which resulted in Fish augmentir g the Warrington score and enabling the home team to retire victors four points ahead.Haltara was tried half-hack during the second portion of the game and proved a suitable substitute. His place at lull back was tilltd by Moores—brother of "Freddy '— and although he did not do anything of a brilliant nature he at least kept the visitors from augmenting their score. FiEh again came out en top in the scoring line placing two tries to his credit, whilst Hoclseuhall at the other end of the line showed himself well able to take advantage of an opening when it pre- sented itself, by picking up and scoring in unmistakable fashion. Bate cap tiined his team in a manner vihich augurs wet' for to-day's match and the experiment he tried in p!actig H.)ekonhall in the centre and Jones an the wing will no thubt be repeated when ensasion de mends.</p>
        <p>To MOTIIER9.--Mre. Winslow' Soothing Syrup has been used for over fifty years by millions of mothers for their z.hlidren whit; teothlug, with perfect :florets. It will relieve the poornufferer irnmediatmly It is pleasant to taste ; and produces natural okoet sleep. ny relieving the child from pain Anti the litt:e 0,-.rnb awakes •• as bright as a button."Of all Chemists Is lfd per bottle.—Advt.</p>
