      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>War and the Call to Prayer.</head>
        <p>War and the Call to Prayer.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. John Peers has been good enough to notice my letter on this subject. I regret, however, that I am unable to enter into a controversy with him on the con- flict in South Africa as the standpoints from which we view the horrors of war seem to be entirely different. Your correspondent would tare up the sword because the Millennium has not yet come but, in the light of Christ's gespel, I am compelled to regard, war as a crime against God and man. Mr. Peers will, however' find much to encourage him in the warpath if he will con- sider the example of the good men who love to preach about the saving of men's souls and at the time do not object to the destruction of their b)dies for trade or political purposes. Mr. Peers seems to be very wroth with the people who are endeavouring to stop the war and its awful cost of blood and treasure, and deseribes them as devil's advocates. This is strong lant-uage, and your corres- pondent must excuse me if I say that it would mere fitly apply to the breakers up of a peace meeting than to those who desire to stop hostilities. The devil is always on the side of wrong doinz, and has been truly des- cribed as 'I a murderer from the beginning." Besides, Mr. Peers knows very well that peacemakers are called in scripture "the children of God."—Yours truly, lONA. March 13, 1900.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Warrington Guardians and the Nursing Association.</head>
        <p>Warrington Guardians and the Nursing Association.</p>
        <p>Sir,—lt is with feelings of amazement that I read in your report of the meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians on Wednesday last, a statement made by one of the gentlemen to the effect that there is "a general impression in the town bat the benefits of the District Nursing Association ar., confined to the Middle Classes." . _ _</p>
        <p>If such an erroreous impression can exist after the steady work of the association in the town for upwards of 24. years, it is surely time that the impression was removed, and the townspeople made acquainted with the facts. _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>It cannot, I think, be generally known that the "Quesn Victoria's Jubilre Institute for Nurses," to which the Warrington Association is affiliated, and by whom its work is periodically inspected, took its origin from the Jubilee of Queen Victoria's reign, when her Mijesty was pleased to devote £70,000 of the " Women's Offer- ing " for' the special object of training nurses to tend the sick poor in their own homes. The perversion of this charity from the original "special obj-ct " has not been achieved up to the ~resent, and up and down the country are hundreds of the " Queen's Nurses " daily ministering to the needs of the poorest and most neg- lected of the Queen's subjects. Florence Nightingale, writing to the late Duke of Westminster, who was ap- pointed one of the trustees of the Fund, under tier, Royal Charter, says :—" God speed to your noble effort in favour of District Nursing for town and country; and in commemoration of our Queen who (tares for all. We look upon the District Nurse, if she is what she should be, and if we give her the training she should have, as the great civilizer of the poor, training as well as nursing them out of ill-health into good health (Health Mis- , sioners), out of drink into self-control ; but all without prea chine, without patronizing,as friends in sympathy." While, however, the primary reason of the existence of the District Nursing Association is for the benefit of the poor' "patients are not excluded who though unable to incur the expense of a private nurse, are able to make some contributions to the funds." To quote Rule 14, "The services of the Nurse are free of charge to the really poor, but those who have appreciated the service; of the nurses are invited to contribute to the funds of the Society, however small the amount. Sults contribu- tions should be sent to the Superintendent at the Nurses' Home, and a receipt signed by the Treasurer will in all ases be returned." The cases on the books at present represent anything but the Middle Classes. A glance at the list reveals a succession of heartrending histories of suffering and poverty, sueh as perhaps only the District Nurses in their daily contact with the people, and their intimate acquaintance with the circumstances of their lives, can adequately realize. Limitations of time and spaces torbid enumeration of these cases here, but to any who are sufficiently interested in an association which has for its obj .ct the provedine of skilled attention for the poor of the town, in their time of sickness and need (and that in their own homes), I would extend a cordial invitation to come and inspect the books, or better still see the work itself. By an extensive response to this invitation, the vagueness of" general impressions," and all such hazy and mis- leading surmisings would assuredly soon Dive place to a confident knowledge of facts, and a resulting ready co- operation and support. Thanking you for kindly inserting, this letter, I am, yours etc., (Miss) F. E. WHITFIELD, (superintendent). 41, Arpley-street, March sth, 1900.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>SIR JOHN BRUNNER'S ATTITUDE ON THE WAR.</head>
        <p>SIR JOHN BRUNNER'S ATTITUDE ON THE WAR.</p>
        <p>Questions by His Constituents.</p>
        <p>Mr W Bing, of Winsferd, a preminent member of the Liberal party in the North with Parliamentary Division, has addressed the following letter to Sir John Brunner, the member for the division : Dear Sir Jahn,—ln a speech delivere 1 by you last week at the Gladstone Club, Northwich,you are reported to have said :—" Who was going to get any good out of the war? He dld not believe any of the mineowners would, and he exp,ctld that before the British occu- pied Johannesburg the machinery in the mines would be smashed, and the mines themselves would be destroyed by explosives. He bad not the shadow of a doubt that twenty millions of the capital expended in the mines would be utterly lost." These remarks have Neu the subject of animated discussion by a goodly number of your constituents. Toe interpretation put upon tirm by some fe,,, impliel that you have some knowledge of what is going to take place, or that, by the use of the words imputed to you. you intended to give some en- couragement to acts of violence on the part of the Boers against the property of private individuals. As one of your constituents, and knowing that all such interpre- tations are contrary to any sentiment of your mind and absolutely foreign to your nature, I have ventured with great respect to call your attention to what you are re- ported to have said and the constructions which are being put upon it." On Saturday Mr. Blagg te;:eived the following reply from Sir John Brunnrr _ . _</p>
        <p>Dear Mr. Blagg,—The American philosopher said Never prophesy unless you know" I prophesied without knowing, but I based my anticipation upon the well-known fact that there are among the Boers many fanatics who hate the gold mines and everything con- nected with them, believ lag them to have been a curse to their country. If these people do destroy the mining companies' plant,we shall get no indemnity from the com- panies, and shall have heavier taxes to pay ; so we must all hope that the damage may be prevented. The idea that I wish for so deplorable a catastrophe is very far fetched, and need not be remarked upon. What a memorable pleasure it was yesterday to join with the members cf the two houses in attending in the great courtyard of Buckingham Palace to express our grati- tude to, and admiration and reverence for, her Majesty. She has, I assure you, touched all our hearts by her de- cision to visit Ireland, and thus to render her thanks for the splendid valour of her Irish regiments, and her sorrow for their losses. This decision, and the order that all Dish regiments shall henceforth wear the shamrock en St. Patrick's Day, are universally believed to have come, not from the advice of Ministers, but from her Majesty's own warm quick sympathies. God save the Queen !—Yours faithfully, JOBS T. BRUNNER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>BILLIARDS.</head>
