      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>TH E EX ANI IN ER, FRI PA Y, MARCH 16. 1900.</head>
        <p>TH E EX ANI IN ER, FRI PA Y, MARCH 16. 1900.</p>
        <p>head, and scattered his brains. There were some fearful sights to see. Poor fellews with arms and legs shatter d, and faces betcered beyond recognition. The folfewing evz had a rough tire of it, sheth fl.; ing all over toe pleee ; in fd.c., ,t was dangerous two miles away from the troops. One piouecr sergeant wa sitcing by a fire a great way from the firing lice, and a 5h311 struck hen. The shells were flying all over the place like birds in the air, then they would drop and run along the earth and burs% Sine of them, thank /leaven, don't burst, therefore do no harm. Our brigale has had to retire. We have one General shot through the head, bat he is still alive. The Beers keep sending white flags down to ask permission to bury their dead. Oa Friday nieet, the 26-es January, our cimpany was on outpost duty. They were coming home about 9.30 at night, when the Boar 3 sent a few shells, an i fired aIA teina gun on them. They had to run for it. This is terrible butchery of men. I won't be sorry when it is over. We have not had any sh lter for eight days, and have been fighting evn..y day ! This is the longest bett'e known in history. We are moving now to some other place ; in fact, we are never in one place over two days, I trust everyone at hem e are in good spirits. I rnysid an in good health, and trust in God to come borne safe to you. I have no more to say only goodnight, and pray for your son's safe return is my earnest wish."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS.</p>
        <p>CAPTURE OF BLOEMFON-</p>
        <p>TEIN.</p>
        <p>Lord Roberts' Triumphal Entry.</p>
        <p>BLOBAIFONTEIN, MAUCH 13.</p>
        <p>The Free State, in spite of urgent representations from the Transvaal, decided to surrender the capital yesterday, Mr Steyn leaving secretly at 630 for Hroonstad, the new seat o: Govornmeut, via Winberg, without replying to Lord Roberts'a demand to surrender within twenty-foar hours.</p>
        <p>The keys were given up four miles out at eleven o'clock.</p>
        <p>Lord Roberts, heading a cavalcade a mile in length, descended the southern slopes besides the railway and entered the city at one o'clock, meet- ing with an enthusiastic reception throughout the route. He stopped on the way to order the instant replacement of goods which were being looted at the Artillery Barracks by Kaffirs. There were renewed cheers as the procession passed into the market square. A-great reception was given to the British commander in the club. Going to the Government Buildings Lord Roberts took possession of Bloemfontein in the name of the Queen. Afterwards he crossed the river and entered the grounds of the Presidency, amid great cheering and singing of" God Save the Queen." A Union Jack specially made by Lady Roberts was hoisted at 1.30 in the grounds. Tho Boers and their sympathisers have fled. Mr. Steyn's brother entertained General French at his farm yesterday, and Lord Roberts hid break- fast there to-day. He stated that Mr, Steyn had become a nonentity.</p>
        <p>Official Announcement at ,Pretoria.</p>
        <p>PRETORIA, WEDNESDAY. The following war bulletin has been issued by the Government this morning : Yesterday Bloemfontein was occupied by the Britieh,after our burghers had retired in a northern direction.</p>
        <p>The set of Government of the Free State had already been transferred to Kroonstad.</p>
        <p>The Suggested Intervention.</p>
        <p>Firm Reply by Lord Salisbury.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Thursday afternoon, Mr. Balfour stated that the United States Govern- ment had been asked by the South African Republic to intervene with a view to a cessation of hostilities Lord Salisbury replied that Her Majesty's Govern- ment did not propose to accept the intervention of any Power in South African affairs. (Loucl cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>LADYSMITH DAY IN TORONTO.</head>
        <p>LADYSMITH DAY IN TORONTO.</p>
        <p>Letter from Mr. E. Dignum.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Dignutn, of Penketh, has handed to us the following interesting letter from one of his sons in Toronto, which shows the intense patriotism of the Canadian people. Toronto, March Ist. My dear father,—lt does an Englishman's heart good to be in this city of Torento to-dar. News of the relief of Ladysmith reached the city about an hour ago and in an incredibly short time the university students about 200 strong had gathered together to march along the principal streets, with Union Jacks flying, shouting and singing patriotic songs with the wildest kind of enthusiasm which evoked the heartiest response from the crowds on the street. Whilst sitting in my office here I could hear the crowds cheering wildly, whilst on the faces of every one that one meets is depicted auoh expressions of relief and gladness, that one cannot help bac realise the intensity of the pent up emotions which had evoked such demonstration of joy. Several times this week reports have reached us that Ladysmith was relieved and which were ascepted as true, and each dis- appointment seemed more bitter than the last when the reports were found to be untrue. I was at a public entertainment two or three nights ago, when what pur- ported to be an official telegram was reed from the platform that Ladysmith was relieved, when the whole audience broke forth into one spontaneous cheer and then rising en Masse thundered forth God Save the Queen led by an orchestra. I rose, feeling equal to making myself heard above the voices of all the others, only to find that the intense feeling of excitement that svpipt over me had almost completely taken away my voice, and I have no doubt but what many ethers had a similar experience. The news regarding Cronje althotigh reeeivod with the wildest demonstrations of joy, flags flying and bells rieging throughout the city, hardly comes up to the feelings evoked by the news of this morning. The heart of the people was evidently wrapped up in Ladysmith, and Canadians are rejoicing, to-day witu the mother land with a jey that cannot be felt in any greater degree by the most loyal subjects in Great Britain, and which is en evidence of the strength of the lasting tie which binds us heart and soul to her as part of one great nation. You are such a thorough going Englishmen yourself that I felt that it would rejoice your heart t 3 hear just how we are affected by news of such vital import to the nation, and so have written you before there has been any time for me to lose sight of the warmth of the enthusiasm evoked, and which you, and possibly your friends wilt no doubt be pleased to hoar of.—Your affectionate son, TED.</p>
        <p>P.S. Since I closed my letter I have been around town and it has the appearance of a regular gala day, public buildings, warehouses and shops are decorated with Union Jacks, and almost every other person on the streets has a small flag struck on some part of their per- son, which they have obtained from boys who are offer- ing them for sale at every street corner- The (xeitemeat shows 2Go'signs of abatement. A gentleman has just come in from the City of Hemilton about 40 miles from here, and he says the excitement there is even greater than here, almost a general holiday being observed, business people having closed up their places of busi- ness, to go out on the streets to meet their friends and neighbours to exult together over the good news.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>Warrington Man at Jacobsdal; "Having a Rough Time of it."</head>
        <p>A Warrington Man at Jacobsdal; "Having a Rough Time of it."</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Durkin, of Warriegton, has received a letter from his son Private James Durkin, of the York and L acaster Regiment serving under Lord Roberts. Mr. Durkin has two other sons on service at the Cape, one I being in the 12:h Lancers and the other in the 3rd South IlLanc..shire Militia. Private Durkin (the writer of the Jotter) is discharging the duties of servant to Major Evans. The letter is dated Jacob;dal, 17th February. a the commencement the writer says he has been at Enslin, Graspan, and Modder River. They started from Madder river about the beginning of this menth, and have bad a rough time of it. The heatis so intense that they could drink water all day long, if they could get it. He at the time of writieg was with Lord Roberts' column on the way to Kimberley. This is the firet bit of civilization they have sceu SiIIC9 they left Modder River. Some of them have been" putting-up" at a house at Jacobsdal, and the writer says it is a treat to have a plate and cup to eat and drink out of. He men- tions that once they wera nearly caught by 800 Boers, but they got up too early for them. They however, capturee their (the Btitish) convoy with nearly all the supplies, but he believed that they got it back again. The writer concludes with affectionate remembrances.</p>
        <p>Lance-Sergeant Monks' Last Letter.</p>
        <p>IThe following is a copy of a letter which has teen 'received by the mother of Lance-Sergeant H. Monks, of the let Sox% Lancashire Regiment, who resided in Wakefield-etreet, and who, unfortunately was kil.ed on the 22nd February in the operations for the relief of Ladysmith. The letter is dated 28. h January, Springfield Oamp—"Just a few lines to let you know I am quite safe acd sound after our terrible ex- neriencFs around Ladysmith. On Saturday the 21st January, our brigade came into action with the Boers. The Lancashire Fuiiliers and two companies of our regiment were ordered to take a position on a hill. Our Genetal, Woodgate, went with them. They took the hill all right, but when they got on top it was quite flat, and there was no shelter. Worst of all they were short of ammunition. We c3uld Rot go t their asaistacce.for the hill was alive with shells and bullets. They stayed four hours and thee had a chance to came down, and what a tale they told. We lost one captain, one lieuten- ant, and 43 men. One sergeant wire was killed was speaking to a c3rporal nex,, to him. He had knelt dawn to fire, and pur hie band in some blood. He saw this, and putting his hand to his head slid • Am I shot here?' and the next second a bullet whizzed through baud and</p>
        <p>_r---,_;_</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>Orders for More Warrington Volunteers.</head>
        <p>Orders for More Warrington Volunteers.</p>
        <p>An important battalion order was issued on Wednes- day morning by Lioutenant-Colonel J. C. Ridgway,com- manjing the Lot V.R. (Prince of Wales's Volunteers, South Lancashire Regiment. L read as follows : "African Service.—The Commanding Officer is pleased to annoutise that orders have been received to organize another half company from this battalion for service with the Ist Battalion in Seuth Africa, provided sufficient numbers are register,‘d and medically pasfed for this duty. 'rho half company would consist of :—Two subalterns, two sergeants, one bugler, three corporals, 49 privates, oLe stretcher barer. Names to be given at 01.1C:3 to the SergeantAlej-ir. Medical eximinatien will commence nut Friday at 8 p.m, Toe Commanding Offic,er is fully aware and appreciative of the patriotic spirit of the battalion, and Lbe effolts it has already made, and there- fore hopes that it witl be able to meet this fresh call to duty by its country. Last year's recruits may also, if 20 years old, ivgis:er their names, aed application will be made for permission to enliat them, it medically fit, provided there are vacancies."</p>
        <p>A County Court Bailiff's Patriotism.</p>
        <p>Before hearing the disputed cases a", the Warrington County C3urt on Thar3day, his Honour Judge G wynae- James said he understood that Mr. Newton, whit was one of tb. baiiiffe of the Curt had under the last Array order been called up. It was optional to him under that order wnether he responded to the call or not, but if he did so he was liable to serve in Eng. land, for twelve montns. Mr Newton wished to know what his poiition in that court would be when he re- turned in twelve moxths' time. Mr Newton might make his mind perfectly eaay in reference to Chat matter. If he served for 12 months, at the end of that pried be (his Honour) should at once reappoint him in his posLion. Everyone inust admire the way in which employers of labour had kept plases open for Bervants wh3 uad had to go out to do their duty for their country and he shguld be the last pers9n to ref U3O to offer the same privilege to Mr Newton when he came back. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>Mobilization of Troops at Orford Barracks.</head>
        <p>Mobilization of Troops at Orford Barracks.</p>
        <p>The mobilization of the rtservists e seetions A, B, and C of the King's Liverpool Regiment, who have been on fuesugh for several months, took place at Orford Bar- racks in the early part of this week,and it is satisfactory b record that with few exceptions the wlyAo of the men have respondA to their country's call. The men, who number about 270 will le ye Warrington for Soubainp- ton on the night of the 23d inst., and in all probabithy embark for the Cape on the following day. Local Men Killed in the War Requiem Mass at Warrington.</p>
        <p>A solemn requiera mass was celebrated in St. Mary's Church, Buttermarket-Kreet, Warrin4ton, on Tuesday morning, for the repose of the souls of the late Lieut-Colonel M'Carthy O'Leary and the men of the local regiments killed in tee war. The Mayor (Alderman Roberts) accepted on behalf of the town the invitation of the Rev. Father Wilson, O.S 8., to be present and asked the members of the Council to accompany him. Accordingly at 9.30, a representative gathering assembled at the LE'swn Hall and waked in processional order to St. Mary's Church. The procession was headed by a small de- tachment of volunteers who were succeeded by members of the Borough Police Force with whom were Chief - Inspector Poillips and Inspector Rowbottom. .Inandediately preceding the Mayor were the mace, and sword levers, Sergeants Dean and Bell. The Mayor wore his robes and chain of office, the latter having been draped for the occasion. He was accompanied by the Deputy Mayer (Alderman Wright) and the Town Clerk ( olr J Lyon Whittle). Amongst those also present were Lieutenant -Colonel itidgway, Major lioome (adjutant), and Quartermaster Potter, Alderman Fairclough, Alderman Hutchinson, Coun- cillors Smethurst, A J Jolley, Rogers,A Jolley,James _Evans, F R Roberts, lllis jn, Lee, Soillcock, Baird, and Peacock; Messrs S Wilson (deputy town clerk), 111 F White (deputy magistrates' clerk), T Lang - din (borough surveyor), J Sutcliffe (assistant sur- veyor), C Madeley (librarian), W S Haddock (gas engineer),F Taylor (secretary of the gas works),and J'Deas (water engineer); R T Fairclough,Dr White, Dr Peacacke, Dr McCarthy, Nlessrs T Birched], J.P., Fowler, Flepherd,S Davies, and Ashcroft. 'the Volun- teers wore crape bands and the police crape rosettes.</p>
        <p>A large number of military men were present from the Barracks. Detachments of the 3rd South Lancashire Militia and the 3rd King's Liverpool Regiment wore provided with seats at the left side of the centre aisle. The officers present included Colonel 3 H Spratt, Major E H Molyneux Seele, Major F A Adams, Captain Macdonald and Captain C E Edridge. The relatives of the deceased Colonel were represented by Miss Considine and Master McCarthy O'Leary.. A most impressive service was conducted. The Rev Father Wilson, 0.5.8 , wis the celebrant and he was assisted by the Rev Father O'Shea of Liver- po)l (deacon), and the Rev Father Crowe, of Sr. Albans (sub-deacon). A Gregorian chant was sung by the choir under the leadership of Mr C Eccles. solos were sung by Mr Eccles (biss), and Mr Joseph Dolan (tenor). The organ is at present undergoing repairs, and a harmonium had to be used. The beautiful sanctuary had been draped in mourning, and a catafilqee h•ld been erected, upon which was the Union Jack. earrounded by six candles. Father Wilson gave the Absolutions at the catafalque,after which a very beautiful and impressive function was brought to a conclusion. The collection taken was cn behalf of the widows and orphans of the soldiers of the lccal regiments who had been killed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>Special Services at Walton.</head>
        <p>Special Services at Walton.</p>
        <p>Special services were held in Walton church on Sun- day with reference to the war. At the Morning service the Rev E.C.E., Car'eton IT&amp;S the preacher, and he spoke of the war as being one of these things which Scripture describes as " judE,,ments,” z nd as specially appropriate for that reason, for thoughtful consideration in the sea son if Lent. In the afternoon the Vicar conducted a children's service. In the evening the church was crowded, many being unable to obtain admission. The Warrington Ambulance Brigade were invited by the 'Vicar, as their ch ipliti, to attend, and about forty mem- trra were present in uniform, headed by Captain War- ing and Captain Crosfield. The preacher was Canon Willis, and he spoke of the lawfuiness of war, and also of tbe mingled feelings of prayerfulness and of thankful' neis suited to the state of affairs in South Africa. At both the morning and evening service the members of the Walton Brass Band accompanied the singing 60f the hymns in conjunction with the organ. Mr Busby pre' ided with ability at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>WARRINGTON DISTRICT SURVEYORSHIR</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON DISTRICT SURVEYORSHIR</p>
        <p>Mr. S. S. Newopmbe Obtains the Post.</p>
        <p>IA meeting of Warrio;ron Rural District Council,was held at ths Highway Offices, Beweey Chambers, on Wednesday. There were present :—Messrs Ball (chair- man), Knowles (vice chairman), Houghton, Dignum, Brooks, Johnson, H ,lton, Thornton, and Forshaw with J C Sutton, clerk. The Council proceeded to the ap pointment ot A Surveyor in the place of the late Mr.- Thompson. M .ssrs. Newcombe and Wrigh!-, assis- tants to the late surveyor h kyle?: pnviously 'sea irivitm to send in applications for the post with their testhnonials, the Council pro- needed to the consid-ratian of the same with the result that Mr. B. S. Newcomb was aopointel at the annual salary of £163 on the gvuod of his seniority in years and czneriencs of the wo:k. I was however also decided to continua the services of Mr. W. Wright as assistant to the surveyor at the annual salary of £75 and also to provide additional assistance for office work. Beth gentlemen having been informed of the result expressed heir thanks and eati3faction with the arrangement.</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs ant Astims and Bronchitis. Of Cheraists oaly.—Advt. COAGULTNB Cement for broken articles, 61. and is.; Postage hi. Sold everrwhere, Home and Abroaci.-.. Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON</p>
        <p>SLEEPING OUT.</p>
        <p>At the Town Hall, Newton on Saturday mprning lefore Mr. Gillespie, J. Marriott, was crgd wir:a ,leepincr out at the glas bottle works, and was Eant o gaol for 7 days. EARLESTOWN WOILEN'.9. LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the members of this association was held in the Co-operative Hall, P.arlestown, on Monday Miss Oreenall presided in he absence of Mrs R,awlins. After the opening ,liss Marrow read a paper on " The War and Chris- Can work in the Transvaal," rom the pen of the tiev Charles Phillips of Jonannesburg. Miss Webster followed with a paper on" The half time system as it affects the education of girls," written by Richard '‘Vaddington as president of the Half-time Council of Teachers. The paper was brimful of informs- !:ion, and proved most interesting to the members 1. vote of thanks to Miss Marrow and Miss Webster was moved by Kiss Greenall and seconded by Mrs Jones, and was heartily accorded. Tea was after- wards served to those present. MANCHESTER CITY MISSION, EARLESTOWN BRANCH.</p>
        <p>In connection with the above a tea party and entertainment was given in the Co-operative Hall, Flarlestown, on Saturday evening. There were about 100 present to tea, which was mainly prc- vided by means of donations and gifts of provisions from funds of the ciuse, and about £4 13s was realized as profit. During the evening it was stated that during the year the Mission had been instru- mental in raising £76 93 84 for the following funds :—General Mission Hall fund, £3O 18s 110 including a donation of £lO from Mrs Evans ; P.S.A. book prize fund, £l3 ifs ; managemen fund, £4ss 7d; Sunday school, .E 9 12s 11,0 ; choir fund, £6 12, 6d; Manchester City Mission, £5 7s 2d; Foreign Missions, £3 63 24; war fund, £1 13'; benevolent fund. £1 2s. The entertainment was much enjoyed and consisted of glees, duets, songs and instrumental items, efficiently given by Miss Mather, Miss Corlett, Miss Watkins, Miss Holliday Messrs fleaton, Webster, Dring and Twemlow. Mr B Corbett presided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>HARMONIOI73 QDDFELLOWS AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>HARMONIOI73 QDDFELLOWS AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>Oil Saturday evening a smoking concert was given in connection with the Viaduct Lodge of Oddfellows at the Wellington _Hotel, Earlestown. The Ilaydock Glee Party were present, and under the conductorship of Mr. E. Leyland, submitted an excell,eat selection of music in a very able man- ner. There was a large muster of members and under the chairmanship of Mr. N 0 E Josephs, a most enjoyable evening was spent. The usual toasts w.lre honoured and the following programme was submittel:—Glee, "Banks of Allan Water; humor- ous song, "All fine goals" Mr RJones;song Mr E Leylan(J; recitation, "Uncle " Mr J Mather; song, "The Diver," MrJ Vernon; humorous song, Mr B Jones ; song "Acchored," Mr A Leyland ; song "bast roll ca'!," Mr J liazelden; humorous song, "Come down,' Mr R Jones ; sang "Star of my soul,' Mr E Leyland; recitation "Forethought," Mr J Mather; song "Soldiers of the Queen,"Mr A Leyland violin solo, Mr R Foster; song, "Only to see the old place," Mr J Razelden ; glee, "0, who will o'er the LOWIIS," party. A cordial vote of thanks to the Glee Party and the Chairman terminated the pro- ofyAings</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLARY.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLARY.</p>
        <p>The Hope of Earlestown Lodge meeting was held in the Co-operative Hall, Wargrave road, on Mon- day, Bro Cutler, C.T , presiding. After the opening services the Secretary read a letter from Bro Rev J A Lee, accepting an invitation to visit and address the Lodge in April. Bro Clarke, E.S., gave the report of the I;istrict Lodge, also of the Convention which showed good work being done. The next meeting of the Convention being held under the auspices of this Lodge, it was resllved on the motion of Bro Newman to invite Bro Hayes, district treasurer, to visit. 0:1 the motian of Bro Clarke, ES., it N7/13 resolved that the Lodge send a petition in favour of Sunday Closing for England and in favour of the Intoxicating Liquors (Sale to Children) Bill ta Colonel Piiking- ton, M.P. for this Division, and to Mr. Balfour. B-o Bunting, of the General Gordon, Garston, spoke as to the fraternal feeling he had had shewn to him by toe members of the Lodge. lie being a stranger was welcomed as a member of the Order. Bro Plant V.S.J.T. Samaritan Lodge (Haydock), thanked the members for their Co-operation with the Lodge at llaydock, and said there were plenty of people who said the same of the Lodge at ilaydock as the old Cumberland woman said of the locomotive when she saw it in the station she said " it'll nae go, it'll ace go," but when she saw it begin to move and to puff, puff, and go faster ant faster, she exclaimed ne'er stop, it'll ne'er stop," so it would be at HaydockATne programme was "The history and mystery of Wines" and descriptive readings of the manufacture of wines (foreign) and their composi- tion were given by Bros. Bring, Warburton, New- man, Woods and Cutler. VOLUNTEER SMOKING CONCERT AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening a smoking concert of a successful character was given at the Legh Arms Hotel, Newton, in connection with "F," Company of the 6:h V.B ,Kings Liverposl Regiment, in aid of the Local War fund. There was a very large at- tendance over which Sergeant Instructor Hancock presided. The opening item was the toast of "The Qa.?en and all the Royal Family," given by Sergeant Instructor Hancock, and heartily responded to. Sergeant McEvoy of the Inniskilling Fusiliers was present he having been wounde4 at the battle of Colons° and invalided home. The following pro- gramme was vere ably submitted :—Song " t'oldiers of the Queen," Private W Devereux ; song "Boys of the Old Brigade," Mr W Dudgeon; song "The lads in navy blue," Mr R Galloway; recitation " The Abient-roind3d Beggir." A collection was here made by Buglers Perry and Plant 83 Od being realised. Song" 11(101i :An," Staff Sergeant Brown; soup;" The Tipperary Christening," Ser- geant McEvoy; violin solo " Intermezzo-Cuckoo," Mr H Pennington; song "The British Volunteer," Mr W Devereux ; song "Since my wife started act- ing," Staff Sergeant Brown; song "Mary of Argyle," Mr R Galloway; whistling solo "Soldier and a man,' Mr J Howard; recitation, "Charge of the Light Brigade," Corporal H J Jones; song "Richard- sons's Show," Sergeant G. Baker; song " Song that will live for ever," Corporal W Pendlebury ; recita- tion " Bill Adams,"Mr E Grace; song "Private Tommy Atkins," Mr A Horobin ; song "Pat Murphy," Staff sergeant Brown; song "0' let it bs soon," Mr R Galloway; Mr GreaTes very ably accompanied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>ST, HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST, HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS COLLIERS Y. TRH COLLIERY COMPANY.</p>
        <p>A case, which promised to be of great interest to the colliery employees of the district, was brought before the St. Helens magistrates on Monday morning. Mr. H. L. Riley,instructed by the Miners' Federatioa, appearel for plaintiffs—James He!shy, John Rigg, and Joseph Webb; and Mr. Ellis, of Wigan, defended.—Mr. Riley said that the three men had surmnoped Messrs. Bromilow,Forster, and Co., of the Ashton's Green Colliery, on a claim fcr £5 wages due. NVithcat referring to all the questions raised in the particulars, both sides fully agreed that the substantial part of the dispute between them rested upon the accuracy or inaccuracy of figures, which ought to be identical, in the possession of both sides. They had agreed to the case being adjourned sine die with leave to the plaintiffs to bring it on after seven days' notice, and in the meantime they were going to compare their figures. Subject to these being satisfactorily adjusted they felt sure that the other small matters on one side or the other, for it was a case of claim or counter-claim would be adjusted in an amicable spirit.—Mr. Ellis agreed to this statement, and said the cage would prob- ably have boen settled airsalv had they not found there was a discrepancy in the figures.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>SUICIDE OF A ST. HELENS BOTTLEMAXER.</head>
        <p>SUICIDE OF A ST. HELENS BOTTLEMAXER.</p>
        <p>Mr Brighouse, coroner, held an inquest at St. Helens Town Hall, on Wednesday morning, on the body of Thomas Wnittaker, aged thirty-two, of 100, Westfield- street, who was found drawned in a pool of water on the Recreation Ground near Dentm's Green, on Sunday morning. Deceased's mother said he was a b tltnaker She saw him last shortly after nine o'clock on Saturday night, when he went out ha the bickyard. He had been very low all the Saturday, and had complained of severe pains in his head for about a fortnight. He bad been drinking heavily, but had st,)nped drinking on the Thursday. Thomas 011 ham, coachman of Rigby-street, saW that on Sunday morning he saw the deceased in Denten'a Gnen, near the bottom of Greenfield-road, and not a minute's walk from the pool whe7e he was after- wards found drowned. That would be shortly after half-past ten in the morning. D,,ceased looked very ill. George Rigby, 3, Tamworth-street, spoke to seeing &amp;ceased's body in the pool of water at the Recreation Ground at half-past eleven on Sunday morning. He saw deceased's Jacket, muffin., and cap lying on a heap of t r.clc3 near the pool, with one brick on t)p of them. Conatablo Joseph Evans said the body was about five yards from the sidn o 7 the pool. There was only about two feet of water wlvre tha body was. The body was vitt) cold and stiff. The jury returned a verdict of " Suie; whllst of unsound mind."</p>
