      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The Charge Against a Warn i ngton Painter. AG the Chester ASSiZaS,OI2I Tuesd ay,Arthur Wilson painter, of Warrington, was sentenced to four months' imprisonment for attempted criminal assault upon a girl named Ruth Reid, at Wbitby, on the first November last.</p>
        <p>Death of the Rev. Father Pozzi. The death toot place at Llandudno on Tuesday morning of the Rev. Father Pozzi, who had for 18 years been Rector of the Roman Catholic Church of Brownedge, near Preston. The deceased gentleman, who was a membsr of the Benedictine Order, spent some years of his life as an energetic worker in connection with St. Alban', Church, Warrington. Ile had been staying at Llandudno with a view to recruiting his health. He was 70 years of age. LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES UNDER THE CLOCK, RRIDGE-STRERT.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Accident in Sankey-street. •A man named John Norris, of 36, Dexter- street, Liverpool, was crossing Sankey-street, last Friday afternoon when he was knocked down by a horse attached to a furniture van, one of the wheels of which passed over him. He was picked up in an unconscious condition and taken to the infirmary, where he was found to be suffering from internal injuries. • Warrington Markets. At the Warrington Corn Market on Wednesday wheat (white) fetched 43 (701 b to the bushel) ; red wheat, 3311 d ; oats, 2s 51 ; flour (best seconds) per 2401 b., 19e; bran, is per 201 b. At the Provision Market prices were as follows:--Fresh butter, is 3d per lb ' • fresh eggs, 12 and 13 for is ; fowls, 41 6d to 5s 61 per couple; English beef, 61 to 90 ; mutton 7d to 101; veal, 7d to 10d; pork 61 to Sid.</p>
        <p>Spiritualist Meeting. On Sunday good audiences assembled to hear Mr. C L Hilton, of Blackpool, a blind medium,who gave an address in the afternoon on "Man know thy- self," urging the necemity of the cultivation of the psychic powers. In the evening the subject was "The war spirit—its cause and cure," arguing that the war spirit had its soot in bad government— government on individualistic lines. At both meet- ings he gave psychometry. On Monday evening he gave a number of experiments i psychometry. 1.0.G.T. Temperance Mission Lodge. The weekly session of the above was held on Wednesday in the Acaaemy-street Mission Room, Bro Roberts presiding. After the usual routine) business, Bro J Sheath gave a report of the Con- vention meeting, which was commented upon by Bro O'Brien and. Bro Roberts. The following pro- gramme was gone through :—Sister Nurse, recita- tion Bro O'Brien, song; Sister Fletcher, recitation; Sister Sheath, song: Bro Twigden, recitation ; Bro C J Sheath, song; Sisters Nurse and Sheath and Bro J Sheath, trio ; Bro O'Brien, song. The lodge was closed by Bro Roberts, C.T., at 10 o'clock. AS Is, or CUNNING-RAM' HIGEI-OLASS] MINERAL WATERS—Advt. Presentation to Mr. and Mrs. Arthur Milton. At the Royal Court' Theatre on Saturday evening, in the palace scene of the pantomime "Dick Whit- tington "to commemorate Mr Arthcr Milton's birth- day, the company took the opportunity of showing their esteem for their manager by making him a presentation, which took the form of a typewriter. They also presented Mrs Milton with a case of silver spoons. Miss Susie Beavan (Dick Whittington) made the presentation and Mr Milton having res- ponded on behalf of himself and his wife, the com- pany gave three cheers followed by the singing of " He's a jolly good fellow.' BEST BOOTS FO ALL, UNDER rnE CLOCK,—A' v National Amalgamated Union of Enginemen</p>
        <p>&amp;c.-Warrington Branch. On Saturday evening a tea and public meeting in connection with the Warrington Branch of the above union.took place in the Ragged School,Bank- street. At six o'clock a capital tea was partaken of by abut SO persons. Councillor James Evans presided over the public meeting which was a - dressed by Mr Price. president of the union. This gentleman,on behalf of the Warrington Linion,made a presentation to Mr George Sambrook,late treasurer to the branch, and who is leaving Warrington for Scotland to take up the position of organising secre- tary for Scotland. The presentation took the form of a handsome writing desk with a clock and calen- dar attached, and in making the presentation Mr. Price spoke in complimentary terms of Mr Sam- brook an.l wished him every success. During the evening songs, &amp;c., were contributed by several gentlemen present. The usual votes of thanks were accorded and the meeting closed. LAMPS, Oil Stoves, Coke Stoves, J. S. GARLAND, 61, 53, and 55, Bridge-street 'Warrington.— Advt John Harrow, Limited. The aboye business has been established over a quarter of a century, and with a view to further development is being formed into a limited com- pany. The vender (Mr Harrop) and four heads of various departmsnts will carry on the management which has led to the present success of the business. The profits of last year are greater than any pre- vious one, and the wide area covered by the retail depots in the various towns is such as will enable very extensive developments to be made under the new arrangement. The present issue consists of 30,000 6 per cent. cur,ulative preference shares of £1 each, and we advise our readers to re- fer for full details to the prospectus which appears in our columns .of to-day. The business is well known, the growth has been steady and continuous, and the position to-day is better and more prosper. ous than ever before. We need hardly mention that one of the firm's branches is in Bridge-street, Warrington, where an extensive business is done. The list of applications will open on Monday next and close on the following Wednesday at four o'cleck in the afternoon or earlier, for both town and country. UNDER TUB CLOCK FOR GOOD ,BOOTS.—Advt The Circus, Queen-street. The spectacle " Dicic Turpiree ride to York and death of Bonny Black Bess" has been the chief attraction at the Circus, Queen-street, during the past week. It has been most creditably presented, and crowded audiences have assembled in the build- ing nightly The spectacle is performed by a capable company, the role or Dick Turpin being sus- tained by Mr. St. John Pinder, who has achieved great success by his excellent impersonation of the notorious highwayman, Several thril- ling incidents are represented, the death of Tom King being one of the most effective. In addition to this spectacle a first class miscellaneous programme is submitted. Thomas Sylvester, cham- pion jockey, gives a meritorious performance, and the Sylvester Brothere, acrobats, are artidtes of no mean order, La Petite Rose, who is described as " the youngest equestrienne in the world" contri- butes a creditable "turn." The concluding item is tke spectacle " Briton v. Beer" illustrative of stirring events in the present campaign. During the week several competitions have been held,mem- hers of the audience taking part. The last per- formance will take placa to-night and there will be a day show " this afternoon. KITCWON RANGES, Tile Grates,and Chimney Pieces Large Stock—.J. S A.RLLY omnonger,Bridge-st. Ada Good Templars Convention.</p>
        <p>The monthly session of the ts o. 1 Convention, South West Lancashire, was held on Saturday, in the Barrack yard Mission-room,Hindley. Bro Clarke, ' C.C.T., (Earle,town) presided over a good attend- ance of representatives and visitors from Warring- ton, Earlestown, Ashton, St. Helens, Wigan,Hindley, land the various parts of the area. The minutes of the last session were read and confirmed and the press and credential committees were appointed. The sEcretary, Bro. A. Trancis (St. Helens) read his re- colt for the past month, which was of a very encouraging character. The report of the Executive Committee was read and adopted. It was decided to form a commiztee for the purpose of sending books, etc.. to the Ist South Lancashire Regiment, now with General Buller at Ladysmith. The Com- mittee consists of Bros G Clarke, 46, Xing-street, Earlestown, E Newman ,50, King-street,Earlestown, and A Francis. 2, Ilardshaw-street, S. Helens, by whom all boeks, &amp;c., will be gladly received and forwarded to their destination. It was decided," on the proposition of Bro J Sheath, C Marshall (War- rington), to send the fraternal greetings of the Con- vention to Bro Sergeant-Nlajor Devlin, (Ist South Lancashires) and all other brethren at the front. Bro F Bowler (Ashton) read a paper on "How to make the Convention work more successful." The paper was commented on by several members. The block and gavel was won by the Ashton "Onward" lodge for the largest increase in the half -year,baving added 27 per cent to their membership. The trophy was presented to a deputation by Bro Fenton, P.C. CT., No 1 Convention S.L4I. Lancashire. The next feS3iorl will be held at Earlestown on April 7th. The meeting concluded with, tbe usual votes of thanks.</p>
