        <p>BUILDING SOCIETIES. • --- Annual Meetings, The 25th annual general meeting of the Borough of Warrington Incorporated Permanent Building Seciety was held at the Office of the manager, Alderman T H ISutton, Legh-street,on Wednesday last week. Mr. John Andrews presided and there were also present Messrs. George Holford, T El A ?iikingtornand Henry Houghton (directors), William Caldwell, A Clive, J H Clarke (audi- tor), T A Sutton and Alderman Sutton (menage* THE ANNUAL REPORT. Alderman Sirrren read the annual report of the direc- tors which was as follows :—" Gentlemen.—The Direc- tors have pleasure in submitting the annexed balance sheet, according to which it will be seen that they are again enabled after providing for all salaries and expenses, to recommend the payment of a dividend to snaretiolders at the rate of £4 5s 01; per cent., per annum, free of income tax, also to carry £9 17s Iod to next year's profit. The recent new rules have so materially assisted the directors in extending the useful- ness of the society that they have the utmost confidence in urging upon every member the advisability of doing all they can to encourage working men to take advan- tage of its benefits either by becoming shareholders, who will receive a good interest on tbeir savings, or as borrowers, who are helped to purchase their own (properties on easy terms of repayments. The number _of members on the hooka is 70, of which 19 are borrowers.—JOHN EDWARD WRIGHT. Chairman February 23rd,. 1900." Commenting on the work of the society, Alderman Setton said: it would be seen from the balance sheet that only a small amount of business had been done 'during the year, £507 only having been advanced on osortgages, but notwithstanding that the directors, as the report sheaved had been able to declare a dividend Payable to the shareholders at the rate of 41 per cent free of income tax. He thought the shareholders should do all they possibly cluld to carry out the recomuseedations which the Directors made in their report, to advise workingmen to take advantage of becoming either shareholders or borrowers. He thought that taking everything into consideration, although they had not been doing very much business they, at the same time had been doing that little business with every success. The CHAIRMAN proposed the adoption of the report. Mr. HOUGHTON seconded, and the motion was carried unanimously. ELECTION OF OFFICERS. Mr. CLARKE proeosed that the directors be re-elected for the ensuing year.—Mr. CALDWELL seconded, and the motion was unanimously carried. The directors are :—Alderman John E Wright (chair. man). Hems John Andrews, George Holford, T H A Pilkington, William Roberts, and Henry Houghton. Mr. EIOLFORD moved that Councillor .1 Shaw Green and Mr .1 Ft Clarke be re-elected auditors. Alderman SUTTON seconded and remarked that he did not think they could have two better mea for the posi- tion. The motion was carried. THE RITE OF INTEREST. Alderman SUTTON here remarked that it would have been almost impossible to lend much more money dur- ing the past year. The Society bad b &gt;en accustomed to have over-drafts at the banks whenever they had required money but the bank interest had been so great, soinetoine like 6 per cent., that it would have been impessibie to have done business without loss. It would never pay them to lend money at q per cent.,wheu they had to pay to the bank at the rate of 6 per cent. swarm- Messrs. Jeans and Sou were reappointed solicitors to the society. rhe proceedings terminated with the usual votes of thanks. Permanent Benefit Building Society. The annual meeting of this building society was held in Cairo-street school on Toarsday lest week. Mr James Kenworthy. the President, presided and the attendaucee included Mess's. John Critchley, Thomas Critchley. John Dewhurst, Thomas Ince, George Legge, and P C Price (directors), Mr Kirkconnel and Mr R Proud (secretary.) THE ANNEAL REPGRT. The SECRETARY read the director'a annual repert which was as follows :—" The directors present their thirty third annual etatement of accounts showing the busiuesv done for the past year. The accounts are presented in the form required by the Building Societies Act of 1894. The directors declare a dividend of three pound ten shillings ner cent., which will be paid on S tturday, March 10th, at Cairo-street S. ihool. and afterwards at the secretary's office. The retiring directors are Messrs. James Kenworthy, John Dewhurst, and GPorgP Leger', who offer themselves for 5-election K . —JAMES ENWORTHY, President." The CHAIEMAN, in moving the adoption of the report said they were practically in as good a position as lest yearibecause they were carrying somethiug forward. The unsettled state of the money markets, owing to the war, which he hoped was drawing near to conclusion, had also effected them, bet he hoped that soon they would ssttle down again to their routine business. Mr. WINSTANLEY seconded. Mr. DALLOW exoreesed surpriae that they conld only pay a dividend of 31 per cent considering that trade had been good during the year and property was fairiy well ten Anted. He came to the conclusion that a lot of money must have been spent on some proper', and he thought it would be wise to get rid of the property. The CHAIRMAN said it had been the custom !or years to get rid of property that was a" drug " and anxiety and they invariably sold it. ELECTION OF OFFICERS Toe retiring directors Messrs .14int.3 Kertworthr. John Dewhurst and George Leege were unanitneusly re- elected. Messrs Janus Bolton and William Dick were re-elected auditors. Mr. A Cook, treasurer, and Mr. %Wham sarveyor. The usual veto of thanks brought the meeting to a close.</p>
